The Prague Stock Exchange crashes into the minus at the close of business.
After the steep decline in the morning, the Prague Stock Exchange was able to correct the losses.
Transactions with ČEZ's shares reached almost half of normal day-to-day business.
At the beginning of Tuesday's trades, the Prague Stock Exchange followed up on Monday's slump, when it fell by a further six percentage points.
This time the reason for the slump was on Wall Street.
In the Czech Republic, too, the market's reaction to the outcome of the vote by the American House of Representatives, which had declined to support a stabilization plan for the financial sector there.
Case of shares in Asia
Shares on the stock exchanges in Asia experienced a dramatic fall on Tuesday, although indices were able to compensate for some of the losses over the course of the day.
The Hang Seng Stock Exchange's index in Hong Kong lost almost four percentage points over the course of the business day, but was later able to make up for part of the losses, reducing the decline to about 2.5 percent.
The Hang Seng China Enterprises index, which observes the movement of Chinese equities on the stock exchange in Hong Kong, was down 3.8 percent.
Shares on the Sydney stock exchange lost more than five per cent, but then reduced losses to 4.3 per cent.
Taiwan's stock market fell 3.6 percent, according to the index.
"The timing for the U.S. bailout is uncertain and will affect financial markets worldwide," Hong Kong's Hong Kong monetary authority chief Joseph Yam said.
Hong Kong, although it belongs to China, determines its monetary policy itself, i.e. independent of China's central bank.
Interest rates in Hong Kong are at the same level as those in the United States.
U.S. legislators should return to the negotiating table as soon as possible, according to Australian Prime Minister Kevin Rudd, and adopt the draft for support of the financial system.
Otherwise, it threatens that other countries would also be affected by the slump.
Bloodbath of American Stocks
The U.S. House of Representatives on Monday rejected the plan for supporting the financial system, for which up to $700 billion (nearly $12 trillion Kč) should be spent.
Legislators thus ignored President George Bush's call to support the plan.
In Bush's view, the plan would address the fundamental causes of the financial crisis and thus help stabilize the entire economy.
American stocks experienced a bloodbath on Monday, and major stock indices recorded the biggest slump in more than 20 years.
The Dow Jones index fell by almost seven percent, a similar slump last recorded in 1987.
The index had already dropped before the vote, but there were hardly any signs that the draft would not get through in the House of Representatives, the index experienced a free fall.
Congress makes concession: U.S. government allowed to pump $700 billion into banks
U.S. congressional leaders and the administration of President George W. Bush have agreed on a broader form of an agreement on financial assistance to the American financial system.
Voting on this is scheduled to take place early next week.
U.S. lawmakers made a breakthrough in talks on the adoption of a rescue plan in the form of a grant for the American financial system worth $700 billion (about $12 trillion kronor).
However, this doesn’t mean victory yet.
Congregates still need to negotiate some details of the agreement before they are allowed to publish the final version of the law and vote on it.
The plan to support the financial system will be negotiated in the American Congress on Monday.
On Sunday, Chairman of the Banking and Financial Services Committee Barney Frank told Reuters.
In the Senate, according to differing information, it could probably not be voted on the plan until Wednesday at the earliest.
According to economists, a notice that the plan will be adopted should be the first significant psychological factor for reviving financial markets.
After that, however, there is a "disillusionment" due to the complicated mechances on how to help the markets in practice.
Paulson: The plan must be effective
“We’ve made significant progress.
We've clarified our differingent views on what a package to stabilise the market should look like," Democrat Nancy Pelosi told Bloomberg.
According to her words, there could still be a final vote on Sunday.
Legislators' representatives met with U.S. Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson during the night to give the government fund its final form.
This provides for a purchase of unsalable mortgage assets from the financial companies, which plunge the financial firms into heavy losses and jeopardize the stability of the entire system.
“We are on the verge of a definitive agreement on a plan that will work and which must also be effective in the market.
You have to continue the recovery plan, but I think we're at our destination," Paulson said.
A signal for Asian businesses
The global financial crisis is also clearly reflected in stock markets, which are falling sharply.
According to the words of the Democratic senator Harry Reid, lawmakers are trying to reach a final agreement in Congress before Sunday.
They want to reassure investors in front of business in the Asian financial markets, which are in the first time zone, where a decision of Congress could influence the business on Monday.
So far, however, it is not quite clear when both chambers of the American Congress will vote on the law, there is also uncertainty about whether the negotiations at the last minute could still fail because of a problem.
Legislators are hoping it's passing in the next few days.
However, the law will still undergo some changes.
The total amount intended to assist the system is to be divided into two parts.
The initial $350 billion is to be made available as soon as possible, as President George Bush demanded.
Congress, however, can block the distribution of the rest of the money if it has the impression that the program does not perform its function at another $100 billion and later the final €350 billion.
Bush evaluates progress positively in negotiations
Although the president has veto power in this decision, the Congress can veto its veto.
Despite these changes, however, the basic idea of the program has persisted, which was based on obtaining finances for the buill of bad mortgage securities, the value of which has fallen because hundreds of thousands of Americans are no longer able to repay their mortgages.
“We’ve worked out everything.
The House of Representatives should be able to vote on the bill on Sunday, the Senate then on Monday," according to Republican Senator Judd Gregg.
U.S. President Bush was also pleased with the progress.
His spokesman Tony Fratto said Bush was pleased with Saturday's progress and "appreciates the mutual effort to stabilise our financial markets and protect our economy."
Restriction of golden parachutes
The chairman of the banking committee of the Senate Christopher Dodd, the senior negotiator, announced that the legislators had agreed on a responsibility for the money program, the protection of taxpayers from losses, the conclusion of perks and a timetable for the financing of the plan.
The new law is also intended to restrict the so-called golden parachutes for directors of the companies involved in the programme and sets up a committee to supervising the Fund.
This is to be headed by the Ministry of Finance.
“We’ve worked very hard on it and made a big step forward towards an agreement that works and is important to the market and to all Americans,” Paulson said.
The plan also expects help for homeowners who have trouble repaying their mortgage.
The government should negotiate new installments on mortgages it buys up, with the goal being to lower the monthly costs for the debtors and allow them to keep their homes.
Marek: Psychological help
The proposed plan is a good idea according to Patria Finance's main economist David Marek, but implementation is very difficult.
"This is because one does not know by what mechanism and at what price the problematic assets are purchased.
That will be crucial for how the financial sector and the government split the losses among themselves," Marek told server iHNed.cz this week.
Whether the plan will be adopted now or early next week is not decisive in Marek's view.
“Much more importantly, congressmen agree to support the plan and that they say goodbye to it as soon as possible.
The psychological significance of the agreement that there will be a farewell is the most important thing at this moment," Marek stressed.
Duel on Messer's cutting edge: MP3 player against mobile phones.
We will advise you on your choice
While almost every mobile phone can play MP3 files, no MP3 player can make phone calls yet.
Therefore, of course, it looks like it is better to buy just one phone.
Nevertheless, there are plenty of reasons why you should buy an independent player.
The choice depends entirely on the type of use and the demands of the future owner.
I'm sure it's difficult to buy a great expensive turntable at home to listen to Maxim Turbulenc's latest album.
Once such musical novelties do not appear on LPs, and from a qualitative point of view there is also no objective reason for it.
However, if you have LPs with art rock, jazz or blues on the shelf, you probably dream of at least one.
This is also the case with the music while traveling, i.e. with compressed music, to put it simply - music in MP3 format.
There are price and quality categories here too.
For some, it may not be acceptable to spend eight thousand crowns on a player, but it may be difficult for a Pink Floyd fan to handle headphones for twenty kronor from the market.
Two extremes, between which there is enough space for everyone.
The selection is wide-ranging.
The service life of the batteries is also crucial.
Some good music players can play much more than just music, but what cellphones can’t, they don’t even create hint.
No modern device can be used as universally as the phone.
In addition to the primary functions, talking on the phone and texting, it can play music and videos, record both, it sometimes has a better, sometimes a worse camera and can perhaps also navigate.
And if you still include the smartphones and communicators, you almost have a small computer in your pocket.
It's a small miracle, because no refrigerator can grill and no TV can take pictures.
But the universality of mobile phones also entails disadvantages.
However, the more features you use on your phone, the more often you’ll need to reload it.
And playing music is one of the biggest energy guzzlers.
Few mobile phones can play music for more than ten-twelve hours, usually it is much less.
In this regard, music players (especially the simplest) are significantly more powerful.
That’s why you’re supposed to expect that they won’t be able to play music for more than a few hours.
If you then call from time to time, you prefer to buy a charger for the office - this also applies to more expensive devices.
With MP3 players, you will not have this problem, even the less good ones will last at least 9 hours of uninterrupted operation at one time charging.
Another problem exists if you want to transfer the music to your mobile phone.
Unfortunately, only a small part of the devices has a USB termination, in addition, not every mobile phone can be connected directly to a PC without special software.
Of course, you can transfer the music directly to a storage medium, but only a few card readers can cope without reduction with the tiny memory cards that are used in mobile phones.
For players you do not need any special software except for exceptions (iPod).
You only need to feed the device with music from your PC via USB port.
Most mobile phones and players create a music library themselves, sorted according to the name of the artist, the title of the album, the genre or, for example, the evaluation by users.
Some devices only map the tree structure of the address directory, similar to Windows Explorer, some can do both (iriver, Samsung, Sony, Cowon, smart phones with operating system).
players are easier to use, phones can do more
When choosing a specific device, you should inquire in particular about the service life of the battery, the storage capacity, the quality and the type of sound and, of course, the ease of use.
Is music above all a backdrop for you and listen to it every day, for example on the tram for example, then any mobile phone that plays MP3 files is enough.
Which really can do almost every phone.
You also don’t really need to control which music formats a cell phone or a player can handle.
MP3 all create, and most of the music you get will be in this format right now.
The format is one of the most common, and most devices can also play music in WMA format.
Less common is AAC (a format that Apple and its iPods use).
Better mobile phones also make it possible to purchase music online.
You only need to access the corresponding pages of a provider (or another provider) via mobile Internet, and you can purchase a song or album.
This service can already be in and of itself a striking argument for why you should buy a music phone.
Unfortunately, the offer of the providers is poor and is usually limited only to fast radio hits.
The prices aren’t the lowest either.
In general, however, it is assumed that precisely this type of sale of music (whether via GSM, Internet or wireless Internet) will dominate the music industry in the future.
Most people carry the mobile phone with them.
If you want to listen to something, reach for the phone in your jacket pocket, while the player is forgotten at home.
However, it depends on whether you always carry headphones or at least a wireless hands-free device.
If not, you can fall back on the built-in speaker that almost every phone has, but only a few MP3 players.
Arguably the biggest advantage of the music phones will be in the price for many.
The cheapest usually cost less than three thousand crowns.
For three and four thousand crowns, you already get a very decent Walkman.
The biggest handicap for most will be that there aren’t normal headphones.
The best and technically most sophisticated phones with a 3.5 mm opening for normal headphones cost up to fifteen thousand crowns.
In turn, the advantage of the players lies in easy operation.
The simplest - and not necessarily cheapest (see selection) - players can really serve anyone.
A minimum of functions, the similarity when operated with the previously normal cassette recorder or, for example, the turntable is obvious.
There is no threat of a collision in the form of an erroneously employed mobile Internet and a subsequently horrendous invoice for data transmissions.
Which device should be used now
The cheapest mobile phones (e.g. Vodafone 527) usually have a slot for interchangeable storage media (type M2, microSD, etc.).
Even if you buy a corresponding card, two thousand crowns are enough.
But Attention.
For the basic models, usually only cards with a maximum capacity of 2 GB are supported.
Only the most expensive can read 8 GB memory cards.
If you already own a mobile phone and still want to buy an inexpensive MP3 player, then reach for Cowon, Creativ, Sony, Samsung, Emgeton or iriver.
Depending on the capacity (maximum 2 GB), you do not have to put more than 1100 crowns on the table.
Be one of the more sophisticated listeners, take more specialized mobile phones - Walkmans (Sony Ericsson, some from Nokia and Samsung).
These devices have stand-alone control knobs for MP3, and the ease of use comes close to that of a single MP3 player.
The furthest here is Sony Ericsson, whose mobile phones from the Walkman series have the same user interface as the individually sold MP3 players.
The same services in the same price category of up to four thousand crowns, but with a higher storage capacity (up to 16 GB) and a long battery life (usually 15 hours, up to 35 hours), MP3 players from the above-mentioned manufacturers will provide you.
The first virtual provider is already coming to the Czech Republic this year.
Maybe
The mobile phone market in the Czech Republic is quite mature, the Czech providers offer virtually all the services offered by their partners in Western Europe.
Nevertheless, something is missing here - a virtual provider that forces the classic at lower prices.
Maybe he won’t wait much longer.
We have often reported on Mobil.cz that the Czech mobile phone providers are among the most expensive mobile phone providers in Europe.
Incidentally, this was also confirmed by the telecommunications authority ČTÚ, pointing out that the price of a text message here is higher than in the rest of the European Union.
After the European Commission's final steps in roaming fees regulation, a paradoxical situation is likely to occur - for some customers of the Czech mobile phone providers, SMS from abroad will be cheaper than at home.
How is it that the prices in the Czech Republic are so high compared to Europe?
The answer is simple, it lacks the competition that really makes a battle announcement.
Perhaps it sounds paradoxical, in the Czech Republic there are three mobile networks and a penetration of far more than 100%.
On the other hand, mobile service providers like to scold the fact that there is extremely much competition on the Czech market.
Pavel Dvořák, Chairman of the Council of the Czech Telecommunications Authority (ČTÚ), often agrees with Pavel Dvořák, who in the past had often stated that there was no room for another provider in the Czech Republic.
It is probably true that a classic provider with its own network no longer has many chances (by the way, it would probably hardly find frequencies).
However, there is one more option - a virtual provider.
Especially the entry of virtual providers into the mobile phone market entailed a significant price reduction, to which the classic mobile service providers also had to react.
Even before the entrenchment of such companies such as Tesco Mobile or Virgin Mobile, there were similar voices on the market in the UK as in the Czech Republic.
However, it has been shown that there is room for such providers and that they can really be successful.
You don’t have to look long for examples.
In February this we reported on the launch of the virtual provider Mobilking in Poland.
In just half a year, this provider managed to attract more than 100 thousand customers and thus become the fifth-largest provider in the country.
Behind Mobilking is the financial group Penta, which owns U:fon in the Czech Republic, among others.
The advantage of virtual providers is that you can focus on a much more concrete target group.
In order to function effectively, virtual vendors need far fewer customers than a classic provider.
Because you do not need relatively high costs for the construction, operation and maintenance of a mobile network.
In the world, virtual providers usually stand out as low-cost companies - they offer cheap telephone conversations and text messages, which, however, is often accompanied by a less comprehensive range of services.
Tesco Mobile, for example, offers SMS in the UK at least half cheaper than its competitors.
However, as evidenced by the example of Mobilking in Poland, it can also be quite a good move to rely on a non-clichéd target group.
This provider is aimed specifically at men.
"Real guys choose mobilking - the provider they like and who they can trust," said David Seibert, CEO of Mobile Entertainment Company, this virtual provider's operating company.
It’s hard to say whether a concept similar to Poland could gain a foothold in the Czech Republic.
Vodafone Miroslav Čepický’s press secretary also doubts this: “The Czech market is relatively small for a market segment on which most of the virtual suppliers are based.
In addition, it is relatively saturated from the point of view of the range of services."
In the Czech Republic without a chance, say the mobile phone providers
However, it is almost certain that if a virtual mobile phone provider conquers the Czech Republic, this will not be Mobilking.
“While we’re talking about an expansion of this successful project, the negotiations in the Czech Republic don’t look very hopeful.
It will rather lead to a market entry of mobilking in other countries of our region," says Jana Studničková, press spokeswoman of the financial group Penta.
Indirectly, she confirmed that the current mobile service providers in the Czech Republic aren’t particularly inclined to this idea.
On the contrary, they are trying to do everything to ensure that no potential competitor arises.
GTS Novera Group, which wants to take another step to become a real competitor of Telefónica O2 in the field of corporate customers, has already shown its interest in launching a virtual mobile network provider for a long time.
“We have a keen interest in providing our customers not only landline but also mobile telecommunication services.
And the sooner, the better.
We have repeatedly negotiated this possibility with all Czech mobile operators.
With some of them, we have already been able to make negotiation progress," said Pavel Kaidl's press spokesman.
Maybe better times are coming now.
Non-official information shows that the first virtual provider may be expected by the end of the year.
So far, however, this is surrounded by an insurmountable wall of silence.
"At this moment, we don't assume that there is room for a full-fledged virtual provider in the Czech Republic.
Compared to other markets, the Czech market has great specifics.
So far, no subject with a complex and reputable project in this area has approached us," says Vlastimil Sršeň from Telefónica O2's press department.
Other suppliers also point out the specifics of the Czech market.
T-Mobile Martina Kemrová's press spokeswoman adds in rare agreement with O2's statement: "So far, we have not yet found a viable and mutually beneficial project on which one could continue to build."
And she adds: "There will be hardly any virtual provider on T-Mobile's network by the end of the year.
At that moment, we should already have a concrete offer in order to be able to think at short notice.”
Vodafone focuses its attention rather on meeting the requirements of its own 3G license.
Miroslav Čepický comments: "We are open to negotiations with potential virtual providers, especially in the area of cost and infrastructure.
From our customers’ point of view, this is precisely what we consider to be the best.
Vodafone's main strategy in this area lies in the search for a partner to build a shared network for a fast mobile Internet."
The responsible authorities - hand in hand
The approach of the mobile providers is easy to understand, another provider would mean more competition, this would lead to falling prices and thus also to a decline in profits.
So why should such a competitor make it easier to enter the market?
The fact that the providers can pull together when it comes to something also proves the long back-and-forth about the emergence of U:fon.
Arguably the strongest pressure towards ČTÚ at the time when U:fon was launched on the market by T-Mobile.
This is also evidenced by the publicly available information from the ČTÚ websites.
Now, however U:fon is suitable as an argument why you should not allow a virtual provider.
Martina Kemrová commented: "The competitive environment of the mobile phone market has also recently been strengthened by the market entry of a fourth mobile operator.
Taking into account the character of the mobile communications market in the Czech Republic, it is obvious that a new actor would place high demands on the market itself and, above all, its services."
However, in view of this approach by mobile operators, the absolute disinterest of the Czech regulatory authorities is surprising.
It is not only the attitude of the ČTÚ, which unfortunately does not have the competence to intervene in economic competition in Czech - in contrast to Great Britain and other highly developed states.
This has exclusively the Office for the Protection of Economic Competition (ÚOHS).
This, however, referred to his very typical attitude on this entire problem.
“The entry into the market of a virtual mobile provider would then be possible after a contract between a mobile provider and a virtual provider about the rental of the necessary infrastructure.
So far, no such contract has been concluded, because the offer of potential interested parties was probably not sufficiently interesting for the classic providers," said Kristián Chalupa from the ÚOH's Foreign Relations department.
From representatives of the providers, such a formulation would not be surprising.
This is more tharming from an authority to represent consumer interests and, among other things, to ensure that no one abuses their position on the market.
In particular, if Kristián adds Chalupa: "Abroad, where virtual vendors operate quite normally, it can be observed that their market entry has contributed to a price reduction in mobile phone services."
Perhaps, however, the pressure on the correct functioning of the market and a price reduction are not among the interests of ÚOHS.
Truly shocking in this case is the almost toughing harmony of the three mobile service providers, which results from their reactions.
The question of whether this is really just a coincidence is obvious.
Just as the absolutely same gasoline price at the petrol stations is certainly only a coincidence and the consequence of a fierce competition.
“In this matter, the Authority has discovered no indication of any action contrary to competition, and it therefore has no reason whatsoever to intervene in the matter in any way.
None of the providers have a dominant market position, so he does not have the corresponding increased responsibility for his actions on the market.
The necessary instruments for opening up the mobile communications market to virtual providers are also likely to be lacked by the regulatory authority for telecommunications," Chalupa said in his statement.
The interpretation of the dominant position in the market is one of many contentious points.
Of course, there is an analysis of the relevant markets developed by ČTÚ, according to which telecommunications is a single market from the point of view of economic competition.
In such a market, of course, none of the Czech mobile operators has a dominant position.
However, some legal experts offer a different view - for the market entry of a virtual provider, the approval of the network operator is always necessary.
And the mobile service provider - logically - has the monopoly for its network.
This means that it necessarily occupies a dominant position in a particular segment.
However, even if one does not go along with this argument, it should at least be a warning for the ÚOHS that three mobile service providers are strikingly similar.
Complaints from a number of interested parties (including GTS Novera’s mentioned company prove that they encounter very similar obstacles in negotiations with all providers.
The transfer of responsibility to ČTÚ is then quite a common trick in telecommunication services, with which the ÚOHS distances itself from the whole matter.
This has already been observed in some similar cases.
Unfortunately, it has not been possible to enforcing the recent amendment to the Telecommunication Act to extend ČTÚ's competences to economic competition in telecommunication, although a number of experts had promised to improve the situation.
The representatives of the ÚOHS have to know all too well, but they probably have other interests.
It's already possible when no one's looking
The functioning of any pseudo-virtual providers, of which there are several in the Czech mobile networks, remains unnoticed.
These are companies that publish their own price lists for mobile telecommunication services, which, however, are only partly public.
Normally, these are companies that operate in the field of multi-level marketing.
Our own mobile phone services include Amway, Bohemia Telecom, Starlife and others.
The price lists of these companies usually only get to the chosen ones.
The joke of the matter is that the company acts as a classic corporate customer towards the operator, who orders a large amount of SIM cards.
As an important customer, he then receives special price offers (and other offers).
The problem is that the terms and conditions of all Czech mobile operators prohibit a similar resale.
"Should there be cases that describe you, then it can be a violation of the contractual conditions with which we will then deal, which can lead to a cessation of cooperation with the appropriate society," Martina Kemrová replied to our question about this problem.
However, it is quite unlikely that something like this will not get around with those responsible for the mobile operators.
Especially in the case of the three companies mentioned, it can be thousands of customers.
However, no operator opposes such an approach.
From the activities of these companies, they do not feed negligible profits, at the same time they are - in contrast to the actual virtual providers - are not affected by the actual official marketing.
Miroslav Čepický draws attention to possible problems: "The law allows such types of speculation, in spite of everything, the services of these companies are only used by a very small percentage of customers.
In addition, they have to accept that the SIM card in their phone does not run on their behalf, so they can’t have it in fully like the normal customers of the mobile service providers.”
But that alone, the ČTÚ would at least seem strange.
In addition, another question opens up for the ÚOHS.
Do the mobile providers turn a blind eye to such a procedure, why do they lock themselves against a virtual provider?
It should be added that ÚOHS has quite strong competences to uncover such activities, and if it does not want to, it does not have to be satisfied with the explanation of the providers that they know nothing about it and their terms and conditions would prohibit this.
When the Czech Republic is no longer most expensive
As has already been emphasized on several occasions in the text, the market entry of virtual providers entailed a significant decline in prices for the various services.
Virtually in all countries, where virtual providers have been created, the classic providers have tried to prevent their creation practically more or less.
The arguments they used were practically identical - a market that was too small or too saturated, problems in the quality of services, etc.
In the highly developed countries, however, the regulatory bodies intervened precisely at these moments, which by no means tried to safeguard the interests of mobile operators, but those of their customers.
Be it through formal measures or non-formal pressure, they achieved that the first virtual operator was created, followed by others.
Sadly, the Czech regulators are throwing the whole case like a hot potato.
Based on our experience, we cannot get rid of the impression that ambiguity in the competences in these cases is more than favorable.
However, we do not throw the shotgun in the grain.
If the background information is correct and a virtual provider shows up soon, the Czech mobile market may soon start moving again.
If a provider allows the emergence of a virtual mobile provider, the ÚOHS will not remain idle when it comes to discriminating (discriminating against) other interested parties.
And the Czechs won’t make more expensive calls than the rest of Europe.
The new Czech anthem has four new versions.
Finish the best
Listen to the four versions of the new Czech national anthem.
On Sunday, Prime Minister Mirek Topolánek presented the new recordings of the national anthem where my home country on the occasion of the Day of the Czech Statehood.
The hymn was re-recorded by the Orchestra of the National Theatre under the direction of Jiří Bělohlávek.
There are four versions available - with male or female solo vocals, in choral version and in an instrumental version.
Kateřina Kněžínková and Adam Plachetka took over the vocal version.
Otakar Jeremiáš and Jaroslav Krček are editors of the hymn versions.
On the server iHNed.cz you can listen to all four versions.
For Bělohlávek the Czech national song is one of the most beautiful hymns.
He had previously told the Czech Radio that he had selected singers for the solo variants with young, representative voices that are striking and popular.
"From the very beginning, I tried to maintain a clear diction with both and then with the choir, so that one understands the text well, so that a harmony arises," says the conductor.
The Czech national anthem celebrates its 175th anniversary next year.
The song Kde domov můj first resounded in 1834 on the stage of the Ständetheater in the originally Czech piece Fidlovačka by Josef Kajetán Tyl, the music by František Škroup.
It became popular, and people accepted it spontaneously.
The first verse of the song became an organic component of the national anthem after the creation of Czechoslovakia in 1918.
One of the seven state symbols remained even after the emergence of the independent Czech Republic.
What does the "new" hymn sound like
The Prague transport companies are also transporting a chip for the first time on Wednesday
Wednesday, October 1, 2008, will go down in the history of Prague's transport companies as a date on which the first passengers board a metro, tram or bus to carry an electronic coupon in their pocket instead of a paper ticket.
Around one hundred thousand persons who own the Opencard chip card can currently buy these.
Passengers can buy a 30-day, 90-day or annual coupon (valid from 1 January 2009) and at the same time put it on their chip card, namely in the information centres and branches of the transport establishments at Muzeum, Můstek, Nádraží Holešovice, Anděl, Roztyly, Letňany.
The coupons have also been available via the Internet on the pages of the transport companies www.dpp.cz since yesterday.
The service was used by over four hundred customers until the afternoon.
Those who use Internet sales can really forget the output window in the subway stations, where they have so far regularly had to wait in long lines for their paper tickets.
You buy the coupon over the Internet, it is played on the Opencard in one of the thirteen branches of the city library.
"At these points, there is also a switch with trained staff available to the travellers, which advises interested parties on the Internet purchase and also puts the coupon on the card," says the spokesperson for the project Martin Opatrný.
In addition to the libraries, you can also place the electronic coupons on self-servicing vending machines on the Opencard, which are installed in some subway vestibules.
What do such machines look like?
They are red, reminiscent of ticket devaluers, passengers may have already noticed them in the metro stations Roztyly, Budějovická, I. P. Pavlova, Florenc and Vltavská.
"Currently there are five of them in the city, but by the end of the year it should be eighty," adds Opatrný.
The coupon inspects a machine instead of the driver
The operation of the vending machines, which will work from tomorrow, is easy.
You only have to introduce the chip card, and the previously acquired coupon plays up by itself.
The electronic coupons will apply from tomorrow not only in the capital, but also in the surrounding area.
So in the suburban trains and on the bus lines with the numbers 300 to 499, with which especially the inhabitants from communities in the Central Bohemian region reach the capital.
"In the trains that are part of Prague's integrated traffic, train attendants have reading devices with which they can check the validity period of the coupons by time and zone," Filip Drápal of the Ropid organization explains the ticket control. This organization compiles the timetables for Prague's transport companies.
The suburban buses have card readers at the checkout at the driver's office.
When boarding, the chip card must not only be showned to the driver, but the opencard must be attached to a designated location on the side of the checkout.
With this, the driver checks the validity of the coupons.
"If a passenger wants to check the validity of the coupons played, nothing is easier than asking the driver for this information," Drápal said.
A passenger, who does not travel with suburban buses and no longer knows when the validity of the coupon ends, can in turn go to the self-servicing machines, where the coupons are placed on the card.
Here the information about the validity of his documents is displayed on the display of the device.
The Sudeten demanded from the U.S. a referendum
In 1945, the Sudeten Germans asked for the annexation of the Bohemian Forest to the United States.
This emerges from a letter stored in the collections of the South Bohemian Scientific Library.
If we cannot be part of Germany or Austria, we would like to live at least under the auspices of the United States of America.
With such a wish, some groups of Sudeten Germans are said to have approached the U.S. president in 1945.
This is evident, among other things, from a letter that has been preserved in a private archive and which is now kept in the collections of the South Bohemian Scientific Library.
In the letter, the Germans of Krumau ask the American president not to allow the return of the "Sudetengau" - which was taken on the basis of the Munich Agreement by Hitler - to Czechoslovakia.
"We understood the situation correctly and really want to be sure of the words that the American units do not enter German territory as oppressors and enemies, but as liberators," a letter, signed with the words "people to whom the fate of the Sudeten is dear."
Since the connection to Czechoslovakia makes the Sudeten Germans - in knowledge of the Czech national character - suggest that they would expect brutality, recklessness, incomprehension, oppression and slavery, the authors of the letter ask the American president to assume protection over the Bohemian Forest forever.
"We cannot believe that, regardless of the view of a quarter of a million inhabitants of this area, you grant this territory to a state that oppresses and tries to eliminate minorities and tried to eliminate and has contributed nothing to the victory of the Allies," the letter reads.
The reason why the writers cannot sign is supposedly the fear of vengeance cravings of "Czech oppressors".
If the U.S. president had doubts about the opinion of the Bohemian Forest Germans, it would be sufficient if he would initiate a referendum in the Böhmerwaldgau on whether the inhabitants wanted to become part of the USA or live under the patronage of the United States.
Although the text of the letter may sound somewhat naïve in today's knowledge of post-war history, in the opinion of some contemporary witnesses it paints a good picture with regard to the expectations of Sudeten Germans at that time.
Even if the Bohemian Germans at least had to suspect that one would negotiate their deportation from the republic, only a few of them admitted such a possibility at all.
Some were waiting that the United States and Britain would negotiate a kind of autonomy for the Sudeten.
Still others believed they could simply stay in Czechoslovakia.
This is also confirmed by the famous Austrian journalist Gustav Chalupa, who is from Budweis.
He was used in the war as a Budweser German in the navy on the Baltic Sea.
When he came into British captivity at the end of the war and was taken to a prisoner-of-war camp in Holstein, dozens more Bohemian Germans came to him to teach them Czech.
"They believed that if they came home, the Czech would fit them," Chalupa said.
None of the German prisoners of war who had lived in Czechoslovakia before the war, in his opinion, expected that he would be expelled from the republic on the basis of the Beneš Decrees.
Don't think - then pay!
An emotion-packed campaign is aimed at motorists
Most expensive action for safety on the streets in Czech history.
This could also characterize the emotion-packed media campaign, which the Ministry of Transport intends to launch on Wednesday.
Minister Aleš Řebíček's authority pledges to dissuade drivers from dangerous behaviour behind the wheel.
The campaign for 150 million crowns will run on television, on the radio, in the cinema, on the internet, but also on flyers.
It is supposed to dissuade the drivers from too-fast driving and alcohol at the wheel.
And they also remind them to belted before driving.
The campaign intends to appeal to young drivers, motorcyclists, pedestrians and, last but not least, professional drivers.
And what's shown in the spots?
Above all, stories of people appealing to emotions.
This is also evidenced by the "bloody" slogan of the whole campaign:
Don't think - then pay!
The words "doesn't think" are in capital letters on a license plate.
And in a pool of blood then stands the word "pay".
This means that you can pay for a moment of inattention with your own life.
The spots will "shock" viewers by 2010.
The ministry was inspired by similar campaigns in the Anglo-Saxon countries, where you can normally see pictures with drastic shots of accident victims.
It expects the number of accidents and the number of traffic fatalities on Czech roads to decrease.
The spots have been compiled by the Euro RSCG agency.
Sugarbread and whip for motorists
“For the drivers, the motto applies sugar bread and whip.
Sugarbroad means that if they drive at red, for example, they save time.
The whip is then the threat of a penalty or an accident.
The driver must be reminded of the risk," according to Na Homolce Martin Kořán hospital traffic psychologist.
And that's why information campaigns in the press and, above all, negative advertising are necessary, says the psychologist.
In his opinion, panels that alert drivers to a danger are already less effective.
"If you put up a static plaque that warns of a danger, it will probably soon flow with the environment, and the drivers will no longer perceive it," says Kořán.
Olmert would return almost all occupied territories in exchange for peace
The chairman of the provisional Israeli government, Ehud Olmert, has advocated the return of virtually all territories that were occupied in 1967, i.e. the Jordan country, East Jerusalem and the Golan heights, if one could make peace with Palestine and Syria in exchange.
He emphasized this in an interview, which was printed on Monday by the daily Jediot Achronot.
"We have to reach an agreement with the Palestinians, which means it will be necessary to return almost all (occupied) territories, if not all," Olmert told the newspaper.
"We retain a certain percentage of these areas, but we will have to cede a similarly high percentage (Israeli territory) to the Palestinians because there will be no peace without this," he added.
He acknowledged that an eventual return would also include East Jerusalem.
"The one who wants security in Jerusalem and, above all, doesn't want tractors or bulldozers to roll over his legs, as happened to one of my best friends, will have to forgo part of Jerusalem," Olmert remarked, alluding to two attacks in the city in July that killed several people and injured dozens.
According to the prime minister, it will be necessary to find a "special solution for the templary and holy and historical places" that lie on the territory of the old town.
“The one wishing to uphold Israel’s sovereignty over the entire city will have to integrate 270,000 Arabs.
This won't work," he continued.
Withdrawing from the Golan heights
Olmert advocated a withdrawal of Israel from the Golan heights, which in his view is an indispensable condition for the conclusion of a peace treaty with Syria.
However, this would also require some effort on the part of Damascus.
"My proposal is not to make peace with Syria solely on the basis of a withdrawal from the Golan heights," Olmert affirmed.
“The Syrians know all too well what they have to forego in order to get the Golan heights back.
They will have to forego their present relationship with Iran; they will have to renounce their relationship with the (Shiite) Hezbollah movement; they will have to renounce the constant support they grant to the terrorism of the (Shite) Hamas movement, the Al-Khaida terrorist network and the jihad (the holy war) in Iraq," the prime minister clarified.
Questions after the accident: Are stewards on the bus unlawful?
The law doesn't know them, in a long-distance co-travel busing is not allowed
In the context of the tragic accident of a student agency bus located near the Italian Padua, the question arises whether stewards and travellers are allowed to walk back and forth on the bus while driving.
"If we wanted to strictly and formally comply with the law, the activity of a steward is contrary to the law on road traffic," said the spokesman for the Ministry of transport Karel Hanzelka.
The accident, in which a twenty-two-year-old stewardess died last week, was likely caused by the driver of the lorry that crossed the path to the bus.
At that moment, the young woman sat next to all the misfortune next to the driver, on the least safe place in the car, even though she had two more places at her disposal.
However, a bus can also have an accident or brake sharply when the steward carries coffee or newspapers.
It also plays a role whether the bus is also equipped with standing places according to the vehicle certificate.
If this is not provided on the bus, travelers will have to sit.
Student Agency's long-distance coaches have no standing seats; which the company's director, Radim Jančura, confirmed to Aktuálně.cz.
“We have discussed this with the ministry, and this did not first consent to the movement of travellers.
Later, this was taken back, and we have reached a consensus that travelers are allowed to go to the toilet while driving when they have their seat.
The law doesn't know a steward
"There is no clear legal interpretation for the role of a steward - no legislation describes this role.
However, there is also no legislation prohibiting the carrier from providing services.
The law on road traffic only knows the term vehicle attendant; however, its rights and responsibilities do not define it," the spokesman for the Ministry Hanzelka clearly adds.
Josef Pokorný's Road Vehicles Department, on the other hand, thinks that the law retrospectively covers this issue.
“It depends on whether the bus also has standing places in the product approval.
That's all then," he insists.
Buses are divided into city buses, where there are standing places and the maximum speed is limited, local buses with standing seats and a higher permitted speed and long-distancent coaches without standing spaces with the highest permissible speed and seats equipped with seat belts.
As in an airplane, says the professional
BESIP specialist for traffic safety Jaroslav Hořín sees no fundamental problem in the movement of stewards aboard a bus.
“With a smooth driving style, not many dangers threaten, the situation is comparable to the service on board an aircraft.
Again, it’s not sure when turbulence will come, and the stewardess can hit its head.”
“However, if a bus brakes sharply, then of course the laws of physics also apply.
Just like on a city bus or on a tram.
The safety of the stewards must be clarified by the carrier through internal regulations," he says and also thinks that this problem is not completely solved from the point of view of the law.
Jančura is ready to cancel the services
Student Agency is the only bus company to offer services from stewards.
The company employs about two hundred people in this function.
The director doesn't regard their profession as high-risk even after the accident.
“However, as soon as I get the impression that the steward’s profession is risky, I’m going to delete it.
Coffee and newspapers can be obtained by travellers when boarding.
This would also reduce costs," he says.
As an interest in safety, Asiana Alexei Litvin's director justifies the lack of stewards on his buses. However, his company operates only a fraction of lines in comparison with Student Agency.
“We don’t have stewardesses, we’re resisting from the very beginning.
Sooner or later, this will end with any fatal accident," he had said for Týden.cz not quite a year ago.
This was an incredible tragedy
In the accident of a scheduled bus to Naples, a young stewardess from the Pilsen region, who had worked for two years for the accident.
The Slovenian truck had probably bent off the standing strip without blinking and had blocked the bus.
The driver of the bus attempted to dodge through a sharp manoeuvre but collided with the truck with the right front.
Jančura denies that the two seats for the stewards were sold.
“These places cannot be reserved on long-distance connections, they are blocked.”
Company wants to sue truck drivers.
With compensation for the young woman's family, the agency will not wait for the courtial verdict, but she also expects financial damages from the culprit.
A change in the law?
Not in sight
A more precise regulation of the movement of stewards aboard a bus could bring a legal regulation on which a thirty-strong team of experts from the Ministry of Transport has been working for over a year.
"However, this has not yet reached a concrete conclusion.
Buses aren't necessarily on the agenda either, rather the points system, the top speed on the highway and the use of mobile phones while driving," according to expert Hořín.
"The Ministry of Transport does not regard the activity of the stewards as something that could significantly jeopardize the safety of travellers," Hanzelka sailing the control of the safety of the buses lies within the competence of the police and occupational safety authorities.
Duesseldorf hopes rest on Frankfurt
There is no shortage of big words in the WestLB board's letter to the employees of the house.
By bringing units of the Düsseldorf-based financial institution with Frankfurt-based Deka-Bank, "the central capital market house of the Sparkassen-Finanzgruppe" could be created, according to the letter announced on Monday.
WestLB would bring a "bloc of competence" with sustainable earning power into such a business.
And in general, such a connection could be the decisive impetus for further bundlings in the Landesbanken sector.
This is how it reads when a board wants to demonstrate capacity for action in a difficult time.
Because the ailing WestLB is still under enormous pressure.
Not only on the part of the EU Commission, but also by the Federal Financial Supervisory Authority, which has only recently made it clear to the North Rhine-Westphalian state government the time to find a solution for the future.
However, there has been talk of a similar connection between Deka-Bank, the savings bank customers because of its investment funds, and WestLB have been mentioned for some time - also on the Frankfurt side.
Although Deka-Bank CEO Franz Waas let it be known at the presentation of the half-year figures at the end of August, in a merger of the house on Mainzer Landstraße with a Landesbank, such as Helaba or WestLB, he sees no sense.
However, the Board of Directors of Deka, which is each half supported by the Landesbanken and the savings bank associations, has instructed the Executive Board to hold discussions with WestLB on joint business activities.
It could be about the capital markets business or parts of it.
Of course, the talks have only just begun in the financial centre.
Deka-Bank, which is one of the more important employers in the Frankfurt financial centre with its 3000 employees, is not the first time when it comes to consolidation in the savings bank sector.
In 2003, it looked for a moment that Deka-Bank could merge with Landesbank Rheinland-Palatinate and Helaba.
However, there was nothing out of this covenant of three.
As soon as the plans had become public, the Hessian state government spoke out against Helaba’s participation in it.
The other two houses did not find each other.
Landesbank Rheinland-Palatinate went instead to Landesbank Baden-Württemberg in Stuttgart.
However, there have always been games of thought about a connection of Deka-Bank with Landesbanken.
Most recently, however, Günther Merl, CEO of Helaba, had spoken out against simulation games about a meeting with his house.
Irrespective of this, however, the realisation seems to mature that that a coming together of the various houses in the public-sectoral sector is doing hardship.
After the state election in Bavaria is now over, a connection of BayernLB with the Landesbank Baden-Württemberg could be approaching first.
This isn't necessarily good news for Helaba.
If the pressure for further mergers increases, you will gradually run out of partners for it.
In the south, she would only be junior partners, even though she has so far experienced the financial crisis far better than the houses in Munich and Stuttgart.
At the beginning of the year, the Sparkassen- und Giroverband Hessen-Thüringen, which holds an 85 percent stake in Helaba, put a stop to a merger with West LB.
At most, the Nord LB would remain.
However, such geostrategic considerations are only one side.
On the other hand, there is the question of how the different business models of the individual houses fit together.
In June, Merl said that the crucial question with which business model the future viability of Landesbanken should be ensured had not yet been answered by the German Saving Bank and Giro Association.
In the end, there could also be a disassembly of the West LB, which, of course, is not desired there.
The letter from the local board also mentions that an "overall solution" for the house will definitely be sought.
How this can be reconciled with the merger of individual units with the Deka-Bank will still be shown.
The second Wiesn weekend is known as "Italian weekend"
For Elena it is the first time, for Luca already the third or fourth time.
But this time Elena is there as his girlfriend, and so everything is different, he says.
Behind them, in the five-litre beer barrel of the Kaiser brand, stands a massively built bearder and proves the approximately 200 Oktoberfest travelers what deep tones his throat can produce.
Elena gives Luca a slap first, then a kiss.
They came to Verona from Bologna.
From here, the bus parking lot next to Puorta Nuova station, four buses, "destinazione Monaco, festa della birra," arrive at 6.30pm this evening at 23.45 p.m.
Three buses are mainly reserved for the Italians, in a bus travel exclusively Brazilians.
In one corner of the parking lot, the South Americans are already singing next to their flag, they have already celebrated at night.
And so loudly, Marco Guarda says, that's not for Italians.
Marco, who wears a long coat with fur lining and glasses with wide silver temples, belong to the buses.
They have been going to the Oktoberfest for five years, there are three tours this year alone.
At night, during the day on the Wiesn, back at 22.30.
Staying overnight would only mean unnecessary expenses, Marco says.
75 euros costs the trip, the welcome drink with Kaiser beer, brewed according to the Bavarian Purity Law, health insurance and the German-speaking tour guide - Marco has attended a language course in Frankfurt - included.
And already the buses roll into the night, on the Autostrada towards "Brennero".
There's no beer yet.
It quickly becomes quiet in the bus, the travelers are asleep.
All right, they'll still need their powers.
It is held in Brixen and in Holzkirchen, where several other buses with Italian license plates take a break on Autobahn 8 shortly before Munich.
When Marco's group finally arrives in the parking lot near Theresienwiese at half past seven o'clock in the morning, there are already about 50 other buses from all over Europe.
Elena, Luca and the others gather, Marco gives a final address, explains the attacking plan.
First of all, put on the yellow cap.
On it "Oktoberfest", in fracture, next to it is the Internet address of Marco's travel agency: "conoscersiviaggiando.it".
Secondly: "When the doors open to the beer tent, it's time to walk!", Marco shouts into the morning.
"Run!" Running and occupying the seats in the back of the tent.
"Not in the front, otherwise we block, and not all yellow hats on the same benches, spread in the room!"
The measure, roars Marco, costs 8.25 euros.
Don’t forget tips.
“Showing to the security guards that we’re behaving well!
As soon as you leave the seats, they're gone!"
Thirdly: "Tonight point half-eleven come back here again!" Understood?
And it's on to Wiesn.
On the way, a yellow cap binds to the trees, to signs and even to the election poster of a large Bavarian People's Party.
You want to be prepared for withdrawal.
It's the second Wiesn weekend, known as "Italians Weekend."
There is no precise information about figures; there is talk of 50 000 who make their way across the Alps and invade the beer tents, ten percent of all visitors are said to have traveled especially from Italy this weekend.
The Munich police are reinforced by seven police officers from South Tyrol, Italian volunteers support the Bavarian Red Cross, which conducts national statistics, in which the Italians take first place in front of Britons (about 60 deployments) and Austrians (about 80) with around 200 medical cares.
Especially popular among Italians is the Hofbräuzelt.
Sleepless nights for creditors of Hypo Real Estate
The German banking system is relatively stable, emphasized German Finance Minister Peer Steinbrück only last week.
How good that the politician used the word "relative" and also weaves that the German banking sector would not be spared from the crisis-ridden developments.
Because it has now occurred at the weekend.
Munich-based real estate financier Hypo Real Estate (HRE), listed in the Dax, receives a billion-dollar loan from a consortium of German banks in order to shield itself from the turmoil in the markets.
A person familiar with the situation told Reuters on Monday the loan aid amounted to up to €35 billion.
The Hypo Real Estate only has to pay interest on the part that it also uses.
The money comes from both private and public banks.
The background is losses with the daughter Depfa.
According to a newspaper report for long-term projects for which he has lented money, the state financier had refinanced extremely short-term - which is no longer possible because of mistrust in the markets.
In the first half of 2008, the bank's cash reserve had shrunk by 95 percent to €2.37 million, and the equity ratio had also declined slightly from 1.6 to 1.45 percent.
Current income and net income from financial transactions had fallen to zero compared to the same period of the previous year, income from attributions to receivables and certain securities had fallen by more than 70 percent to € 5 million.
Only thanks to higher income from reimbursements of downstream clearingling costs from the restructuring of the former Depfa Group as well as the reimbursement of interest reimbursements in accordance with the tax code and from the company’s operating procurement for group companies, the earnings decline of around 16 percent was still moderate.
The former state financier had initially speculated after his privatization in 1990 with commercial real estate loans, which had then been spun off in Aareal Bank.
Because one earned too little in classic state financing, Depfa had then increasingly engaged in infrastructure projects and the financing of joint projects of private investors and the state (Private-Public-Partnership, PPP) and diversified internationally.
On the value of the subsidiary, Hypo Real Estate must now depreciation and depreciation, which will have a "substantial material effect" on the group's profit and loss account, it is said quite indefinitely.
The factual is that the dividend has been cancelled.
It had already been cut by two-thirds for 2007.
Hypo Real Estate has been a major state and infrastructure financier since the Depfa takeover.
It is more dependent than other institutions on refinancing in the interbanch market, as it has no customer deposits.
With the collapse of American investment bank Lehman Brothers two weeks ago, lending among banks has once again largely dried up as banks hoard their liquidity.
Hypo Real Estate needs short-term funds to refinance every year for a part - around €50 billion - of Depfa's loan portfolio.
According to Hypo Real Estate, the financing needs of the group with the loan are covered for the foreseeable future and the company is shielded "from the influence of the currently largely inoperable international money markets".
From other sources it can be heard that thanks to the funds, the refinancing is secured until the end of 2009.
HRE CEO Georg Funke, however, stated that the Hypo Real Estate Group will no longer have to use the money markets for the foreseeable future.
Bundesbank and the Federal Financial Supervisory Authority (BaFin) assume that the marketability of the Hypo Real Estate Group is now secured.
That stands for shareholders and creditors of the bank to hope.
But given the dynamics and uncertainties of the crisis, there is arguably no certainty.
It is even unthinkable that funding does not have to be used in its entirety.
But even the opposite cannot be ruled out.
After late Sunday evening, according to the bank was still facing bankruptcy, the at least provisional rescue is not able to calm the minds.
The share price slumps by 75 percent to €3.30.
On the other hand, bond prices hold up quite well.
While the priority debt is barely moving, the prices of subordinated bonds are yielding more significantly.
Affected above all are the bonds of the former subsidiary Württemberg Hypo and understandablely the maturities beyond 2009.
If hypo-real-estate bonds formerly seem a speculative investment alternative, their valuability must be considered dependent on the stability of the international financial system after the significant intensification of the banking crisis and the institution itself.
As long as the central banks, governments and less afflicted commercial banks are still ready to support the system, it is possible to assume a timely repayment of pensions.
Or this occurs, which hardly anyone dares to hope at the moment - that the financial crisis will be overcome in the coming 15 months.
However, in view of the reports with the partial nationalization, the probability bordering on security has currently only become a legitimate hope.
Above all, long-term creditors are likely to give some sleepless nights with the decision whether to separate themselves from their engagements.
Short-term investors can at least use a final line to form a loss carryforward for the future withholding tax payments on course gains of later years.
Sylvana and the Secrets of the Bento Box
When Sylvana Hiltrop, 16, is supposed to introduce herself on the first day of school at Tachibana high school, the girls start giggling: The German is totally "kawaii"!
What does that mean?
Sylvana already knows the word from mangas - in the new exchange log, she tells about Japan.
“The first day of school in Japan.
I was excited.
Even before the way to school, I had bamboos.
I live on the edge of Tokyo towards Kawasaki and take the train to Tachibana high school for 40 minutes.
I had to find the way alone, fortunately I didn’t get lost.
The start of classes does not seem to be as strictly fixed at the Tachibana as at home in Lower Saxony.
The students trudge in from eight o'clock, until at twenty before nine a short meeting round begins with the teacher, the 'home room'.
But before the lessons started, it was my turn to introduce myself.
I’m not the guy who likes to talk in front of people, especially in front of a class of 40 students.
Because I didn't have a school uniform yet, I put on an old skirt of my host mother, blouse, blue knee socks and black leather shoes.
So in this elevator I put myself in front of the desk and tried to pronounce everything correctly: 'Sayonara Wo she Sylvana...' - I'm called Sylvana, I'm from Germany and have my birthday in a week.
At that moment I was very happy that I had already started learning the language about two years ago.
The 31 girls in the class were all whipping, giggling and wavering and constantly I heard the word "Kawaii".
I already knew that from my mangas.
It belongs to the vital vocabulary of Japanese girls and means 'cute, cute'.
Then I'm quickly scurried to my place.
The girl next to me grinned quite broadly, she found it an honor that the exchange student should sit next to her.
The rest of the hour was pretty quiet, but when the gong sounded, I were really rushed.
Everyone talked and chuckled and asked holes in my belly - it's really hard to understand Japanese English.
At the break, everyone unpacked her Bentos.
My host mother had also made me such a lunch box - that's something different from the desolate German sandwiches.
I had rice with herbs, small sausages, salad from cucumbers and algae, fruit and a kind of meat in tofu coat.
I usually don’t have a pale idea what I’m eating here in Japan.
Things look strange and taste completely novel.
What's certain is that everything's really delicious.
Well, except for the salty pickled plums maybe, brrrr!
Bentos aren’t just lunch at Japanese schools.
It sometimes happens that a girl gives a self-prepared bento to a boy, which she then prepares particularly artfully - in the hope of getting to know the boy better during a break snack.
I've read about that in my manga comics.
I’m curious to see if I can also observe this.
'Why Japan?' my friends asked me when I told them that I wanted to go to school in Tokyo for a year.
Then I always said,
“Because it fascinates me.”
After all, I’ve loved reading manga for years.
And so that I better understand the comics and the culture in it, I also started to learn a bit of Japanese at some point.
So it was perfectly clear to me that I had to travel here at some point.
Because I also have a penchant for tradition, customs and customs.
And they attach great importance to here.
On the one hand, Japan is a country of the latest technologies and trends - on the other hand, it's strict, disciplined and traditional.
And because it’s so well organized, I haven’t felt lost here either, even if Tokyo is the biggest city I’ve ever seen.”
Rats also have feelings
A rat or cockroach flees in case of danger does this mean that it also feels fear?
Neuroscientist Joseph LeDoux is convinced that a lot of animals also have feelings.
But how could you check if they feel the same thing if they behave similarly to us?
Contributions by famous scientists have been published in the Internet magazine " Edge" for several years.
SPIEGEL ONLINE presents selected answers to the question "What do you think is true without being able to prove it?"
I believe that animals have sensations and other states of consciousness without me or anyone else being able to prove this so far, where we can’t even prove that other people have consciousness.
But in this case, we can at least hope, since all human brains have the same basic configuration.
However, as soon as we turn to other species and raise questions of sensation or consciousness in general, we enter impassable terrain because the hardware doesn’t match.
When a rat is in jeopardy, it does the same thing as many other animals: it stands dead, flees or attacks.
People behave similarly, from which some scientists suggest a comparable subjective experience.
I don't believe in that, though.
Two aspects of brain structure make it difficult to transfer our subjective experience to that of animals.
On the one hand, the circuits almost always associated with human consciousness include the lateral prefrontal cortex (because of its involvement in short-term memory and execution/control functions).
This broad region is much more developed in humans than in the other primates and seems to be completely absent from other creatures.
At least in those aspects of our consciousness that depend on the prefrontal cortex, including the knowledge of who we are, and the ability to plan and decide, there is reason to the assumption that man even differs from the other primates.
Another dramatic peculiarity is likely to lie in the linguistic nature of man: Since such a large part of his experience is associated with language, it is often said that consciousness depends on it.
In this case, animals could be excluded.
But even if consciousness did not depend on language, it would certainly affect it, so that animal consciousness, as it may also be constituted, is probably very different from most of our states of consciousness.
That’s why it’s hard to determine what consciousness may look like in other animals.
If, as something internal and subjective, it can neither be measured nor, because of the special hardware, can be analyzed on the basis of our own experience, its exploration presents us with great problems.
Most of my explanations refer to the content of conscious experience, but there is also another aspect of consciousness, which should be scientifically more easily accessible.
One could examine the consciousness processes of animals without regard to their contents, and this is exactly what happens in studies on the short-term memory of primates, in particular on the example of visualization.
But in this approach, which Christof Koch and Francis Crick followed, one shifts to the neural correlates of consciousness rather than to the causal mechanisms: both may or may not be identical.
Interestingly, this approach also stresses the importance of the prefrontal cortex for the formation of visualization.
What now is the result of the sensations?
I attribute them to the fact that an emotional system such as that of fear is activated in a brain conscious of its own activity.
Thus, what we call "fear" is the mental state we find ourselves in when the activity of the immune system localized in the brain (or its consequences, such as physical reactions) proves memory.
From this point of view, sensations are closely tied to the regions of the cortex that characterize primates and in particular humans.
Combined with linguistic nature, there are fine gradations of sensation, because with the help of the words and the grammar we can differentiate and categorize states in order to attribute them to others in addition to ourselves.
Antonio Damasio thinks, for example, that sensations emerge from more primitive activities in the body-feeling regions of the cortex and the brain stem similar to Jaak Pankseep, which, however, focuses more on the brain stem.
Since this network has not changed much in the course of human evolution, it may well be involved in cross-general sensations.
Purely theoretically, I can't disagree with that, but I think it's unprovable.
Pankseep argues that what seems like fear among rats and people, probably feels like this in both cases: But how can you check whether rats and people feel the same thing if they behave similarly?
A cockroach flees in danger of whether it also feels fear?
In my view, mere similarity of behavior cannot yet prove equivalence.
Although the neural comparison helps further: rats and humans have similar brain strains, cockroaches do not even have a brain.
But is the brain stem responsible for sensations?
And even if you could prove this for humans: How did you want to prove it in rats?
I believe that rats and other mammals, maybe even cockroaches (who knows?), have sensations.
But I don't know how to prove it.
And because their sensations presumably differ fundamentally from ours (since the human consciousness is based on special circuits and language), I would rather explore the emotional behavior than the emotional sensations of rats.
In any case, progress on the neural level beckons here, provided that you measure the same with rats and people.
How I do not explore language and awareness in rats, even sensations, I don’t know if they exist.
You might think that's short-sighted, but I'd rather move forward rather than constantly run against a wall.
I'm just a hands-on emotionalist.
