Prices for airline tickets and hotels are falling
In the first half of 2008, flights and overnight stays in Europe were cheaper than in the previous year.
In Germany, flights became up to 50 percent cheaper, according to a recent study.
Flights and overnight stays in hotels have become cheaper again - this is the result of a study by the travel and financial service provider American Express.
The company analyzed its booking data from the first half of 2008 and noticed a significant slump in the second quarter following an increase in prices in the first quarter.
After the prices on the hotel market have risen for years, the overnight stays in many places have already cheapened.
From January to March, prices in 41 out of 48 European cities surveyed still rose by an averaging 6.9 percent compared to 2007.
From April to June, however, prices were 3.6 percent lower than in the previous year.
The trend was most pronounced in Paris and Rome, where travelers had to pay 30 to 37 percent less than a year earlier.
As a cause of this, American Express sees the weak dollar exchange rate, which has led to a “decreasing travel appetite of American tourists”.
Financial centers such as Frankfurt or London are also more affected by the decline in hotel prices than industrial locations such as Hanover, Cologne or Stavanger.
In the Norwegian city, hotel prices even hold the record in Europe with an increase of 26.3 percent.
Even in terms of air fares, the trend in the first quarter was still upwards.
Due to kerosene interesting, travelers in economy class had to pay on average 7.1 percent more than in 2007, while fuel-intensive long-haul flights became up to 29.2 percent more expensive.
From April to June, economy-class airline ticket prices fell by an average of 12.5 percent compared to the previous quarter, but in business and first-class price changes were much smaller.
In Germany, prices plummeted even more sharply than in other European countries.
From January to March, prices were up 11.9 per cent, down 23.6 percent in the second quarter.
On German domestic flights, prices have even halved, according to the study.
The reason for the price slump is, on the one hand, slowing economic growth, the company explains.
Demand is crumbling in many segments of the air travel market.
On the other hand, there is an increased competitive pressure due to the open-skies agreement, which liberalizes air traffic between the USA and Europe.
Sign of the tense situation was the cancellation of unprofitable routes with airlines, as well as the high number of airlines, which had declared bankruptcy in the first half of 2008 - in Europe alone these were 24 airlines.
