Dark matter influences flight from space probes
For years, a mysterious phenomenon in research has caused perplexity: dark matter influences the speed of space probes during so-called swing-by-manoeuvres.
Experts fear Albert Einstein's general theory of relativity might not be quite right.
For almost 18 years, the "Ulysses" spacecraft flew through space.
Because of soon-to-freal fuel lines, she now faces cold death.
Among scientists, a mysterious phenomenon has caused confusion for years - the swing-by anomaly.
The swing-by is a flight manoeuvre of space probes in which they gain additional momentum in the gravitational field of a celestial body, such as Earth.
As a result, the probes can save a lot of fuel.
Cassini-Huygen's European-American research probe used this physical trick, as did the Ulysses solar probe.
However, what the researchers have been surprised for years: The missiles do not behave 100 percent as you would expect according to the time-honoured law of gravity.
Rather, there are irregularities in speed.
The probes are unexpectedly getting faster or slower.
Until now, the physicists could not explain this effect.
They christened him swing-by anomaly.
Some experts worry that even Albert Einstein's general theory of relativity, which describes the attraction of matter through a curvature of space, might not be quite right.
American physicist Stephen Adler of Princeton's Institute for Advanced Study still firmly believes Einstein wasn't mistaken, trying to explain the swing-by anomaly using the so-called dark matter that astronomers in the universe have identified.
"Responsible for the anomaly could be dark matter, which could also be located very close to Earth," he writes.
The space researchers have only known about the existence of dark matter for about ten years.
There is obviously an attraction from it, the effect of which can be observed on other celestial bodies.
On the other hand, it itself is completely invisible, i.e., it does not transmit and reflect any electromagnetic radiation, in particular no visible light.
According to the scientists’ calculations, dark matter accounts for well over a fifth of the total mass of the universe.
Tourists kidnapped in Egypt are free
After ten days in the hands of their kidnappers, the tourists deported in Egypt are to be free again, as the Italian foreign minister confirmed.
The kidnappers from Sudan had demanded several million euros of ransom money.
Six hostage-takers are said to have been killed in a shootout before the release.
