We're reviewing her state of health right now," Frattini said.
The five Germans, five Italians and a Romanian woman, as well as their eight Egyptian companions, were well up and safe, Egyptian state television reported.
The 19 participants of a desert expedition are already on their way to Cairo.
Unclear remains the background of a shooting before the release of the hostages: According to an Egyptian security official, the kidnappers were ready to end the kidnapping before the Sudanese army surprisingly killed six of the kidnappers.
“The problem was solved.
They agreed with a ransom," the official said.
"It's just a matter of receiving the hostages" when "this surprise" suddenly arrived.
Sudanese soldiers had shot dead six of the kidnappers and arrested two of the kidnappers on Sunday in the border area between Sudan, Chad and Egypt.
The hostage takers had demanded a ransom of six million euros from Germany; the money was to be handed over by the German wife to one of the hostages.
The daily "El Masri El Yom" reported, citing a German mediator, that the release of the hostages was delayed because the kidnappers feared for their safety.
According to the report, the kidnappers want to release the female hostages after paying the ransom.
The men should be detained until the kidnappers had a safe escape route.
The German embassy in Cairo declined to comment on the report.
Abuse of stepfather 'leaded to suicide'
The years-long abuse by her stepfather has caused a woman to kill herself, has been reported to a court.
Michael Trerise, 57, from Stithians, Cornwall, was given a six-year prison sentence after confessing eight counts of sexual abuse.
The Criminal Court in Truro learned that he regularly handcuffed his stepdaughter Stephanie Randle when she was aged between five and seven.
Trerise wrote in a confession: "I feel responsible for her death."
The court learned that Ms. Randle killed herself at her home in Mutley, Plymouth, on April 1, 2007, at the age of 34.
When she lived in Coventry as a child, Trerise had handcuffed her and blindfolded her eyes before performing sexual acts while her mother was away to work in a restaurant.
Trerise and his wife divorced in the 1980s and he moved back to Cornwall.
His stepdaughter went to Plymouth.
However, the court learned that Ms Randle, who volunteered a large part of her time for the National Trust, was prosecuted by the abuse that had been inflicted to her twice a week.
Prosecutor Ramsay Quaife reported to the court that friends had said Ms. Randle, who had been a zealous surfer and cyclist, had been 'emotionally dead' since 2006 and "gaved up her life"
In April 2007, her real father Terrance Randle found her dead in her apartment in Plymouth.
It is believed that she killed herself by suffocating herself using a plastic bag.
Mr Randle confronted his daughter's stepfather, who wrote a confession letter that ended this way: "I used to captivate her and blindfold her."
"I didn't sexually coerce them.
I feel responsible for her death.”
Judge Christopher Elwen told Trerise: "You're an extremely strange fetishist and it must've been terrifying for her victim, especially since she has only been seven.
This unpleasant story ends with the victim's suicide.
Worse is the fact that it is very unlikely that the matter would have ended in court if it had not done so."
He added: "I take note of what impact, according to the description of Stephanie's father and mother, has had her act on her daughter over the years.
She would otherwise clearly have been a promising, successful and happy young woman.”
Officers responsible for lost data will be charged
The BBC has learned that the cabinet official, who lost strictly confidential documents on a train in June, will be indicted under the "Public Security Act."
The man was seconded by the Ministry of Defence when he lost two highly confidential documents on a train to Waterloo.
The documents were handed over to the BBC before they were handed over to police.
It's the weakest indictment within the law and there's no indication of criminal intent.
BBC defence correspondent Frank Gardner said the action had been a surprise for many in Whitehall.
He said: "They would have thought it would deal internally with the Ministry of Defence and Cabinet."
The employee, whose name cannot be named, will be indicted pursuant to paragraph 8.1 of the law, the clause dealing with the secure retention of information.
The royal prosecutor's office recommended the officer should be indicted by the Metropolitan police for the highly sensitive nature of his work in the Joint Intelligence Committee.
The documents he misplaced included classified assessments about Al-Qaeda and the capacities of the Iraqi security forces.
He was notified of Monday morning's decision and was taken from his home to a secrectared location.
Housing construction companies face B&B costs
British housing associations could face costs of more than £800 million in the home after bleeting Bradford and Bingley Bank.
The government provided £14 billion to protect Bradford & Bingley's deposits.
The interest on this loan will be paid by all companies that receive money from investors, including the 59 building companies
The director-general of the Association of Housing Associations (BSA), Adrian Coles, said it was "intolerable" that the companies and their members had to pay.
The $14 billion loan will ultimately be repaid when Bradford & Bingley's loans are repaid.
But interest rates accumulated in the meantime are crediteded each year to those 700 financial firms accepting deposits.
The programme to compensate financial services confirmed to the BBC that the 59 housing associations were included in this figure.
It is bitter that those institutions that have behaved prudently ... are now called upon to cover some of the costs of those institutions that have been far less prudent.
Adrian Coles, BSA
It said that each company would have to pay in proportion to the deposits it would hold, and that societies together would have to pay about 18% of the costs.
The Ministry of Finance estimates that interest on the loan will amount to £450 million in 2009.
This means the companies will have to procure £81 million for first-year interest rates.
Adrian Coles, the director-general of the Association of Housinding Associations, told the BBC that he believed this was unfair.
It is bitter that those institutions that have behaved prudently during the real estate upswing are now called upon to bear some of the costs from those institutions that were far less prudent.
According to the BSA, no society has failed since the records existed in 1945.
"We will observe all aspects of this thing over the next few weeks to see what alternatives there are to protect housing associations and their members from potentially significant costs."
The interest due in 2009 covers the seven months from the end of September this year to the end of March.
In 2010, interest rates are due for a full year and that could be almost twice as much.
Since building societies are bilateral organizations without shareholders, their members will have to pay for the cost either through higher fees or lower interest on savings accounts.
Milk becomes five pence per litre more expensive
The people of Jersey are faced with having to pay more for their milk.
Jersey Dairy has announced it will raise its milk's wholesale market price by another five pence per litre from Monday.
It's the second price increase this year, after a two pence increase in February, and comes after investigations by a consulting firm have been carried out.
Retailers are to decide for themselves whether they want to pass on the price increase to their customers.
Before the price increase in February, Jersey Milk's wholesale price last changed in 2002.
Former prime minister of Bangladesh is denied bail
A court in Bangladesh has dismissed a bail request from former Prime Minister Sheikh Hasina, meaning she could be arrested if she returns to the country.
Her defense lawyers said the court denied bail regarding the indictment blackmailed 50 million Taka ($729,700) from a businessman while she was prime minister.
The case is one of several hovering charges of corruption against them.
Sheikh Hasina's longtime political rival, Khaleda Zia, was released on bail earlier this month.
Sheikh Hasina is due to return to Bangladesh in the next few weeks, but her party, the Awami alliance, demanded that the military-backed transitional government should first grant her unrestricted freedom.
Correspondents say that although their suspension of imprisonment has been granted to go abroad for medical treatment, it is not clear as part of her release whether she will be able to remain on the loose when she returns to Bangladesh.
Her lawyers state that she will now appeal to the Supreme Court's decision against the Supreme Court's decision.
"The upper court rejected Sheikh Hasina's bail request because it says she doesn't have to be granted bail, as she is already on imprisonment suspension abroad to be treated abroad," her lawyer Rafique-ul Huq said.
We're going to file a legal complaint against the upper court."
Her lawyers arge that she needs bail to return to the country from abroad to prepare for December's elections.
Sheikh Hasina is expected to return home after October 17, an Awami-Alliance party chairman said on Sunday.
She was jailed last year after the army-backed transitional government took power but had been released from prison in June to go abroad for medical treatments.
Both Sheikh Hasina and Khaleda Zia deny all charges against themselves.
Fears over anti-polio initiative in Afghanistan
Nearly 200 000 children have not been vaccinated in the latest anti-polio initiative in Afghanistan due to the violence associated with the Taliban.
The United Nations carried out a vaccination programme in six provinces last week.
However, they could not reach children in parts of Helmand and Kandahar in the south for safety reasons.
According to a spokesman for the U.N., this was the case despite the Taliban's pledge not to attack or impede the thousands of staff carrying out the vaccinations.
BskyB loses lawsuit in ITV case
BskyB has lost its lawsuit against the Competition Commission's decision to reduce its share of rival broadcaster ITV.
The Competitive Appeals Court has ordered BskyB to reduce its stake in ITV from 17.9% to below 7.5%.
Previously, the Commission had claimed that BskyB's share would distort competition and allow it to have an unfair influence on broadcaster ITV.
BskyB acquired its stake in 2006 by successfully preventing NTL, now renamed Virgin Media.
In April 2007, the Competition Authority reported that BskyB's purchase of ITV's share of ITV's confidence, prompting the competition commission to launch an investigation.
Virgin Boss Sir Richard Branson, consumer groups, and the Ofcom communications office have spoken out against the purchase.
BskyB argued the pressure to reduce its stake in ITV was an unreasonable and exaggerated measure, in particular because BskyB had announced he wanted to give up his voting rights.
New Investigation in the U.S. Prosecutoral Affear
The U.S. attorney general has instructed a special investigator to deal with the termination of the nine prosecutors in the U.S. in 2006.
The occasion was a report by the Justice Department's Inspector General criticizing the actions of the administration staff.
The report criticized White House officials, lawmakers, and former attorney general Alberto Gonzales.
The inquiry was driven by fears the lawyers had been deposed for political reasons.
Special investigator Nora Dannehy is now to clarify whether the officers interrogated during the investigated during the investigatiary have lined investigators to obstruct the judiciary.
Serious allegations, including criminal action, have not been fully investigated or clarified, Inspector General Glenn Fine said in his report.
The appointment of a special investigator with the authority to force the witnesses to testify was one of his main recommendations.
The conflict began when the Department of Justice removed nine U.S. prosecutors from their offices in December 2006.
At that time, the ministry argued that prosecutors would be dismissed due to weak jobs.
However, it was later found that the dismissed prosecutors had been judged no worse than many of their colleagues, prompting many observers to consider the dismissals to be politically motivated.
Some of these prosecutors were involved in the judicial prosecution of senior Republican members of Congress, while others had refused to investigate the allegations overial fraud by Democrats.
U.S. prosecutors are tasked with investigating and prosecuting judicial cases for the administration.
They may be dismissed at any time but may not be deposed for political reasons.
The then head of the Ministry of Justice Attorney General Alberto Gonzales has come under fire for the dismissals and has ultimately resigned his office.
A number of Justice Department officials have also terminated.
The inspector general's report, in particular, has criticized Gonzales' role by bearing "primary responsibility" for the redundancies, "giving up his leadership role," and showing himself "extraordinarily disinterested" in the process
Other officials, including former principal adviser to President Bush Karl Rove, have been criticized for lacking cooperation with investigators.
Meat Loaf's epic song to be honoured
Rock star Meat Loaf is to be honored during the "Q Award" music award ceremony for his epic anthem "Bat Out Of Hell."
The singer, who will be accepting his special golden prize in London next week during the award ceremony, said he was honoured to be recognised.
The song, which lasts nine minutes and fifty seconds, was the ticle of Meat Loaf's 1977 hit album.
"Coldplay" leads the nominations for this year's awards, having been shortlisted in four categories.
"Q Magazine's" editor-in-chief Paul Rees said there were some songs that go beyond such matters as time and genre and "Bat Out Of Hell" was certainly one of them.
The song written by Jim Steinman required a powerful voice delivered by Meat Loaf.
It begins with an instrumental part that lasts nearly two minutes.
The song has been on sale in the UK as a single in 1979 and has reached #15 in the charts.
However, Meat Loaf's "Bat Out Of Hell" has spent about nine years in the charts, selling more than 40 million copies worldwide.
Meat Loaf, whose real name is Marvin Lee Aday, has suffered from serious health issues in recent years. One among these was surgery in 2003 because of irregular heartbeat after collapsing on stage.
In 2007, he had to cancel concerts due to an acute laryngeal inflammation.
Later it was revealed that he had a cyst on his vocal cord.
Obscure street in city, often forgotten and possibly sold soon
New York City has thousands of major thoroughfares, avenues, side streets and other bypasses, some of which are famous and only convenient.
Still, it's hard even for experienced taxi drivers and long-time local residents to say where Extra Place is located, simply because most have never heard of it.
This tiny street is located in the East Village and runs from First Stree north between Bowery and Second Avenue without reaching Second Street.
It is 30 feet wide and 120 feet long and looks more like a dusty alley rather than an actively used thoroughfare.
There's no road signs either.
It's virtually impossible to find them on a standard road map.
However, this street has its admirers.
For example, Avalon Bay, a construction company that recently constructed luxurious buildings on First Street.
It wants to repave Extra Place to create a clean drive-through to the stores and boutiques expected in the new buildings.
Other admirers include the long-standing neighborhood that says she's fascinated by the short alley, simply because she's one of the remnants of the block's past.
However, the city administration department, which is responsible for the protection and erection of the residential buildings and is responsible for the street, has no interest in preserving them.
During a meeting of the municipal council's land use committee this monny, representatives of the city administration and Avalon Bay drafted a proposal to buy the street by the construction company.
Nevertheless, the committee prefers that the road remain in the hands of the city, and has asked for a new plan to be presented in the coming month, according to Susan Stetzer, the district leader in the local council 3.
"Very little space has remained owned by the city and we'd want Extra Place to continue to belong to the city," she said.
"There could be offers on how to repair and operate the road, which could be done by Avalon, but we also want public access to remain guaranteed."
Avalon Bay announced a few months ago to repave the small street and set up seats designed, among other things, for a cafe to which the company wants to rent out space.
The idea that Avalon Bay could buy the road was made public for the first time during this month's session.
Michele de Milly, a spokeswoman for Avalon Bay, said the company wanted to use the road but didn't necessarily want to own it.
"The Avalon Bay's primary concern is to renovate this derelict alley and make it a public meeting place," she said, adding that the city itself had proposed the sale.
Seth Donlin, spokesman for the Department of Building Protection, said it had actually been the city's desire to sell the street and emphasized that the department's task was to develop rather than manage the real estate.
Compared with its colorful neighborhood The Bowery, Extra Place has retained some of its historical impression.
Some believe that the street got its name in 1802, when a large farm that belonged to Philip Minthorne was divided between his numerous children and a narrow strip of land was not claimed by anyone.
The back door of the CBGB music club, the punk rock monument that was closed in 2006, opened to Extra Place and the street is certainly most known as a backdrop for the photo on the cover of Ramones "Rocket to Russia".
The patch was great, said Danny Fields, the Ramones manager, who had taken the photo in November 1976.
The road was full of junk, rags and parts of barrels, posters, leaves and ropes.
Some residents, including bloggers, who write about the gentrification of the East Village, have rejected the idea of selling Extra Place, arguing that this public property, as modest as it is, should continue to remain public.
Others have praised Extra Place as a rarity – a virtually unchanged splinter of the city that should be left alone.
Jeremiah Moss, who runs the blog called "Jeremiah's Disappearing New York" (subtitled "The Book of Complaints: A Bitter Nostalgic View of the Extinguiding City"), has published part of the conversation of the city's history about Extra Place, which was published in The New Yorker magazine in 1952.
"Extra Place is a small narrow impasse that even stays dark during the day and is characterized by rusted metal doors of the warehouses, locked windows and weeks-old newspapers flying past rain gutters".
With a few exceptions, the road looked just as recently on an evening.
A rat ran through the back of the street, not far from the door that used to led to the CBGB, where a more expensive clothing store is located today.
Graffiti could be seen on the walls and an empty beer bottle lay on the floor not far from a fresh bundle of discarded newspapers.
Some passers-by said they had rarely thought about Extra Place, but suggested that the road could be polished.
Fields, added that in his opinion, some people underestimate the allure of the road in its current state.
The best years of my life, he said, were in the places that were dark, humid and disgusting.
Bilinguangual Education Debate: Education in English-Speaking Environment
In this issue of "Education Watch," Bruce Fuller and Lance T. Izumi discuss candidates' positions on bilingual training.
Lance T. Izumi, senior research assistant in California studies and senior director in the field of educational sciences at the Pacific Research Institute for Public Policy, is a co-author of the book "Not as good as one thinks: why the middle class needs a school selection."
Appealinging to Hispanic voters is a complicated task.
Barrack Obama's proposals on education are a typical example of this.
Obama's election campaign mentions that "the odds among African-American and Hispanic students successfully graduating the school is lower than with white pupils" - which is true.
To close this performance gap, Obama's education plan specifically recommends, among other things, a bilingual transitional phase in education for those who first learn English.
Nevertheless, the question for Obama is whether his commitment to bilingual education, which puts the emphasis on teaching in languages other than English, contradicts his interest in reducing the gap in school benefits.
Let's take as an example "Sixth Street Prep," an elementary school in eastern Los Angeles.
The majority of pupils are Hispanic and come from low-income families.
More than a third of the students, many of whom have only been in the country recently, are learning English.
Nevertheless, in 2008, an astonishing 100 percent fourth-grade students passed the state examination in math at a good level.
Surprisingly, 93 percent of children from the fourth school year were rated as good in the English exam.
This astonishing success has been achieved by other methods than those favored by Obama.
Sixth Street's school puts its emphasis on scrutiny and practice, on constant control of knowledge, and an attitude that doesn't tolerate exceptions.
Furthermore, the school pursues the approach according to which the training proceeds in an English-speaking environment. Exactly this is the poit that Obama should pay attention to, according to the headmistress Linda Mikels.
This approach attaches importance to the almost exclusive use of the English language in specialist lessons.
Ms. Mikels, who rejects the idea of bilingual education, told me the school would have a remarkable success with a student who had only just arrived from Mexico. 12 months later you'd be able to enter the classroom and no longer recognize it.
"It works," she noted, "it works for us."
Would Obama take a school like the "Sixth Street Prep" as a model for other schools with a large proportion of Latin American and other English-learning students?
The school's achievements would make the answer to this question easy, but the education policy positions within his own party (the national educational associations have long supported the idea of bilingual training) and his own consistent support of bilingual training make predictions about Obama's answer uncertain.
While he agrees that immigrants should learn English, he recently trivialized the issue by noting that people should stop worrying about the legislation targeting "only English."
Instead, he said: "You are to make sure that your child can speak Spanish".
If Obama actually wants to close the performance gap, he's meant to carefully consider educational models that work rather than despise and trivialize it.
The price of oil falls as a result of renewed fears about the economy
The price of petroleum fell sharply on Monday due to fears that even the American bailout plan for financial markets might not be enough to revive the economy.
Crude oil futures on the New York trading exchange fell 7 percent to $99.80 a barrel in the morning session.
Since last Monday, the price has dropped by more than $20.
In the past two weeks, goods markets have been shaken both by financial turbulences on Wall Street and by hefty whirlwinds in the Gulf of Mexico.
After prices reached their peak of $145.29 per barrel in July, they have fallen to almost $90 a barrel this month to then rise again, as a result of enormous uncertainty in the financial markets.
On Monday, investors have already expressed concern again, although congressional representatives had announced they had agreed over the weekend on a rescue plan that was supposed to be the largest in history.
This news was overshadowed by new worries that the financial crisis was still far from complete.
In the last chapter of the growing crisis for the time being, according to a government statement on Monday, Citigroum is supposed to buy Wachovia Corporation's banking operations.
In the meantime, Belgian, Dutch and Luxembourg governments have partially nationalized the European financial group Fortis.
According to analysts at Barclays Capital, despite the tense negotiations that took place over the weekend and led to a rescue plan, it has apparently failed to revive sentiment in the markets.
As the economic situation deteriorates further, it can be expected that demand for goods, including oil, will continue to decline.
Analysts at Deutsche Bank have written to investors that the forecasts for the global capital markets, interest rates and exchange rates have become extremely uncertain.
We believe they continue to write that the goods markets will also be affected.
“From the perspective of goods markets, we are most concerned about whether the U.S. virus is spreading globally, especially to China.”
The bank's analysts have revised down their expectations for oil consumption due to weak economic growth for the coming year, lowering their forecasts for the price of oil and gas by 20%.
Team record set by series in the second quarter
Centre-back Shaun Ellis thought he'd seen it all after running up more often than anyone in the Jets jersey over the past eight-and-a-half years.
Then on Sunday he saw the second quarter in which the Jets turned an inconclusive game into a triumph.
"I've never seen anything like like it," Ellis said.
After a sloppy game between the Jets and the Arizona Cardinals that matched bad weather, the second quarter began.
However, when quarterback Brett Favre's pass arrived at Lavernue's Coles for a 12-yard touchdown, that marked the perfect prelude to an afternoon where her developing collaboration took a giant step forward.
Favre found Coles in this quarter for two more touchdowns, one slipped as a full-back and the other 10 seconds before the end of half-time.
"I didn't make any particular effort," Coles says of the passports that went to Coles.
"He's a super player.
“Our vote was right, no matter what people think.”
While Favre and Cole's were playing catch, the defense took quarterback Kurt Warner apart.
Defender Darrelle Revis intercepted an accident-ridden throw and turned it into a 32-yard touchdown.
Libero Erik Smith got another pass and intercepted it.
Linebacker David Bowens then made for a loss from Warner.
The last ball loss, forced at Bowens, was probably the craziest.
The Cardinal had captured the ball with 10 seconds remaining in the game when the Jets recaptured the ball and Jay Feely converted a 20-yard shot to the 34 to 0 lead.
These were the most points the Jets had ever scored in a quarter.
When he was asked to describe the madness Erik Mangini, the coach of the Jets "The second quarter or the third quarter?"
To the answers both, because the Jets allowed 21 points in the third quarter without scoring a single one, Mangini said: "Believe me, I enjoyed the second quarter more than the third.
A lot of points have been scored in a short period of time."
Rested Gebrselassie beats his world record
Haile Gebrselassie from Ethiopia has once again shown why some consider him to be the best long-distance runner of all time after he broke his own record in the Berlin marathon on Sunday and has become the first person to make the track of 42.2 kilometers in less than 2 hours and 4 minutes.
With the victory on the flat course in 2:03:59, Gebrselassie improved his previous result of 2:04:26 by 27 seconds, which he had achieved last year in Berlin.
Gebrselassie, 35, has thus set his 26th world record in his multifaceted career, in which he has at least achieved a world championship title or set a world record on every track of one kilometre until the marathon.
Last month, Gebrselassie skipped the Olympic marathon in Beijing, out of fear that in hindsight perhaps exaggerated that air pollution could exacerbate his asthma problems.
Instead, he signed up for the competition in Berlin.
In almost ideal conditions - at sun and 9 degrees at the start and 13 degrees at the finish - Gebrselassie has won with an average speed of 4 minutes and 43 seconds per mile.
Since track runners such as Gebrselassie and Paul Tergat have switched from Kenya on long-distances, the record time for Marathon in the last 10 years has fallen by more than 2 minutes since Ronaldo da Costa from Brazil won 2:06:05 in Berlin in 1998.
After his victory on Sunday, Gebrselassie told the press that he had cramples in his calf two weeks ago and was therefore unable to train for a week.
Clearly he is refreshed after pause and did not come back rusty.
"I'm so happy," he told reporters.
“Everything went perfectly.
I resumed training a week ago and still had some concerns today, but in the end it’s great.”
He has been criticised - particularly in Kenya, Ethiopia's rivals from East Africa - for orchestrating his races excessively, avoiding many world's best marathon stars in general, relying too much on the race leaders, as if that were a track race, and placing world records above the competition.
However, this criticism hardly harms Gebrselassie's greatness as a long-distance runner.
Accompanied by four Kenyans on Sunday, Gebrselassie had reached the center of the track in 1:02:04.
Two elite runners from Kenya, James Kwambai and Charles Kamathi, have continued to challenge Gebrselassie after the pacemakers slackened.
A little more than three miles before the finish, Gebrselassie made his last effort to separate from Kwambai, who finished second in 2:05:36 and thus improved his best time by almost 5 minutes.
Kamathi was third in 2:07:48.
For women, Irina Mikitenko, originally from Kazakhstan and now German, has completed a considerable year by showing the seventh fastest result of all time with 2:19:19.
The world record of 2:15:25 holds Paul Radcliff of Great Britain.
Last spring, 36-year-old Mikitenko had surprisingly won the London marathon in 2:24:14.
She has missed the games in Beijing because of problems that have been described as a back injury. However, she has recovered to win in Berlin and improve her best time in her third marathon by five minutes.
She cleverly planned her run by allowing a quartet of Ethiopian and Kenyan runners to tear out, but overtaken after 30 kilometers.
Askale Tafa Magarsa of Ethiopia was second in 2:21:31, while Helena Kiprop of Kenya came third in 2:25:01.
Pride and rigors in the life of the black population, in a city of a very special kind
Hided between the excesses of amusement parks around Orlando, a few miles from the glittering, noisy and gigantic hustle and bustle, lies a quiet city where the pride and complications of Afro-American culture come to light.
Eatonville, the country's first all-black city and the hometown of Zora Neale Hurston, is no longer as easy to describe as Hurston did in 1935: "the city with 5 lakes, three crocket pitches, 300 brown skins, 300 good swimmers, more than enough guavas, 2 schools and no prison."
These days it's a place of pilgrimage.
Alice Walker, Toni Morrison and Ruby Dee have come to the annual Zora! Festival in Eatonville to commemorate the most famous author of the Harlem Renaissance.
And yet the city it describes is and remains – and which made it a tourist excursion destination by incorporating it into the Florida travel guide, which was produced at the times of the depression by the federal writer project – in many respects a place of very special variety.
Eatonville is as independent, dignified and private as in the 1930s, when Hurston wrote that rural black communities in Florida often refused to share their true thoughts with the white man who "knows so little about us that he doesn't know what's going away."
Even these days, in a year in which a black presidential candidacy, Senator Barack Obama, calls for an open conversation about racial relations, many here continue to be tired of being watched by outsiders.
"We are very careful about how our story is told," said Hortense Jones, 59, a resident of the city and a parishioner of the city's oldest church.
It has to be true.”
Eatonville has long been defined as a paradox of triumph and struggle.
It's both a historical example of the black movement and a community of nearly 2,400 residents where the poverty rate is twice the national average.
It's, on the one hand, a stronghold of literature and, on the other hand, it's an example of - sometimes convicted, sometimes vaunted - black culture of the rural South that has its roots in American slavery.
Unsurprisingly, residents here are both pride and on guard.
And that’s the concerns about Eatonville’s image actually started with Zora, as everyone calls Hurston here.
She introduced to the world her hometown in books that came from the heart and were written in dialect, such as "Mules and Men" (1953) and "Their Eyes were watching God" (1973)
Five heels in the Florida tour guide changed the city, which is very close to Route 77, a road that runs through the often-forgotten center of Florida, to the scenes of black history and human drama.
Naughty and Oskar both in real life and literature, Hurston led readers to the store of Eatonville's first mayor, Joe Clarke, and then turned to more private matters.
"Away from the road to the left," she wrote, "is the brown-and-white painted modern school, with it's well-kept gardens and playgrounds that Howard Miller always takes care of, even though he can barely read and write."
She also mentioned the new husband of widow Dash and wrote that Lee Glenn "sells drinks of any kind of any kind, and whatever-sets-up-to-hour rooms."
In just a few hundred words, Hurston associated Eatonville with self-government, but also illiteracy, second marriage and sex.
This was certainly not a travel guide.
In fact, this wasn't a portrait cherished by everyone.
"Zora described things as they were," according to 90-year-old Ella Dinkins, one of Johnson's girls immortalizing Hurston by quoting the men who sang songs about their beauty that were full of sexual allusions.
She added: "Some people didn't like this."
One still remembers Hurtson here as a lively eccentric who regularly returned after her family moved to Jacksonville, Florida.
Augustus Franklin, 77, recalled that when Hurston came into the city, she mostly did so without warning, in a noisy Chevrolet, smoking and clothed with pants, in a city that imagines itself somewhat on its venerable style of clothing today.
According to Mr. Franklin, most residents were fascinated, while many despised them.
"People were always happy to see Zora," Mr Franklin said.
But "she never stayed too long," he added as he rocked in his chair, on a rear-facing terrace looking at Lake Sabelia, where Hurston was in all likelihood baptized.
When Hurston died in 1960 she was poor and her books out of print.
Together with most of the world, Eatonville seemed to have forgotten them.
Although she was once a famous author, a contemporarie Langstin Hughes and the only black woman at Barnard College in the 1920s, she was buried in an anonymous tomb at Fort Pierce, Florida.
There was no great commemoration in Eatonville, no large-scale public readings.
"I don't think they've understood their contribution in the world or their legacy in the least," according to Valerie Boyd, author of "Wrapped in Rainbows," a biography about Hurston that was published in 2003.
The turnaround came in the 80s.
Orange County authorities wanted to put a five-lane highway through the city to replace Kennedy boulevard, the community's slow two-lane main street.
Orlando's suburbs had already pushed Highway 4 through the western outskirts.
The proposal was put forward when Eatonville was still recovering from a difficult period in its history.
Forced integration, among others, had put an end to the relatively idyllic isolation of the community.
In the 1950s, the fight against racial mixing brought hatred to the community.
"In that time, a bunch of white boys often came and they threw oranges and stuff at the off-sitting people," Mr Franklin said.
“Once upon a time there was a woman who was killed.
They threw a watermelon out of the car.”
In 1955, Hurston questioned the Supreme Court's request in a letter to the Orlando Sentinel's request to enforce integration by describing its decision in the Brown case against the school board as "insulting rather than respecting my race."
Inhabitants today say that although the abolition of racial segregation has been positive in some respects, it weakened group cohesion in Eatonville and self-confidence of its young people.
"Black children were used to being embraced - I remember it - one hugged his teacher in the morning and you hugged his teacher in the evening," according to N. Y. Nathiri , daughter of Ella Dinkins and executive director of the non-protecting group to protect the Eatonville community.
This could be maintained for so long, she added, until teachers and pupils no longer came from the same place.
"You didn't hug your white teacher, because he was a white teacher - I mean there's a cultural divide there," Ms Nathiri said.
However, civil rights have helped create space for further Zora Neale Hurstons - black writers, actors and artists who defied prejudice, just as she did, with positive self-confidence and statements such as: "How can anyone deny the joy of my society!
"I don't get it."
In 1975, writer Alice Walker made her way to Hurston's nameless tomb and began fighting for the revival of her fame.
Five years later, a much-praised Hurston biography of Robert E. Hemenway could be found on the bookshelves, once again making it part of the American literary canon.
The long-distance project came at exactly a time when Eatonville's most famous daughter was once again in the light of publicity.
And this time, Hurston's old neighbors saw her as a savior.
The community began planning a Hurston festival in 1988 to show what the district could destroy with the construction of the highway.
Thousands of fans came to the opening event two years later, and every January many return for the celebration.
A few years later, the district turned away from its road construction plans.
"The plans of a five-lane long-distance road revived the city, accelerated the whole thing at top speed, awakened genuine civic pride," Ms. Nathiri said.
Ms. Boyd put it a little more simply: "Zora saved Eatonville."
The victory against the long-distance road project has contributed to a change in the city's self-image.
There were more and more travelers, like Rachelle Munson, a lawyer who has been going to church here since 1993, and locals began to re-estimate the past.
Eatonville was added to the statewide historical register in 1998.
A new one-storey library (named after Hurston, of course) opened in 2006 on the newly paved and embellished Kennedy Boulevard.
These days, Eatonville is still a peculiarity in Florida: just 6 miles from downtown Orlando, it can sometimes feell like a back alley in the summer rain, as small as it was founded by only 27 black families in the 1880s.
(It’s 90% black these days.)
Outsiders in search of Eatonville's story and its meaning are often still treated with caution.
For most interviews, a permission in advance is necessary and certain things - such as the wall-painting of Eatonville's oldest church painted by a white and depicting black men during field work - must not be photographed.
Many in Eatonville, like Ms. Jones, a courageous, self-confident teacher with a penchant for dark red, still fear her shielded community will be misunderstood.
And yet, at the same time the Hurston festival was getting bigger, an increased degree of home pride was also born.
Young people, in particular, tend to see Eatonville as Hurston saw their entire breed: beautiful, with all its problems, neither worse nor better, and just as proud, creative and diligent, silly and mixed as any other racial and ethnic group in America.
It's the sincere love for this city that you can hear in the voice of Mr Franklin's nephew, 18-year-old Edwin Harvey, who's planning to return to Eatonville after college to work in the city administration or with the police who, according to him, could use some help.
And even the younger ones, like Alondra and Alexia Kenon, eleven-year-old twins from Winter Park, have learned to describe Eatonville correctly.
"Most people who only drive through here will think, "Oh, this city isn't anything compared to all the others," Alondra Kenon said the other day on a Sunday after church.
"But if you actually stop and look at its history for a moment, it's a very beautiful city."
Here with us in New York they speak fluent financial latin.
At first glance, nothing has changed.
If tourists in Manhattan stayed away from the newstalks and the massive television screens in Times Square, they wouldn't even realize that they're in the middle of a debacle.
You'd never believe that above their heads, in the shoebox-like offices with the glass facades, bankers and hedge fund administrators who have the monopoly in New York, have the worst day of horror every day since the 1929 stock market collapse.
The suppliers are still pushing along sidewalks with loud rattlers the metal tea wagons and carrying trays loaded with muffins for the breakfast sessions.
Latinos were still shuffling between apartments, like human packing animals loaded with around 50 ironed T-shirts hanging on a yoke-like pole lying on their shoulders.
Office workers are still waiting on food stalls on the sidewalks for their breakfast bagel and it's still impossible to get a yellow taxi on a weekday between 4 p.m. and 7 p.m.
It didn't feel like a crisis at all until I decided Thursday afternoon due to all the doomsday quirk to open a new austerity account.
Although I had decided to put my obligatory course on the quiet late afternoon in my JP Morgan Chase tribal branch, there were already about 20 people queuing.
Not at the money counters to withdraw or deposit money, but at the customer service desk where regular customers order cheques, ask tourists for the way to the department store Macy's and where new customers open accounts.
Most before me, I later learned from Starr, my "personal bank employee with a license," queued for the same reason as me.
Contrary to me, however, they had already withdrawn their savings from smaller banks, in places from where they commuted, such as New Jersey, Pennsylvania and Staten Island.
They had withdrawn money from so-called thrifts (similaring a bit of a building society), cooperative banks and those local savings banks that have branches only in one state and say they treat their customers like family.
Last year, 15 of these banks have quietly gone bust.
In the past few days, Washington Mutual, a bank with $327 billion investments, went into bankruptcy administration and as last Friday New Yorkers drove home over the weekend, Wachovia, another bank that owns $440bn worth of savings, was in search of a saving buyer.
In the fear of another collapse, the office and store employees brought their cash before me to a bank that is seen as a safer, a bank responsible for the most rescue purchases on Wall Street.
Quite normal New Yorkers have appropriated flowing financial Latin in recent weeks.
A woman on the phone in my Chinese tribal supermarket made reference to Lehman Brothers with so much familiarity about Lehman Brothers, the busting bank and its emaciated-looking chief executive Dick Fuld as if she were blaspheming about a lazy colleague.
And Lyn, the 50-year-old uniformed concierge of my apartment block's commemorating from our usual entertainment topics about Brad and Angelina and my groceries.
"I now have all my savings in money market funds," she told me after my visit to the bank.
“I’ve changed, I’m not taking any more risks.”
Muslemian tomb desecrated during right-wing slip in Austria
Heinz-Christian Strache, a left-winger of Austria's right-wing Freedom Party, and Filip Dewinter, a member of Belgium's right-wing Pertei Vlaams Belang, are attending a demonstration in Antwerp on Thursday, January 17, 2008 to represent the "Cities against Islamization."
They carry signs in the style of traffic signs on which a mosque is pictured, crossed through with a red line.
The police accuse right-wing extremists of burial desecration in a Muslim cemetery in Austria, on the same weekend in which extreme right-wing parties record significant gains in the country's parliamentary election.
In the night from Friday to this morning, more than 90 graves were severely damaged in the cemetery in Traun near Linz in an, according to the police.
The perpetrators sprayed Jewish symbols such as the David Stern on some of the tombs, but criminal investigators consider this an attempt to disguise the motive of extremists driven by hatred of Muslim immigrants.
It's not clear if the incident is in any way related to the elections.
A spokesman for the local Islamic community said they were "deeply shaken" at the news of the burial desecration that took place as the religious month of Muslim Ramadan draws to a close.
At the same time, a round of conscience research is underway in Austria after the country slipped to the right in yesterday's parliamentary election.
The FPÖ and the Alliance for the Future of Austria, the two far-right parties that received almost 30% of the vote, campaigned for a strict anti-immigrator’s law in their election campaign and some of their election slogans were criticized by opponents as xenophobic.
Heinz-Christian Strache, chairman of the FPÖ, which won 18% of the votes, campaigned for a ban on headscarves and burqas and even expressed his aversion to food, which is generally associated with Islam.
At his last rally in Vienna, he spoke of a "European Brotherhood" to prevent the growth of Islam.
Both parties want to prohibit the construction of mosques and minarets with the argument that these are political symbols of a continuous Islamization of Austria and Europe.
Polluments and analyses carried out immediately after the election, which state that the extreme right are the most politically powerful bloc in the country, indicate that this new development is based on young voters who are worried about their future in the European Union.
Foreign travel curfew for Gary Glitter
Gary Glitter, the glam rock star who has been convicted of children's sexual abuse, was banned from travelling to France and Spain.
Judges imposed a six-month suspension on Glitter after informing the authorities who control him that he wanted to travel to Spain via France.
Police in Kent were granted last Thursday to impose a foreign travel curfew after she presented the judges with evidence of Glitter's criminal record for child sexual abuse.
Glitter, real name Paul Gadd, did not attend the hearing at Ashford Local Court in Kent, but was represented by Corker Binning.
A spokesman for the Court reported: "On Thursday, September 25, the Kent Police at Ashford Local Court applied for permission to prevent Mr Paul Glad's foreign trips to France and Spain until March 25.
Paul Gadd wasn’t present in person.”
As required according to the register for sex offenders, Glitter, 64, had informed the authorities that he wanted to travel abroad.
He must inform the authorities if he wants to travel abroad for more than three days.
He must also notify the police 7 days before his scheduled departure.
Failure to notify the authorities could result in a prison sentence of 5 years.
Police in Kent said they wouldn't discuss individual cases or Glitter's possible whereabouts in the country.
A spokeswoman for Kent's police unit said: "The police in Kent will do everything necessary to protect children in this country and abroad."
"In our view, it is easier to control someone who is located in the United Kingdom because we have a comprehensive system to effectively prevent any potential risks."
"It's top concern of the police in Kent to protect the residents of Kent and we are working closely together to oversee the behavior of registered sex offenders in the country to minimize any risk that could exist for the population.
Glitter returned to the UK last month after spending 27 months in a Vietnamese prison for abusing 2 girls aged 10 and 11.
When he was released, Glitter flew to Bangkok, where he was scheduled to take a flight to London.
But he didn't feel comfortable and refused to fly to London.
The Thai authorities didn't let him through passport control and he flewed back and forth to Hong Kong after some.
Chinese authorities also denied him entry and he flew back to Bangkok, where Thai police and border officials insisted he was returning to Britain.
Last but not least, he arrived at Terminal 3 in Heathrow on August 22 and stayed there while his lawyer David Corker went to Uxbridge Local Court in western London, as Scotland Yard had successfully asked for permission to demand that he register as a sex offender.
Glitter had previously been imprisoned in the United Kingdom for 4 months in 1999 for possessing images of child abuse.
According to the Interior Ministry, Interior Minister Jacqui Smith was reassured and satisfied that Glitter has been barred from travelling to France and Spain.
`I receive the master backstage to capture his character, Günter Brauer, photographer.
No heels, no ruffles, no costumes for performance.
The nature of the great flamenco dancers, who forego concentration and dedicate themselves to interpretation in order to get a relaxed appearance as a woman or as a man.
On the one hand artists
On the other hand personality
I look into private life, into those moments that are foreign to the audience, I know when I can find gesture typical of the personality, the depth of a stolen gaze, or the express strength of the hands that are worried because they aren't going to enter the stage.
In his project `Flamenco-people', the German photographer Günter Bauer presents varied theatrics of some productions on stage with singers, dancers and guitarists. From this side he was still little known for the artists.
With this proposal, the photographer creates a special iconography of flamenco representation on different levels.
The exhibition, which is part of the program of the month of photography of flamenco, reflects the international importance of flamenco, which is getting bigger from day to day by the day, the artist explains, making it clear that `Flamenco-people' can be `any human being, but if you look at the photos, the viewer realizes that there are no typical images of artists on stage.
Quite normal people on the portraits are artists who are able to convey passion, sadness, joy and thus move the audience.
In order to capture the personality of these artists, you don’t have to take photos on stage.
Their personality and character are in their hearts.
Which interested me was to create a personal atmosphere that would allow me to capture the characters of the greatest flamenco masters with the photo camera.
Bauer, inspired by the `Cante de las Minas' festival, embarked on a long journey in 1997 to photograph singers, guitarists or dancers using a digital camera.
Thanks to the image sorting function of the camera and the existing material, images were created that are reminiscent of classical photography.
The application of various stylistic means destroys the classical basis of the image, which gives the image the modernity and the idea, as the farmer flamenco understands: `music with traditional roots that changes and surprises every day′′′.
These are not only the dance, the singing or the instrumental playing itself, but the personality of the artist, his character, who seduces the audience with his art.
The search for the essential, for a certain something that becomes visible in the form of hearing, being, is and has been the fixed goal of this work, which will be presented in the gallery of El Fotómata in the near future.
Well-knowned personalities in Flamenco among them Diego El Cigala, Chano Lobato, Cristina Hoyos, Enrique Morente, El Lebrijano, Antonio Canales, Vicente Amigo and others are among the narrow selection of the nearly seventy artists who have been photographed.
The project became an anthology of flamencos and will soon appear as a book, with all portraits from the exhibition.
Bauer’s passion for the portraits led him to take up this idea again two years ago and to realize it as `Portrait of the Veterans of the Sport (`SportARTproject').
Although the concepts of `Flamenco-people' project are different, the photographer admits to `I really like photographing people, getting to knowing them and putting me in their state of life, whether for a short moment or a longer time.
I think that in the field of photography the portrait is the most difficult genre.
You meet or come across people, personalities you sometimes don't know at all, or they leave you alone for a couple of minutes to take a photo.
Apart from these circumstances, as a photographer, I always try to realize my ideas.”
The collection of today's portraits, titled `Flamenco-people', is exhibited in the gallery des El Fotómata, which is located in the street called la calle de la Mata and runs parallel to la Alameda de Hércules. You can visit the exhibition until October 16.
Vargas Llosa confesses that the Roma, whom he is currently writing, is `a rabidity'.
He does not yet know where the plot of the novel takes place and what it is all about, but Mario Vargas Llosa persistently tries to advance the project he is currently working on, a novel with the protagonist Roger Casemant, a nationalist from Ireland who lived for twenty years in Congo and moved to the Amazon.
Yesterday, during his presence at the Hay Festival Segovia festival, the preferred Nobel laureate admitted that the project he deals with is `a rottaryness' and `everything indicates that he wouldn’t have to write the novel’.
`If I were prudent, I wouldn't have done it, but I'm going to do it and hope to finish the novel at some point,' so he concluded his participation in the festival, where the ideas, thoughts and words were exchanged. Within the four days he has seen hundreds of writers, historians, journalists and thousands of people who were in Segovia.
The author of `La ciudad y los perros' was one of the most outstanding personalities at the third festival and made predictions.
Vargas Llosa, who has won premium of the Hay Festival and to which most of those present gave her voice, is compared to the old edition of `Our mutual friend' and can be seen in the Juan Bravo theatre.
He was introduced by Félix Romeo and found that he had an audience in front of him, who realized that the Peruvian, who has a wonderful memory and great sense of humour, was not given enough time to be able to go through everything again: how his vocation to literature arose, which arises from 'extraordinary adventural lust', from `joy of reading' and from `more pleasure of reading' and from `More
The Hispanic novelist acknowledged that there is ‘no way ‘no way’ that one can measure and how to know whether books change life, ‘but I refuse to believe that literature is pure entertainment, a fleeting experience,’ Vargas Llosa emphasized, who claims that she “evokes effects” and `to brings people even more apart’, that’s why I asked the writers.
At the festival in Pozoblanco, the weather did not play along: bullfighting festivals were partially cancelled.
After the announced bullfighters determined that the floor of the pitch was in poor condition, the bullfighting festival, with which the festival in Pozoblanco was to be completed, was moved to yesterday morning.
Thus, with the consent of Fermín Bohórquez, Pablo Hermoso de Mendoza and Leonardo Hernández, the organizer has decided to postpone the bullfighting festival.
Thus, the festival was subjected to unsettled weather, which is why two out of three celebrations could not take place.
On Saturday it rained, which is why the second planned bullfighting festival could not be celebrated.
The matadores consisted of José Luis Moreno, Curro Días and Juan Bautista, who gradually entered the bullring.
So there was only a celebration on Friday.
The winner there was Daniel Luque, who cut three ears and triumphed after which he had left the passage with pleasure and aesthetics.
On the same evening Alejandro Talavante met two horns and an ear of the Taurus, which he had succeeded more by galloping than by his bullfighting.
And even worse was El July, who passed through the Arena de Los Llanos without difficulty, but also without fame.
After fighting so badly, much of the audience reacted with boo-cries at his second fight.
In Álava, two persons wanted to escape the control of their chariot loaded with two thousand kilos of hashish.
Morning: five minutes before ten.
Traffic controls in Ertzaintza, in the urban area of Armiñon (Álava).
Two police officers urged the drivers of the car to stop, whereupon they took flight.
It looked like they wouldn't have to stop with the load of more than two thousand kilos of hashish.
The police tracked them and arrested one of the drivers, the man, who is 28 years old, known as A. A. J., as announced by the Interior Ministry of the Basque state.
The detained person was accused of drug trafficking and forging official documents.
The incident took place when officers from the Álava region's traffic police had already completed traffic control on the N-1 and drove pass through Armiñon's urban area.
They decided to stop a car to carry out the route control. The driver did not follow the instructions, suddenly increased the speed and tried to escape control.
The officers began the chase until the car stopped after a few kilometers on the edge strip. At that moment, the drivers got out of the car to flee via a country road into a wooded area.
The police of the city of Ertzaintza finally caught up with one of them. In the car, an Iveco Daily, the drug traffickers transported 2,178 kilos of hashish, which was seized by the police.
The front part of the car was loaded with hashish bundles, which were later routed to further police departments for accurate laboratory analysis and weighing the bundles.
In addition, the officials found that the license plate was `double', so that it did not comply with the license plate specified in the motor vehicle papers, which is why there was also an arrest for forgery of official documents.
Ertzaintza police are continuing the investigation to make the arrest of the second suspect, who, as already mentioned, managed to escape, but also to determine the origin of the seized drugs.
The 28-year-old A. A.J., was taken to a police station for questioning. He will be presented to the magistrate in the next few hours.
Actress Heather Locklear has been arrested for driving under the action of an unknown drug.
Actress Heather Locklear, who is known by the role of Amanda from the "Melrose Place" series, was arrested this weekend in Santa Barbara, California for driving under the effect of an unidentified drug.
A witness had watched as she tried in a rather strange way from her parking space in Montecito, spokesperson for the traffic police of California told the magazine `People'.
The witness recounted in detail that Locklear `after 16:30 p.m. pushed through the accelerator and made a lot of noise by trying to move her car over backwards out of the parking place, and as she drove backwards on her sunglasses several times.
Slightly later, the witness, who at the beginning the actress probably didn't realize Locklear stopped on a nearby road and got out of the car.
The witness was the person whot notified the emergency service because she was 'fear of locklears'.
When the police patrol arrived, they found the actress sitting in her car, which stood across the road, thereby blocking the traffic.
`She seemed to be disoriented,' that's why they took the policemen to a special drug and alcohol center to take a test.
According to a police spokesperson, the actress appeared cooperative, excessive alcohol consumption was ruled out from the first moment. `According to the first observations of the officers, we believe that Locklear was under the influence of an unknown drug," according to the spokesman.
Locklear was arrested on suspicion of driving under the influence of an indefinite substance that could not be determined and taken to a local pre-trial detention at around 7 p.m.
Two months ago, Locklaer was discharged after undergoing treatment from the clinic in Arizona, where she was treated for anxiety and depression.
Llamazares insists that the Andalusian Association of the United Left will become in charge of the Silva case.
Gaspar Llamazares, the VL's chief coordinator and lawmaker of the United Left, has assured today that the regional association for education will be the one who will make the decision in the case of Seville's former City Council for Youth and Sporting City Hall, Francisco Silva who resigned last week after being accused of doing business with family members.
`In such cases I am a great federalist, and the Andalusian organization will be the one that has to take responsibility for it," Llamazares pointed out what course of action the party must have against him, who until now sits as a city councillor on Seville's municipal council.
Nevertheless, the chairman of the VL defended the necessity of `transparency' in the possible investigations of the rule violations that Silva may have begun: `The VL always insisted that such cases receive the maximum transparency'.
With regard to this issue, he emphasized that education has 'a strict ethical code for all public offices'.
`Sangre de mayo'
Inspired by `Episodios Nacionales' by Benito Pérez Galdós, José Luis Carci in `Sangre de mayo′ represents the revised version of the Spaniards’ famous uprising against the French. This is an admirable movium in filmography, with about 15 million euros investment for the filming.
In this way, the Cineast has attempted `to represent a lot of personalities and social analysis', which he has always admired about the works of the famous novelist, explained today Carci during the presentation of the film with Quim Gutiérrez and Paula Echevarría at the beginning of a long directory of performers.
`Sangre de mayo' with a film length of more than two-and-a-half hours, will be presented in cinemas this Friday and will later be shown on the Telemadrid as a four-hour miniseries.
José Luis Garci, great fan of Galdós's literature, assures that thanks to this filming his `childhood dream' has come true. This he owes Esperanza Aguirre, which three years ago commissioned him by the autonomous community Madrid to shoot a commemorative film for the second celebration of the century on November 2 in Madrid. Mainly because the story was based on 'Episodios Nacionales'.
The total contribution of 15 million, made for filming by the autonomous community of Madrid, Garci offers, as he added, the ‘one-off possibility’ with the help of decorations and corresponding clothing the image of ‘confused Spain, which chose the false leader for the struggle, because the people had been left behind by the struggle for independence and under the dominion of Ferdinand of the VII”.
However, the Cineast regrets that you couldn't count on the financing as with 'Alatriste' or `Los fantasmas de Goya' (twice as high as with `Sangre de mayo'), so some scenes from the script with Horacio Valcárcel, with whom Garci worked almost his entire career, could not be shot.
For this reason, Garci, who was also film director in `Volver a empezzar', has contributed through his ordinary way of working: he put himself on the stage to the actors, with whom you must first rehearse before you start shooting and for those who hadn't made a casting, he appeared `humiliating' and giving respect, `part of the profession of the film director is to provide help in the cinema.
Many performers he encounters `Sangre de mayo' have to repeat in front of his camera, as in the case of Miguel Rellán, Carlos Larrañaga or Fernando Guillén Cuervo.
Garci, an Oscar candidate in four nominations (one of which he has won), is of the opinion and assured that none of the three contestants representing Spain in front of the Academy of Hollywood this year represents a young generation of Spanish filmography. Neither `Sangre de mayo', nor `Siete mesas de billar francés' and finally also not the selected film `Los girasoles ciegos.
Garci doesn't expect awards for this film.
`In the beginning, you think you're working for offspring, later you realize that it's only for flourishing, and now you're satisfied when you've produced another movie,' Garci explains after more than 30 years of professional activity.
Isabel Bayón recalls the remembrance of seville dancer Tórtola Valencia
A flamenco dancer from Sevelite worships a dancer, who is also from Seville.
Carmen Tórtola Valencia: her father was Katalane (Florenç Tórtola Ferrer) and mother Andalusian (Georgina Valencia Valenzuela), she was born in the Triana district and when she was three years old, her family moved to London.
She was a dancer and expert on oriental dances.
She had an exciting life, she was one of the first fighters for the emancipation of the woman, such as Isadora Ducan, Virginia Woolf or Sarah Bernhardt.
She was a Buddhist, vegetarian and campaigned for the abolition of the corset, which restricted the free female movement.
Isabel Bayón fascinated the life of this woman, who left its mark on the dance and aesthetics revolution in Europe in the time between wars.
`I wanted to work with Pepa Gamboa and she proposed this role to me, which seemed impossible to me."
`We're trying not to tell about her life, but to preserve her spirit,' said the flamenco dancer.
Their dance fundamentals helped me a lot in flamenco.
`The Dance of the butterfly' seemed to me, for example, like Guajira, or `the dance of the serpent' like tango.
Her dancing was sensual and I've been told that my dancing is also in this aesthetic realm," she asserts.
The staging expects two extraordinary collaborations: Miguel Poveda, who will sing again for Isabel at one of her performances, as he also did in `La puerta abierta'; and with flamenco master Matilde Coral, who exceptionally wanted to work together with one of one of her ex-pupils.
`Matilde plays Tórtola, a very mature personality.
I'm happy because she agreed to participate in my work and I'm very honored."
The staging also expects musical composition and the guitarists Jesús Torres and Paco Arriaga, with the singing of Miguel Ortega, el Pulga and Moi de Morón; with the percussion of El Pájaro, as well as the viola of Rafa el Viola and the collaboration with the actor Juanfra Júarez, he `takes on the role of the artist again and again.
In this work one also tells in a certain way about the life of this woman, who became known in places as far away as India, the United States or Russia and also throughout Europe, even though she normally lived in London.
"I wanted to recall her back, because I think she's one of women who have an enormous influence on a certain epoch and whose works no one today appreciates it even less," says Bayón.
The performance will take place today at 20:30 at the Lope de Vega theatre.
The publication `Diario de un poeta recién casado′ also includes triennium
The presentation of the first illustrated edition of `Diario de un poeta recién casado′ at Saint Stephen's Church (New York), in a place where Juan Ramón Jiménez married Zenobia in 1916, offers a corresponding atmosphere to carry out planned activities on behalf of the Andalusian Council for Culture, to celebrate the `Triennium of Zenobia and Juan Ramón.
Thus, `the Triennium of Zenobia and Juan Ramón' will be concluded with the new edition of this work, with the international congress, which takes place in New York, with the restoration of the house museum of Juan Ramón Jiménez-Zenobia and with the traveling exhibition `Juan Ramón Jiménez: `Aquel chopo de luz′, which was opened in May.
The Andalusian Council for Culture has also received eight hundred copies of `Diario de un poeta recién casado′, edited by Pedro Tabernero and illustrated by Pacobo Pérez-Enciso. The aim is to make the person and the work of the Andalusian world poet more well-known.
U.S. can't possibly imagine itself without Paul Newman
“Sometimes God creates perfect personalities.
Paul Newman was one of them.”
This sentence comes from actress Sally Field, who collaborated with him in 'Sensational Reporter', and from the then prevailing notion of U.S. cinema, which was abandoned, from the whole of America until Newman came and followed her.
Look what it cost her to give him the Oscar.
and when he withdrewed in 2007.
And he knew he was very sick.
Nevertheless, this was a big blow for everyone.
“What one sometimes feels is substantially more than an expression, and now I’ve lost a great friend,” Robert Redford said, crying, ‘my lives and my country were the greatest drolls of his live.’
No one was afraid not to ridicule or exaggerate.
As the ‘father’ of cinema and fatherland, he has died at a sad moment when there is a fear of terrorists and the market collapse, in which the depreciated self-esteem of Americans is on the hopeless quest for reasons to regain dignity.
If in this hard time without virtue only a few people you can admire remain, you don't necessarily have to be Luis Cernuda to love Paul Newman.
For everyone, he embodied a platonic ideal of beauty and truth.
He was the official enemy of Richard Nixon and suffered as well as various losses both gains: from the foundation dedicated to the fight against drugs and was founded in honor of his son from the first marriage, who died of overdose, to his own brand of homemade and organic products.
All of this is a gift for the country, in which it cost him a lot of effort to prevail against a deadly food culture.
A broad wave of amazement strikes house-high over Hollywood without regard for nationalities and nationality.
The last James Bond, the Englishman Daniel Craig, confirmed that not only one human has died, but the whole era.
Englishman Sam Mendes, film director and Oscar-winner, said working with Newman was no more or no less than the top of his career.
Russell Crowe, the nasty boy who came from Australia, dared to describe Newman as his greatest inspiration.
`The greatest of all of us has left us,' affirmed George Clooney, perhaps one of a few who dare to live how Paul Newman lived: maintaining high demands on righteousness, leading a responsible private life far from the cameras.
This isn't always easy or even possible in today's media jungle.
But there's someone trying, and Paul Newman was an example of that.
He divorced once and was married to Joanne Woodward for fifty years afterwards, to whom he was faithful, although there were certainly many opportunities not to be.
`What should I eat a hamburger on the street when I have fillet at home?' he often joked himself.
In heaven, he'll be proud that on earth his daughters have the same opinion as his fans, which isn't necessarily common in America.
“Of all the great roles he playeded, none he was so proud of those of the faithful spouse, the loving father, the attentive grandfather and those of the philanthropist,” they say.
For the Olympics, the streets in Madrid are deficient.
Yes, the dream of many of us is to host some Olympic disciplines in Madrid, after the failure for 2012, it may be 2016.
But I wondered, ‘Are we prepared for that?’
Yes, we're following the example of the old streets, I'm saying it isn't true!
She was described as a perfect road, and according to my assessment, she was the worst road with everything there was.
Of course, that's a lot of nonsense, if you'd pay attention to the assessment and I could check it, this clearly wouldn't have come through.
But let's go through everything from the very beginning.
The nonsense starts with picking up tickets, which allows you to go first.
It doesn’t seem bad to me either, but if they’re already letting you go, they’re supposed to give you everything (including chip to avoid traffic jams on the road).
Well, don't.
Here they only let you get the ticket (the first nonsense that's to go there only because of it) and (after an infinite queue) there's the surprise! The T-shirt was only in the size `S' (a second nonsense because I need the size XL)
Where do we actually live?
Or is it that after the crisis it was decided to buy only small, much cheaper shirts made of less fabric?
All right, things like this are a small thing compared to the days on the road.
Apart from the fact that you have to go very early to get the chip, there is already a queue of at least 15 minutes and, besides, you have to leave the backpack.
The wait lasted more than 35 minutes, which is incomprehensible in the case of a road where 11 000 people are expected.
Finally, as was to be expected, the road started and we hadn’t left our backpacks in front of it, even if it had once succeeded, but it would be more difficult.
In addition, it looked to wait for rain in these 40 minutes, so there was a temptation to organize a popular uprising and to raid the cottage.
I think tomorrow will certainly have some 500 people pneumonia and some like me won't even appear in time for the classification because of this chip I've gone, I can already imagine that the exit has been stampeded incorrectly.
Of course, if I were asked about a perfect road, this road would never occur to me.
This is how I think we'll be dreaming for a long time about a city that's well-prepared for the Olympics
Microsoft looking for a (virtual) receptionist
At the `Emerging Technology Conference', hosted by MIT, Microsoft unveiled the advancement of the new image recognition system and human-interaction, an entity of artificial intelligence without a lot of intelligence, which, however, is able to perform some primitive tasks, such as ordering a taxi (or in the case of Microsoft, this is a route planner to move on the company's campus).
Thanks to a camera and a form and face recognition system, this `receptionist' can see how many people are in the room, who they are and even remember how they were clothed, it can listen and understand the keywords of their entertainment.
It can also classify those present into the groups after the talks.
In the presentation video, `the receptionist' notices a third person, but she understands that this person does not belong to the group that is having conversations with them, and that neither is a company employee.
She asks if the person wants to visit someone here and replies that she's going to listen soon.
All without the blue screenshot
Impressive
This is certainly the future.
Now the company has better things to focus on, for example on the next version of its Windows operating system.
This week they spoke very superficially about Microsoft’s change of strategy.
Windows 7 will be faster and won’t have so many embedded programs.
Steve Ballmer noted that if the computers were equipped with the essentials, instead of filling them with the commercial garbage, they would work five times faster.
Everything is just a finding when you consider that Microsoft inflicted one or other function and `extras' in Windows versions.
The change of direction is worthwhile
Microsoft is right, the operating system is gradually losing importance, just like the software.
When Google Chrom appeared on the web, that was part of the theory that it was Google's first step towards competing with Windows.
Skilfully, many pointed out that the browser is not an operating system.
The operating system, as an indispensable part, will always be necessary for hardware and software to function simultaneously.
The operating system performs the function, which can be compared to the function of his wife or husband when it comes to telling his mother-in-law something.
It's true.
The system will never disappear, but you have to think of your ‘death’, not as an absolute, but as a relative truth.
What Ballmer said this week is confirmation of this idea.
People pay less attention to the operating system every time, but more to the web browser.
The computer is only started to run Firefox, Explorer or Chrome and every day we spend more time surfing than in the surroundings of the `windows'.
What's half in the computer of existing applications?
Less is more
And Windows 7 must be more compact, much more compact than Vista.
Easier, not so exaggerated, less complicated.
Microsoft's interest is more to integrate its online services, which can then eventually become the alternative to Google.
In reality, they need that, without a doubt, and I'm taking a little example of that.
I took the photo of this place in Redmond, in one of the recreation rooms for Microsoft employees at the beginning of the month.
A sign is attached to the soft drinks machine: `Your search. Our future'. It prints a request to use your own tool for online search.
In 2006, an independent study concluded that 66% of employees at Microspok, Google used it for research on the net.
By the way, last week it was also confirmed that the new version of the Windows Mobile 7 has been delayed.
It won't appear at the beginning, but at the end of 2009, rather even in 2010.
Microsoft is looking for the ability to update Windows 7 and Windows Mobile 7 simultaneously.
Every day the phone turns into a computer.
The Android and iPhon are already on the market, but the other delay will cost money.
Le Fabuleux Marcel
Pablo Picasso did it in Costa Azul, Leonardo di Caprio had this brand in 'Blood Diamond' by Edward Zwick, just like Al Pacino in `The Godfather' by Francis Ford Coppola. At the same time it belongs to clothing of a well-built pastor.
When we begin to enumerate the countless star performances, we can draw back to the image of a very powerful man, with a pointed beard, who had raised heavyweights in a travelling circus.
Basically, that's a typically masculine T-shirt made of cotton, sleeveless and fitted.
The English speakers call it `tank'. However, Lle Fabuleux Marcel de Bruxelles surpasses all other brands.
Firstly, because of its great name, even though it's called 'Marcel' in a narrow circle. The legend tells that the naming is associated with sports and comes from boxer Marcel Cerdan, the lover of singer Edith Piaf.
`La Marcel' towers over everyone because of its excellent cotton, because of its distinguishing unisex cut and is characterized by a small Belgian flag (barely recognizable), which these T-shirts have as a detail on the neckline.
And attention, the Belgian company is not only focused on the production of `tanks'.
They also have college sweatshirts, boxers retro, cardigans and cashmere, cotton trousers, t-shirts with stripes, babies, mini shorts
Modeled and comfortable underwear.
Although, it's a pity to use it as undeclorage.
They're very appealing.
"I want to be a young mother; I don't worry about the candidate"
Her big demeanour with her sister did not prevent her from developing her own personality.
Mónica Cruz is dainty, but full of joie de vivre.
At the time, the siblings Gruz take their new activity in fashion very seriously.
For Mónica, this means considerably more than for some pretty and rich girls who maintain their reputation.
She was in Paris at the promotion of the company Mango, where she relocates and also makes the presentation of the most important premiums in fashion that the Spanish fashion house sponsors.
Merciful and shy, winner on the short distances.
Tell us what you're doing in Paris.
I have come to present the new collection for this autumn/winter season designed by my sister Penélope and I for mango, as well as visiting the presentation of the second edition of `Mango Fashion Awards' and taking part in the dinner in Valentino's honors.
A very intense working time, which is exciting at the same time.
You are working for the Spanish house, what is the basis of your new collection for the fourth season for Mango?
My sister, both I am fascinated by the cinema of the 50s and 60s; there we find a lot of ideas.
I like to walk through a city and observe women how they are clothed, how they combine their garments, I often do that.
Dancer, actress and fashion designer, you're a very versatile woman, aren't you?
I think yes, but after all, everything meets in one and these areas have a lot of things in common.
Ever since I was little, I've been thrilled with the clothes; I've always wanted to go to my grandmother to rummage around my aunt's wardrobes and see her clothes.
That was great, almost better than going to an amusement park.
Even later, as a dancer, I always tried to pay attention to clothing.
You know, we dancers like having our own style in classes and rehearsals to look in the mirror and feel good.
As an actress, if you get the costume, you put yourself into the real actor role.
After all, everything is linked together, it's like this, a lot of respect for the costume designers and their professionalism.
Recently you turned 30, have you not fallen into a crisis?
Oh, something.
I'm feeling better than ever, as a thirty-year-old I feel good.
I think the woman is getting mature, has experience and can enjoy life with fewer mistakes.
I'm not afraid because of my biological clock.
I've done the things I've wanted to do since I've been little, and that's given me a lot of inner joy.
So, aren't you thinking about becoming a mother, aren't you?
For a long time, I have known to become a mother, because my body demands this, and I also want to be a young mother; I don’t want to miss it.
As for the candidate, I'm not worried about that.
I'm going to give my child great feelings of happiness and raise it with a lot of love.
As far as film art is concerned, you keep an eye on the works you participated in and that were shot last year.
That's exactly how it is and I very much want them to be presented to the public.
Me, as a woman who comes from theatre, am very impatient and restless and wants to have everything at once that, of course, cannot be immediately.
I very much wish that `La última hora' and `En busca de la tumba de Cristo' will be performed for the first time.
We'll see how they like you
A few days ago, you appeared on the run with Naomi Campbell, is that right?
That's all right, don't say that.
It's not like I went on the run to model.
Naomi Campbell, who I've known for some time, has simply called me and invited me to London to take part in a parade for her organisation's charity 'Women for a better world' and the clothes we are showing. I know that two garments from our collection have been auctioned for mango to raise the money for Naomi's NGO.
People don't know Naomi's good heart.
There's a lot of things being told about her. I, as the one she knows, would tell you they're wrong.
Even so, I'm going to support this good cause she's involved in.
She asked me to take part in the parade for this big-hearted thing, but if she comes and asks me to make a handstand, I'll do that too.
To support women in the Third World what is also needed.
The motorists, who can relapse because of the alcohol level, will have to install the `Alcolock'.
The authority orders that the driver, who had three official penalties because of the alcohol level, installs this system in his car, which forbids him to start the car in a drunken state. If he is alcoholic, then the car does not start.
The initiative was already announced last month in June and is already part of the report of the attorney general's office. According to their data, it is a pilot project, which will last for a year.
The purpose is the resocialization of the driver, which is why he gets a course for re-educating traffic behavior.
In addition to the Prosecutor General’s Office, according to experience, the Ministry of Transport and the Foundation of the Technical Institute for the Safety of Automobiles will also take part.
According to statistics, between 30-75% of motorists who have been reported more than once for driving under the influence of accounted for driving.
The `alcolock' would prevent this because it is a system connected to the car, it measures the alcohol content in the exhaled air and prevents autostart if the driver has taken alcohol test; or if an alcohol concentration is detected, which is higher than allowed.
It costs more than € 1 000, the costs will be borne by the one who imposes the sanction.
In this context, a study carried out in the U.S. in 2007 has showen that cars equipped with these devices prevent up to 65% of rule violations in the first year after its introduction.
In Spain, it is anticipated that it could save 115 lives every year.
The measure is confined to administrative sanctions for two reasons: to tackle this behaviour before it becomes a criminal offence.
And in order to facilitate their implementation, legislative reform can be implemented in the ordinary way, and nevertheless a reform of the Basic Law that is necessary for the law enforcement process is called for.
The device is not yet available in the Spanish markets, in any shop, although it has already been installed in some commercial vehicles and public transport vehicles.
Just tomorrow, the House of Representatives will vote because of the initiative of the Spanish Socialist Workers' Party, which proposes to install this system on school buses. This has already been done in France.
In Mexico and without 'peep-toes'
With British punctuality, at 17:40, at the time provided for by the program, next to the presidential hangars of Benito Juárez de México DF airport, landed the aircraft of the Spanish Air Force, with which Prince and Princess of Asturias were traveling.
The sun went down, but their rays have still forced to use their hands as a parasol to see the royal couple getting out of the plane.
Right on the gangway: the Spanish ambassador and other authorities
A few hours before that, I had asked a taxi driver on the road if he knew the prince and princess of Asturias were visiting the city to formalize the official trip.
Ah, very good, well, you can't take any people at the airport at this time she's from here or so
No, she's the Spaniard, but she's lived here for a few years.
-Oh so, very good
But the Mexican press knows exactly who the prince and princess of Asturias are and know the importance of the trip.
There are many who are waiting in the hangar of the airport and on the red carpet, also military protection of 27 soldiers of the navy, who give honorary salvo.
The prince appears smiling, clothed in navy blue, and she, the princess, with long hair blown by the wind, without ordinary `peep-toes', she may have been told that a thundry is expected in DF.
Before entering the hall, they smile and are prepared for the photographers, where the official entourage awaits.
At the door of the hotel where they will be housed, one of the four largest in the city, a reporter from Mexican television is waiting for them who likes to make jokes.
The best premium in fashion
Probably there was no more suitable place in the world than Paris to showcase a premium of fashion and there was probably no substantially outstanding fashion designer and bonvivant than Valentino.
Mango presents the second edition of the `el Bóton-Mango Fashion Adwards' fashion competition for the promotion and international promotion of young fashion talent.
This will be awardeed a cash prize in the amount of 300 000 euros.
Ayer Sandrina Fasoli, the Belgian winner of the first edition, yesterday saw her drawings in the shop window of the Mango branch on Boulevard Haussmann number 54 in Paris and almost cried because of the emotional excitement.
By the way, this is the company's second-largest store in Europe and was recently opened. It offers a sales area of 1 335 m2.
The largest store is located in London.
So many events have happened that have been associated with thanking fashion designer Valentino for his jury chairmanship in the competition. It all ended with a luxury dinner at Opera National de Paris.
In the surroundings of many famous people, the board table organized by Mango seemed spectacular.
Nati, with the heavy heart that was broken by the pain because his brother died recently, pulling himself together. He put on the suit from the fashion house and had some jewelry from ELAH, which were breathtaking. He held on to the arm of his son Luis, who is almost two meters tall and accompanied Valentino at the dinner, in his honor.
Andik, the owner and lord of the House of Mango, greeted the guests and was such a cavalier, so that he ended together with the Baron von Rotschild without jackets, because jackets they lented to the ladies.
Mónica Cruz, rushed to the shop and had a pair of trousers and a shirt, which she modeled herself, and for dinner she wore a long, black sexy dress. On a night walk through the opera, accompanied by Goya Toledo, they showed us a hall for ballet rehearsals, so she got into excitement, remembered the old days, grabbed a pole and made a kind `
Inés Sastre, didn't join the walk through the palace's premises, but before that she had shown us the photos of her son on the phone.
The next morning we could look forward to the presence of Oliver Martínez, a personality who always evokes a stormy enthusiasm, and Jon Travolta, who after his arrival (with his own bed varnishes and towels) at the Hotel Internacional in Paris, asked in advance to have a room without smells.
the stars beneath the starry skies of Paris.
That's how life is.
1001 advice against stress
There isn't 1000, but one more way to forget chaotic everyday life with fast-paced everyday lifes.
And even if there are few, everyone is collected in a book in which it says how to relax. The book arrives at the bookstores on September 30.
The alarm clock is a punishment when it rings at 7 a.m., but even worser seems the idea of getting back into the hectic rhythm that current life demands.
The office is called the tension and the computer leading to the back pain.
The stress is 21st-century evil.
But there aren't one rather than two methods to combat daily exhaustion and the problems of how to shut down from the world of work.
However, the number is increasing, no more and no less than 1001.
In addition to yoga positions, the classic massage or spa treatments, Susannah Marriott presents in the book the whole list of possibilities adapted to the tastes and time of each.
The work against stress means `1001 possibilities to relax' and so comes the tranquility that the book conveys into the bookstores of the publisher Pearson Alhambra and DK.
Anyone who thinks it is impossible to achieve tranquility in the areas such as the workplace with the help of personal contacts, this manual refutes such faith.
In addition, the methods are offered how to stay in contact with nature or suggested the 100% natural recipes that lead to relaxation.
If the adrenaline in your veins no longer rises to restore balance of the body and the stress has become a problem, the moment has come to sit down on the sofa, turn off the brain and focus on reading.
Talking in your sleep
Some children speak at night
I don't mean those who wake up and tell us something that happened the day before, but the children who speak during sleep.
If it happens to you for the first time, at best, you will be terrified, but in reality it has no meaning.
This is a positive sleep disorder called speaking during sleep.
It's quite common, especially among children and adolescents.
Certainly, more than one of you has been told that yough was speaking at night.
This phenomenon can occasionally occur (once in life, for example, such as during the high fever), or regularly (every day, once a week, once a month), the next day the child cannot remember anything.
There is no single reason that causes this phenomenon.
Often connections with other appearances in sleep are sought, such as nightmares, panic anxiety at night, enuresis noctura (bed wetting at night), speaking in sleep, but there is no reason for it.
As we mentioned earlier, it can be due to very high fibers, stress situations or overwhelm.
In any case, this is a little explored phenomenon.
The frequency with which it occurs is very variable.
It may be that one calmly speaks, whispers in individual words or it can be quite an opposite (cries, crying, long discussions).
In cases, if the utterance is very intense, it is recommended to observe the child and get advice from a specialist to check to see if there is a problem that causes anxiety and comes about in this way.
We've said it's a positive disorder, so it's not being treated, whether it's associated with another sleep disorder that can be treated (for example, with sleep apnea or with stress).
What you can do is perform a series of simple preventive measures:
Don't try to arouse the child before sleeping.
So, choose quiet play for the kid and avoid stressful television programs that are horrible and a little too wild.
In the nervous children, who are recommended relaxation, relaxation methods can be used to help them get a certain inner calm.
This helps the child to sleep soundly.
If speaking occurs during your sleep, try to keep calm.
Keep in mind that it is harmless to the child and the next day it won’t remember anything that happened.
Useful advice
Don't wake it up.
This is useless, because the child is unconscious, so you will tear it out of sleep.
Even though the child speaks, it sleeps, so it rests.
Remember, if it is frightened after a couple of appearances, it is recommended to seek advice or to find out what reason the child has for the fear.
If they understand what it says (which isn’t so common), the child doesn’t put under pressure to carry out an interrogation and get more information.
So you won't be able to achieve anything with it, because when the child sleeps, he is unconscious and can't think about it.
Besides, you’ll hear what it says, but you won’t know what it’s dreaming and why it’s saying, so be careful with the interpretation of the information and the conclusions you’re drawing.
Maybe, don't tell the kid that you'll hear this at night and know its secrets.
Not even as a joke.
This causes a lot of anxiety.
So don't worry, it's nothing bad, that's a moment that only lasts a few minutes.
Illhäuser ... finally
After a long period of thirst, Illhäuser is finally back on course for success on the pitch.
Without much effort, the yellow-blacks scored numerous goal chances and managed to implement one of them, and thus to win the game.
The tempo of this encounter, in which the attackers struggled to show their skills, was not very high.
After a first attack by guest captain Kauffmann, whose long-range shot narrowly missed the angle, Illhäuser was supposed to dominate the game with easily, and it managed to take turns challenging Denmark's defence with a long-range shot with a high-pass, and Romain with a shot with the foot to challenge Denmark's defence.
The opposing squad blocked in the middle of the field, leaving only the lively Riahi at the top of the storm.
The start of the second half should be decisive with first two goal chances won by Riahi and Temporale, and immediately afterwards with the first goal for the Illhäuser by Marais, who stood just right to take on a ball that had been fended off by Diebold in a first attempt.
But the Ill-American scorer should not be able to enjoy his luck for long and about twelve minutes later to be sent to the bench, whereby his teammates had to continue to play numerically inferior.
