Sarkozy meets angry fishermen
Before he flies to the United States, Nicolas Sarkozy will make another detour to Brittany.
In view of the resolute stance of the fishermen, who blocked the entrances to several Breton and Norman ports on Monday, the French president decided to arrive Tuesday morning in the port of Guilvinec in the Finistère to meet with their representatives.
The latter pledged to receive him `with a constructive spirit', and hope that he will come with `concrete proposals'.
The president's visit takes place at the same time as a general assembly of the crisis committee set up by the Breton fishermen to enforce measures in light of the extreme rise in the diesel price, which has reached an average of over €1.14 per liter.
According to Elysée Palace press secretary David Martinon `, the president will discuss possible solutions with them to help their companies to become competitive again and to secure workplace conditions and the payment of sailors'.
Sarkozy is awaiteed with determination by fishermen.
Tuesday morning, they spearheaded in the Morbihan and Calvados protest marches that caused traffic jams of nearly ten kilometres in length.
Quiberon's sailing port was also blocked, but links to Belle-île did not break off.
Quiberon peninsula was demarcated with a barrier.
Already on Monday, the sailors expressed their determination by burning tires and palettes in front of the fuel depots of the ports of Brest and Lorient from the early morning, while demonstrations were helded in Concarneau or Saint-Brieuc.
The movement extended to other ports of the Atlantic coast and the English Channel, such as Sables d`Olonnes, in Vendée, where the fishing coat did not go out, or Le Havre, where about twenty boats blocked the manoeuvres of the merchant ships.
The fishermen of Turballe and Croisic in Loire-Atlantique disbanded the blockade of the significant total refinery in Donges in the evening.
Those from Boulogne-sur-Mer decided to wait for Wednesday's meeting at the ministry before deciding on possible measures.
In fact, the crisis committee in Guilvinec will have to decide on the reactions to the movement and its participation in the discussion planned at the Ministry of Agriculture and Fisheries in Paris.
But some Breton fishermen don't want to be represented by those in charge of the national fisheries committee, which has not endorsed their movement.
`The strike will continue', because `the fishermen will not give in on any point' and `do not want to go to Paris," proclaimed Liliane Cariou, president of Lorient's Fisheries Committee.
The professional fishermen, who pay no taxes on diesel, currently costs about 50 cents, but they think that the profitability limit of a boat can only be reached with diesel for less than 30 cents per liter.
