Embraer implifies its delivery forecasts for 2008
The Brazilian aircraft manufacturer expects this year with 215 planes delivered, despite the looming recession, as a danger to the global economy.
Embraer, the world's fourth-largest aircraft maker, is halting its delivery forecasts for 2008 with 215 aircraft delivered, reiterated its president Fredrico Fleury Curado.
In the first half of the year, the aircraft manufacturer delivered 97 aircraft.
The Brazilian manufacturer expects much from the Chinese market, especially for its planes of the order of 30 to 120 seats.
To date, Embraer has delivered 41 machines to Chinese customers in eight years.
AIG would consider relinquishing more than 15% of its assets
According to Sunday's release from the Financial Times, the insurer American International Group, which was financially backed by the U.S. Monetary Authority, is considering handing over more than 15% of its assets in order to repay the $85 billion that has been stretched ahead.
AIG is prepared to target the sale of much of its businesses, beyond its life insurance on an international level and its retirement savings in the United States.
According to the FT, the board of directors of AIG met Sunday evening to review possible sales.
No confirmation could be achieved at the AIG.
Assets that AIG would potentially separate from include its aircraft leasing branch International Lease Financial Corp., its 59% stake in reinsurer Transatlantic Holdings, as well as its real estate assets and investments in private equity, the FT further explains.
No final decision has been taken regarding these sales, the FT continues.
AIG must quickly make the sales to repay the loans over $85 billion that the federal reserve has granted it, including at the risk that the American authorities will receive 80% of their capital and the current shareholders will be lost to the same extent.
Analysis Historical Paris of Session Historical Morning Meeting
The New York Stock Exchange on Monday, after the Chamber of Representatives rejected Paulson's plan to bain the banks, reported a historic fall in prices: The Dow Jones lost 6.98%, an unprecedented loss of nearly 800 points, and the Nasdag 9.14%.
The Dow Jones Industrial Average (DJIA) is down 777.68 points to 10,365.45 points.
As for the points, the strongest case in its history (684.81 points) had previously been recorded on September 17, 2001, day of trade decline following the terrorist attacks of September 11.
According to definitive firing figures, the Nasdag index fell by 199.61 points to 1,983.73, and the widely used Standard&Poors 500 index by 8.81% (106.85 points) to 1,106.55 points.
After the unexpected rejection of the plan to rescue the banks by the Chamber of Deputies, which should again allow some stability in the markets, Wall Street collapsed, which had already struck at the beginning of the quarter.
By 228 votes against and 205 in favour, MPs denied Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson the funds he had asked for.
Nearly two out of three Republicans rejected his plan.
"A bad picture is being circulated here that portrays Congress as incapable of adopting a plan," said Lindsey Piegza of FTN Financial.
`This shows how much the market counted on this plan,' she added, fearing that a project adopted in several days `too late' comes.
“If the project is indeed doomed, it will quickly be the task of the Fed (the U.S. central bank) and other central banks to get to grips on the crisis, which would confine credit conditions even more,” warned Brian Bethune, head of Global Insight.
And Miss Piegza emphasized, highlighting the feeling of utmost urgency even more, “that “every day that passes brings the ruin of a financial enterprise” in the United States.
After the bankruptcy of Washington Mutual on Friday, the American authorities planned the purchase of Wachovia's banking operations by its competitor Citigroup.
In Europe, the crisis suddenly accelerated, leading to emergency measures in favour of banks in the United Kingdom, Benelux countries, Iceland and Germany, an indication that the markets are facing 'not only an American crisis but a worldwide crisis', the analyst at FTN Financial added.
The market for basic consumer goods has grown considerably.
State treasury revenues within ten years have declined from 3.632% to 3.827% on Friday evening, and revenue within 30 years last night from 4.161% to 4.357%.
Obama, winner after points in the first televised duel
Friday's first debate by the two candidates for the White House was followed by another to find out to find out who emerged as the victor, John McCain or Barack Obama.
As expected, the QGs proclaimed their own victory in this television duel, which was to be persecuted by a third of American families.
Political analyst David Gergen estimates that John McCain's performance, though quite handsome, wasn't 'brilliant' enough to allow him to stop his decline in the electoral polls five weeks before the presidential election.
The Gallup Institute, of which an opinion poll was published on Saturday, according to Obama is ahead with five points (49% to 44% for McCain), is of the opinion that the Democrat clearly makes the better figure.
Among still undecided voters, i.e. those that matter now, Obama emerged with 39% as the winner from the polls, while according to a CBS poll, 25% of them see McCain as the winner.
Obama should be able to confirm his lead on Thursday with the good performance expected by his competitor Joseph Biden at first and only public debate with his rival Sarah Palin
“It would be a mistake to underestimate them.
She has good instincts, but warned Bill Clinton this weend.
Seventy-year-old McCain did not cease his undeniable experience in the field of foreign policy to the forefront, quite clearly underlining his opponent's 'naivety' with regard to these questions, which formed the focus of this first televised duel.
“Senator Obama doesn’t seem to understand that...,” he emphasized several times.
“I don’t think Senator Obama has the necessary experience and knowledge to become president.”
A point of attack that has been preached in Republican election commercials since yesterday.
Obama himself emphasized that he represents the future.
He expressed himself briefly and precisely, without being upset.
He brought to the fore his ability to make good decisions and renew the image the world has of the United States.
"McCain still hasn't understood, Obama, on the other hand, already," set out a Democratic election commercial this weekend, in which Republicans were accused of failing to have grasped the full extent of the crisis that weighs on the `American from the street'.
“During the 90 minutes of the debate, John McCain had a lot of things to say about me, but nothing about you,” Obama denounced yesterday in front of 20,000 people who had gathered in pouring rain on the occasion of an election event in North Carolina.
He didn't even mention the words `Mittelstands' or `Workers'. 'The financial crisis gives Obama, whom Americans think are most suitable to save the economy even on the verge of the abyss, a clear advantage.
`The coming week could be a crucial turning point,' influential Democratic Senator Charles Schumer estimated yesterday.
Democratic Representative Rahm Emanuel, a former member of the Clinton administration, compared Obama's "good economic instincts" with those of Bill Clinton during the 1998 financial crisis.
For the Democratic nominee, the current crisis is the closing verdict of an eight-year poor economic policy implemented by George Bush and backed by Senator McCain.
Having re-emphasized that he would probably even manage to lower taxes for 95% of Americans despite the fluctuations on Wall Street, the Illinois senator pointed out that the creation of a possible energy independence of the country and the promotion of renewable energies, along with the introduction of a health insurance system, were a top priority for him.
McCain, on the other hand, wanted to build a plant of 45 nuclear power plants by 2030.
The two candidates thoughtfully evaded the questions, which on the plan to save the financial market over $700 billion (€470 billion), which was passed by Congress last night and is extremely unpopular with the people.
`Did you notice that an election took place in Minsk?”
Young people and opposition activists demonstrated Sunday evening in the main square of the Belarusian capital to reject the results of the parliamentary elections in advance.
A few hundred Belarusians have gathered Sunday evening since polling stations were closed in Minsk's market square to protest the fraudulent nature of parliamentary elections.
Although no result had yet been proclaimed, numerous young people had come to demonstrate against the power of Alexander Lukachenko: `The dictator shall resign'.
Some admit that they didn’t vote at all: “What’s the use if they don’t do what they want with my voice anyway,” says one teenager.
They do not believe that the regime, which has shown itself in the best light to resume negotiations with the European Union, is ready to make a democratic turnaround.
Sanctions, including non-granding visas to 40 leading Belarusian personalities and throwing up certain fortunes, could be lifted if these elections were judged to be clean by observers.
According to the opposition, they weren't.
`We were expelled by the electoral commissions and our observers were not allowed to take part in the counting," regretted Yuri Potemkin, press secretary of the Union of Democratic Forces, a heterogeneous merger of twelve parties.
This is a cosmetic change, points out Alexander Milinkievich, former opposition candidate for the 2006 presidential election and leader of the pro-Europe movement, because it matters who counts the votes, and with us this is the executive.
When lawmakers of the opposition move into parliament, it's because they've been appointed, not elected.
But their entry into parliament can mean airtime on television, meetings in the regions, and legislative proposals.”
The opposition, therefore, owes itself to interfere in the political game, because `the dialogue with the European Union must be opened.
Because if our economy, which is heavily indebted to Russia, falls into a crisis and we don't reform, we run the risk of becoming Russia's protectorate.
Well, we want to be independent."
The opposition fears that the Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe, which has followed the election, will not recognize the election as valid.
It stresses that turnouting figures appear too high, and that the expansion of voting in advance (26% of Minsk voters) has paved the way for any kind of manipulation.
“Have you noticed that an election has taken place?” asks Social Democrats Alexander Kozulin, one of the last two political prisoners released in August this year.
There was no one in the polling stations.
And there will be a maximum of five members of the opposition in parliament, namely those who are most likely to conform to the ideas of the government.”
The silence of the night descends again over October Square.
On television, President Lukachenko and the members of the Central Electoral Commission congratulate themselves on their success.
Disuase - From the blood that became safe again
Hema-Quebec celebrated the 10th anniversary of its founding yesterday, and its president, Dr Francine Décary, had every reason to rejoice, because Hema-Quebec has become one of the safest and most efficient blood banks in the world.
And above all, she has regained the public's trust, which had been severely shattered by the scandal from contaminated blood in the 1980s and 1990s.
It is known that national blood reserves managed by the Blood Donation Service of the Canadian Red Cross during the 1980s were contaminated with the HI virus and hepatitis C virus.
Faced with this drama and public dissatisfaction, the Government of Canada launched in February 1994 a public inquiry led by Judge Horace Krever aimed at shedding light on the circumstances that had led to such a tragedy and to work out guidelines to prevent a renewed catastrophe of this nature.
While Judge Krever was still reluctant to submit his report, several provinces at that time set up a committee whose aim was to develop a new system for managing canned blood in Canada.
In Quebec, the Gélineau committee fulfilled this mandate.
According to Gélineau and Krever reports, two blood banks were established in Canada: Hema-Quebec, who is in charge of Quebec, and the Canadian blood company to supply the other provinces and the two territories.
`1998, when Hema-Quebec started her work, barely 38% of people had confidence in Quebec's blood preservation system, Dr Décary recalls.
The situation was discouraging.
Due to the enormous efforts to improve the safety of canned blood and by working in this field during the following years, we have regained the trust of the public," she says, before proudly stressing that at the beginning of 2008, almost 94% of Quebec's population had confidence in Hema-Quebec.
However, Dr Décary maintains aware that `trust is something extremely fragile'.
Hema-Quebec has acted according to the principle of caution since its foundation on 28 September 1998.
At that time we witnessed the occurrence of numerous cases of the human form of mad cow disease in Europe, a new species of Creutzfeldt-Jakob's disease, tells this woman, who has been in charge since its inception in 1998.
“And we didn’t have the slightest idea if this disease could be transmitted via blood.
So we invoked one of the main directives of Krever and Gélineau's reports, according to which exclusion measures are applied if you don't know this and if a disease progresses only slowly, as is obviously the case with Creutzfeldt-Jakob's disease, which manifests itself through degeneration of the brain, which inevitably leads to death.
So in April 1999 it did not take blood from people who had resided in the United Kingdom for more than a month since 1980.
Well, a few years ago it was proved without any doubt that this disease is transmitted via the blood.
Thus this event has shown us that the principle of caution that we used has quite meaning.
In 1999, a much more accurate technique for detecting HIV appeared on the market: the nucleic acid tests (NST), which make it possible to detect the occurrence of the virus itself directly in the blood of the donor, and not the antibody against the virus, as was the case with the tests used so far.
The NST made it possible to shorten the incubation period that takes place between the time the person was infected and when the first viruses can be detected by our techniques in the blood, the specialist explains.
This time could be reduced to a duration of less than a week.
Thus, the risk that certain donors could be infected without knowing it, and this could not be recognized, was substantially reduced.
These improvements made it possible to significantly reduce the risk of transmitting AIDS via a blood transfusion.
These days, one in 12 million people are at risk of receiving HIV-infected blood.
“In other words, you’d have to distribute $12 million before someone is infected with HIV.
Since Hema-Quebec distributes between 400,000 and 500,000 blood preserves every year, it would take more than 24 years before a case where HIV has been transmitted via a canned blood.
I believe that nowadays you can feel comfortable if you get a blood transfusion or prescribe it to restore your health,” says Hema-Quebec’s director.
`Our challenge for the next ten years is to maintain the safety of our products.
You never know what pathogen can enter the system.
Only last year, the Chikungunya virus appeared on the island of Réunion, which, just like the West Nile virus, is transmitted by insects via the blood.
We need to continuously monitor," Dr. Décary continues, before stressing that Hema-Quebec must also provide an adequate supply in addition to a safe supply.
“The need is increasing as the population is ageing.
Because treatments to fight cancers are more aggressive, patients often need new red blood cells or platelets.
The ageration of the population is also having an impact on the blood donor network, which on average includes only 3% of Quebec’s blood donation residents.”
Any healthy person who is 18 years of age or older and meets the eligibility criteria can donate blood every 56 days, i.e. six times a year.
On average, 14% of people who provide their blood aren't accepted because, for example, they've contracted AIDS or malaria, and those who have traveled to a country where malaria is spreached are banned from donating blood for one year.
`With these 3% we manage to obtain an inventory that meets the needs of hospitals.
Lack of supplies are very rare.
But you have to renew the members of this donor network as they get older and can't donate blood anymore.
It's hard to convince people to donate blood but, most importantly, to keep them and get them to become regular donors.
Life is so hectic these days that one rarely finds the time to spend an hour donating blood," regrets Francine Décary.
With an annual budget of $297 million, Hema-Quebec not only provides canned blood, but also human tissues, including bone grafts, heart valves, eyeballs and skin, which is used almost exclusively for serious burn injuries.
These tissues are inspected, processed and frozen before being temporarily stored at Hema-Quebec, which also manages Quebec's only public umbilical cord blood bank.
The umbilical cord, which was formerly considered biomedical waste, contains rich blood on stem cells that can serve to treat serious diseases, such as leukemia.
`A few weeks agove we celebrated the storage of our thousandth umbilical cord blood sample, and we plan to lose more than a thousand annually.
“We should probably start making it available for transplants early next year,” explains Dr. Décary, who describes this program of umbilical cutting blood sampling as very popular.
“Many mothers aren’t convinced they don’t have access to it, because Hema-Quebec lacks the means to offer this complex and costly program in all Quebec hospitals.
Soccer - Impact in the semi-finals
Montreal's Impact qualified yesterday, thanks to a 3-to 1-win (4 to 3 with all goals) against the Sounders from Seattle in the second and decide game of the quarterfinal series at Saputo stadium, where the overall goal ratio counted, for the semi-final round of the elimination games of the USL's first league.
Midfielder Antonio Ribeiro scored the decisive goal in the ninetieth minute of play.
After a first-half without a goal for both one side and the other, midfielders David Testo and Rocco Plancentino landed the other hits for the formation of Montreal.
Roger Levesque was the only one who stood in the way for the Sounders.
Let's remind ourselves that at the first game of Friday's pre- and second-game series Impact had earned the Starfire Complex in Tukwila in a suburb of Seattle a 2-defeat.
In the semi-finals, Impact will compete with the Whitecaps from Vancouver.
Saturday night Thunder was able to make up for a 3-deficit through a 4 to 3 victory in the return game in Minnesota, but the Whitecaps moved into the semi-finals with an overall goal ratio of 5 to 4.
Impact arguably faces the Whitecaps Friday and Sunday, but the details of the game series will be determined today.
In short - Siegestor by Kovalev
The Canadian team's number 1 trio is ready to tackle the season.
Last night, the unit formed from Alex Kovalev, Tomas Plekanec and Andrej Kostitsyn pushed forward the attack on the Panthers from Florida with a stormfire, resulting in a 3 to 2 victory.
