I need another game to make me feel comfortable.”
`He fixated too much on that in the first half, Guy Carbonneau noted.
But I think he liked the end of the game.
He received stormy applause.”
Charest relies on the north
The prime minister does not rule out elections before the end of the year.
As soon as the Liberal Party's General Assembly came to an end, Jean Charest moved to Jean-Talon in Quebec yesterday, where the new health minister Yves Bolduc (left) is trying to be elected in today's partial election.
“Shifting the course of our last major border in the north”: this is the long-term goal that Jean Charest presented yesterday by visual means at the General Assembly of the Quebec Liberal Party in Levis, following the guidelines entirely.
During his lecture, a kind of picture mill was projected behind him.
On three huge screens, spectacular images of hydropower plants, mines, viewed by Quebec in `Google Maps' turned, maps, etc.
big means as during the election period.
By the way, the Prime Minister refused to rule out that he will hold the elections this autumn, even if he announced that his schedule was very full.
The `Plan Nord' is the fourth element of a `vision' that the Prime Minister allegedly has for Quebec, namely that of a `new economic area', which provides for an agreement with France regarding labor, an agreement between Canada and Europe and settlements with Ontario.
Although it has been frequently mentioned in the prime minister's speeches in recent times, the opening up of the north - the northern area at the 49th latitude - had never been the subject of a presentation.
However, the prime minister limited himself to explaining why the north was `a jewel with enormous potential', but did not name a date, investment sums or precise priorities for the upcoming projects.
At most, a number of some of the prospects of what could be worked on more: above all on water energy, on the forest, but particularly in the mining sector, favoured by the growth of emerging economies and the renewal of public infrastructure.
According to Mr Charest, the North possesses nickel, zinc, copper, platinum, gold, silver, iron, titanium and even diamonds.
This `area twice as large as France' `is near us, is in Quebec.
It's not just near us, it's within us," he emphasized, as to make it clear that he seeks to realize a national founding dream.
Quebec has grown very much through its connection with the north, he emphasized: the road to gold in Abitibi, the technique of Quebec, the James Bay.
"It's ours, it's our future," he added after taking a theatrical pause to look at the huge card behind him.
The north, 70% of the territory of Quebec, includes part of Labrador that Quebec has always demanded, the prime minister told the press.
“This is a traditional stance that all governments pursued.
There is a boundary line where there has been no agreement for a very long time, if the opportunity presents itself, I will take this stance again on behalf of the government," he explained.
In addition, `with climate change we need to rethink our vision from the north,' he explained, talking about the Northwest Passage, which will be navigable all year due to the ice melt.
Against this new geo-political background, the Prime Minister thinks that it is almost a duty to occupy this territory.
And the inhabitants of the area, the indigenous peoples and the Inuit?
The prime minister said that the Quebec government would deal with them very cautiously, and that they would benefit from this project, especially the boys.
Two former ministers from various parties, Pierre Corbeil and Michel Létourneau (former Pekist MP from Ungava), have already initiated deliberations at about thirty communities.
A Committee of Ministers headed by Benoît Pelletier, where Julie Boulet (transport and mineral resources) occupies an important place, has commenced work.
The Prime Minister does not rule out a great assembly, `not necessarily a summit', but an opportunity in which `one will create a common vision'.
Moreover, the development of the North will take place in accordance with the principles of sustainable development.
The Plan North will be `discussed, regulated and environmentally friendly', he promised.
Jean Charest described this `vision' as if it was a continuation of those of the liberal prime ministers who were in office before him.
`Like the liberals who were in office before us, we rethoughted to turn the challenges into new growth and development chances.
This, by the way, was one of the weekend's repeatedly raised themes, which opened on Friday with a big cocktail party in celebration of the 50th anniversary of the party's takeover by Jean Lesage.
One of the adopted resolutions envisages renaming Beauharnoi's power plant into Adélard-Godbout dam.
“From Godbout who created Hydro-Quebec, to Lesage, who has achieved the silent revolution and the nationalization of water electricity, to Bourassa, who made the James Bay, our party always knew how to determine the future,” Jean Charest explained.
To the press, he referred to a different, this this conservative project, to the program `Roads to the North' by John Diefenbaker, Prime Minister from 19595 to 1963.
With regard to the Eastmain1a-Ruper hydropower dam project, Mr Charest emphasizes that its start-up is planned for 2012.
"This remains between us, but I intend to be part of it," he confided to his party members, which provoked in those applause storms.
The Prime Minister, who said in April that there would be no elections in 2008, and certainly also in 2009, has indeed not yet ruled out the possibility of holding an election this autumn, which is possible for him by law.
However, he stresses that his schedule is full, mainly because of a Community Council mission in China and a summit between Quebec and New York.
The weekend’s General Assembly provided the Liberal Party leader with a platform of nationalist plans, above all from ‘goals relating to the constitution’, which were readily adopted: the PLQ in particular calls for the Recogny of ‘Queybec’ in the Constitution.
It also wants Quebec to appoint members of the Council of Canadian Broadcasting and Telecommunication, and for them to veto the decisions taken in Quebec.
On the part of the opposition, the guidelines of the Plan North have been taken up with skepticism.
Parti Québécoi's chief executive Pauline Marois has reintroduced the policy for the development of the North-du-Quebec, which the Landry government introduced in 2001.
According to her, the Liberal government, when it came to power in 2003, refrained from applying it.
If she had done it, `we would already be much further on today', judged Ms Marois alongside her candidate in Jean-Talon (where the election takes place today) Françoise Mercure.
The ADQ has advocated supporting the government's adoption of a plan North.
"But what we want is that this will not only be a great notion," declared party leader Sébastien Proulx in representation for Mario Dumont, who is coming back from vacation today.
Letters - A disgraced country
Mr Vic Toews, ex-'Justice' minister (what irony) in the Harper cabinet, it was who last summer allowed himself to say about Louise Arbour that she was a `disgrace' for Canada.
This woman, who has wageded a never-ending struggle for justice and deserves all our admiration to cover so with reproach, says just as much about the people around the prime minister as about Stephen Parker himself.
We are dealing here with a government that was the only one from all the countries affected to have not brought home their compatriot, who was trapped in the Guantanamo concentration camp.
A government that doesn't even take into account that Omar Khadr was still a juvenile when the things happened that he is accused.
The only government that, despite the fact that Canada has signed the Geneva Convention, breaks its word and lets a child's soldier vegetate in inhumane conditions.
The only one condescending to a judicial parody to assess the defendant's guilt.
Let us recall that UNICEF in particular, Amnesty International, and the Association of Canadian Lawyers are pleading to bring Omar Khadr home so that he gets a fair trial in Canada.
It is precisely precisely this government’s eyes to torture: it is of little interest to them that, after so many years of torment, a young adult will certainly be mentally broken forever.
We aren’t aware of it yet, but we are witnessingly circumcising of our rights, our freedom and our ‘democracy’ here, this word, which Harper constantly wears like a flag in front of him, and that sounds so meaningless from his mound.
So the time has come when there is no more any morality: one may arbitrarily mistreat, torture and imprison a Canadian citennant, at the discretion of a head of government, who to this day is only supported by the minority (may he belong entirely to the minority after 14 October...).
Many constituents believe to find their salvation in a party that preaches virtue, law and order. But they don't see that this government has renounced what constituted our country's reputation and dignity.
The shame, Mr. Toews, lies in this dismissal, and nowhere else.
Free Opinion - Barrick Gold's Orwellian Speech
In its September 17 issue, Le Devoir published a letter from Mr Patrick J. Garver, deputy director-gener of Canada's Barrick Gold.
This confirms, and pursues an Orwellian logic that the world’s largest gold extraction society sought a slandering lawsuit for six million against three authors and a small destitute publishing house, with the aim of opening a ‘public see-through debate’.
If it's true that judges are independent and impartial, then they don't judge the truth, especially in the case of defamation lawsuits, but about the law.
As expected, this fundamental difference was completely concealed by Mr Garver.
For Barrick Gold, the best way is to engage in a public debate, to ruin anyone who dares to hold one of their opposite opinion.
No matter how the trial turns out, the authors and editors of Noir Canada will have to spend considerable sums to prepare their defense and fight with very unequal weapons against an army of lawyers paid out of the almost inexhaustible pockets of the gold mine.
Moreover, the tactic in these unequal struggles is often to ruin the defenders in order to force a consensual settlement that silences the opposing side and also deters anyone who dares to deal with the question again.
If Barrick Gold really wanted a public debate, she would have had to present her view of things before public opinion.
In contrast to what is commonplace in many of the countries where Barrick Gold works, debates in Canada are usually played out by exchanging ideas rather thanking.
So it would have sufficed if the gold mine had disclosed its books and files and opened a real debate through the newspapers, media, publications and the financing of independent audit teams that could have shed light on this issue.
But Barrick Gold doesn't seem to have confidence in public opinion, brushing aside the petitions and letters in support of Noir Canada's writers with a hand movement.
Could Mr Garver perhaps explain how he can simultaneously ask for a 'public' debate while disregarding the opinions of this very public?
Seemingly, in the world of vice president, this word encompasses only the generously paid lawyers, and not the community of all Canadians; a quite cramped view of the term.
In a democracy, even slanders have the right to speak.
What Barrick Gold doesn't understand - or doesn't want to understand - is that the public who supports the authors and editors of Noir Canada is doing so largely because she rejects the gold mine's brutal stance.
This public is willing to listen and judge Barrick, but on the basis of a civilized exchange of arguments, and not within a framework that many perceive as a gaggle lawsuit (in the course of `public debate' Barrick Gold has just made a demand to the authors of Noir Canada, in which it requires them to no longer use this term...).
Aside from Mr Garver's surreal speech, Barrick Gold's complaint is a direct attack on freedom to academic research and truth-finding that is fundamental to any democratic society.
It completely denies the right to cite credible sources and discuss facts and interpretations that form the basis of intellectual work.
If it is impossible in a country like Canada to explore and discuss topics that the rich companies in the world do not like, who else can do that?
If Barrick Gold really wanted a public and transparent debate, she could lead it by following the scientific norms used by Noir Canada's authors.
She has a right to respond.
The university environment to which we belong has long known how to get to grips with debates and discrepancies and to resolve conflicts.
Barrick Gold's disproportionately lawsuit clearly demonstrates that, contrary to what she pretends, she has no desire for transparent debate.
This is a loss to the university world, just as for public debate, and it's time for governments to take action that restricts this kind of unjustified lawsuits.
Bault for Wall Street - Democrats and Republicans understand each other
The Bush administration and senior congresswomen, Republicans and Democrats alike, finally came to an agreement yesterday on the most extensive economic rescue plan since the Great Depression of the 1930s.
The new version of $700bn of the $700 billion plan to halt the financial crisis, which has been shaking Wall Street for months, has yet to be embraced by the two congressional chambers.
The main contents are: a better control of MEPs over the practical application of the plan, new mechanisms to secure the reimbursement of public funds employed, new injunctions to curb the number of homes seized due to arrears, as well as the right to monitor the salaries paid to the executives of the assisted companies.
U.S. Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson, the president of the Chamber of Deputies Nancy Pelosi, and Republican Republican representative at the negotiations, Senator Judd Gregg, announced yesterday, a few minutes after midnight, the good news, after a two-day negotiating marathon.
“We’ve made great progress towards an agreement that’s going to be effective in the market,” said Henry Paulson.
“It all happened to protect the real economy and the American people from the course crisis on Wall Street,” she said.
“This is the fundamental point: if we don’t do that, the trauma, chaos and unrest in Americans’ daily lives will be enormous,” explained Republican Judd Gregg.
Negotiations had accelerated during the last two hours for fear of the effect that a renewed failure on investor sentiment would have.
The deadline that had been agreed was the market opening this morning.
The two American presidential candidates, who only took part in the negotiations late, showed themselves rather as proponents of the agreement, but without failing to express their dissatisfaction that they must be met in this extreme way.
Democratic Senator Barrack Obama insisted on the addition of certain injunctions, especially those designed to allow owners to retain their homes, and those concerning the salaries of senior executives of the companies rescued by the state.
`If you ask the taxpayers to take extraordinary measures due to the irresponsibility of certain people, that is nothing to congratulate.
But these measures are necessary," he explained on American television stations.
When he was asked on another broadcaster if he will vote in favour of the proposed plan, his Republican opponent John McCain replied: `I'd like to know the specifics first, but I hope so.
“The guiding principles I have read [show] that this is one thing we will all need to digest, and with which we will be able to move forward.
The option of doing nothing at all is quite simply not acceptable," he said.
The $700 billion plan is geared towards using taxpayers' money
It comes 14 months after a crisis triggered by the bursting of a real estate bubble that initially led to the collapse of high-risk mortgage loans (subprime), before it expanded to other forms of more widespread bonds (PCAA), and eventually culminated in a freezing of financial strength and the ruin of banks and financial institutions.
It was a group of Republican lawmakers who last week let the first attempt to reach a settlement fail.
Since the Democrats have accepted that certain `toxic' assets of societies in distress should only be secured by the government and not bought, it is believed to escape the impasse.
In order to round off perspectives for everyone in Congress, an injunction has also been added that says that if the government were to make losses within five years, it could then levy new taxes in order to regain its invested capital.
The necludes the payment of an initial sum of $250 billion, to which another €100 billion can be added if the president considers it necessary.
The payment of another 350 billion must be passed by Congress in a re-vote.
In return for its assistance, Washington will receive shares in the supported companies, which will earn him dividends in the event of profits, and which it can sell again if the storm has passed.
The Federal Government will have the opportunity to stop the attachment procedures relating to the homes attached to the real estate loans affected by this plan, and will also set deadlines for repayment and severances paid to the senior employees of the companies concerned.
The developers of the plan would like the Chamber of Deputies to vote on him as early as today, and the Senate then on Wednesday.
Despite all of their efforts, it's not yet certain that their plan will get the green light.
In fact, more votes rose yesterday against this project, on the part of both the Republicans and Democrats.
'Quite obviously Washington doesn't understand us,' Republican Scott Garett, a member of the Chamber's finance commission, explained to his voters.
“Yes, we have a big financial problem.
Yes, there are solutions.
But not the one that involves asking the taxpayers to cash in.'
Democratic lawmaker Brad Sherman, head of a group in Congress called `the Kaukus of skeptics', portrays things differently but comes to the same conclusion.
“Four hundred excellent economics professors, including three Nobel Prize winners, have written to us to tell us: we ask Congress not to be pressured and to carefully consider which way to go,” he said.
At the same time at the same time, we in Europe once again got proof that this is a global crisis.
At an emergency meeting, the three Benelux countries announced the partial nationalization of Forti's banking group for €11.2 billion ($16.3 billion).
The intervension of the governments of Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxembourg became necessary after the French bank BNP Paribas announced that it would refrain from buying up its competitor, who is in great trouble since she lost 2 billion euros ($2.9 billion) in the crisis due to the high-risk real estate loans in the United States.
Fortis employs 85,000 people and is one of Europe's twenty largest banks and the ten largest insurers.
“That’s not lost money,” Dutch Finance Minister Wouter Bos declared yesterday.
To compensate, we have the right to vote and influence in the bank.
This is exactly what savers and households value now that uncertain times are announcing.”
During this period, on the other side of the English Channel, the British government was in the process of nationalising another bank brought into trouble by the financial crisis.
According to the English press, the government would take over all current loans from Bank Bradford and Bringley, over 41 billion ($75.5 billion) from real estate loans.
Spanish bank Santander announced yesterday that it would bough the British bank's branches and customer accounts.
Bradford and Bringley would be the second bank the British government must nationalise since the beginning of this financial crisis.
Paul Newman (19252008) - Death of a Dedicated Icon
His beauty with the azure eyes was for a long time his trump, no one would say his weakness.
Had the American actor freed himself from the narcissistic mirror with the charming smile?
Yeah and no.
His sensitivity, his sensitivity and an obvious talent have probably also kept him away from a position of power that gives birth to the truly terrifying monsters.
But what a great endurance runner!
Paul Newman, who died on Friday at the age of 83 from the aftermath of lung cancer at his ranch in Connecticut, remains one of the last icons of American cinema of the golden years.
He was less monumental than Brando, to whom he resembled and for whom he was for a long time a kind of rival on the screen, more careful in his spectacle, certainly also more fragile, but also an apostle of freedom and blasting dramaturgical conventions.
Mixed blood often makes wonderful children.
He was born in 1925 in Ohio as the son of a German-born mother.
One of the glorious faces of the American dream was to emerge from its European roots.
With no excessive ego, extreme quality feature.
Because it reflected an entire country that was in love with the self-made-man, the fighter who was injured in the Pacific War, who studied acting at Yale in the early 50s and then visited the Actor's Studio in New York before succeeding in the theater, on television and soon in the cinema.
It was precisely this man who fought for civil rights that was also the driver, who finished second at Man's 24-hour race in 1979, and also a sex symbol, even though he lived together with actress Joanne Woodward in a flagship relationship since 1958.
With him also disappear the unknown facets of this mysterious, restrained, committed and hypersensitive to the glamour of show business, whose mystery will never be completely solved.
a decent man, in the purest sense of the word.
Paul Newman's death put cinema lovers in wobble this weekend, while all his big roles were played on the dark screen of memories.
More than half a century of American film art in constant change passes with him.
That's when the charming cowboy from "Butch Cassidy and the Sundance Kid" (1969) comes to mind again, and the young man who rejects his sensual wife (Elizabeth Taylor) in The Cat on the hot tin roof, which was filmed by Tennessee Williams in 1958.
A much shorter memory (1990) is evoked by his sensible Mr. Bridge, who at his old age is disturbed by a wedding that didn't fulfill all its promises.
There have been so many other Paul Newmans throughout history.
This criminal, stormy, violent and unforgettable boxer Rocky Graziano from Somebody Up There Likes Me by Robert Wise, a role that was intended for James Dean, resummened after his death, which Newman properly made known in 1956.
This sympathetic crook in outstanding The Sting (The Clou), which was awarded the Oskar for best film in 1973.
As a bitter and vengeful billiard player in Robert Rossen's wonderful The Hustler (1961),
That face through the times, that's he.
It's still him.
Epoches unite in his resume.
The youth knows him especially from the cult film Slap Shot by George Roy Hill (1977), as Reggie Dunlop, the little orthodox coach of an ice hockey team.
His fans found him again in The Hudsucker Proxy of the Brothers Coen in 1994.
With a final role in 2003 as the godfather of the Irish mafia in road to perdition by Sam Mendes, the circle closed.
In contrast to several cinema legends who withdrew early enough from the film world, Newman should stay in the saddle for a long time, revealing Hollywood, his assets, his pomp and his concessions.
French filmmaker Bertrand Tavernier saw him, not without humour, as a living challenge to 'underplaying' when he judged his overly expressive performances.
The profession was not always easy for the beautiful Paul Newman, to whom the roles flew for a long time.
However, he was less likely to play Sunnyboys as an outsider: neurotics, alcoholics, nasties, or the desperate, with whose perfect depiction he reflected his slipping and rebellion.
As a director, Newman unveiled unfamiliar sides of his true sensitive sensitivity.
His Rachel, Rachel from 1968 (according to Canadian Margaret Laurence's novel) with his wife Joanne Woodward in the lead role, drew a subtle portrait of women full of melancholy.
He, who lost his son Scot by an overdose, dedicated his 1984 film Harry and Son, a work full of sensitivity and sadness.
At the flood of honours poured over Newman yesterday, his close friend and former gunmate Robert Redford (they had played together in The Sting and in Butch Cassidy and the Sundance Kid) greatly stated: `His presence improved my life and this country.'
Generosity and joie de vivre were essential characteristics of the deceased actor.
Apart from his legendary career, a great champion of civil rights, environmentalism and independent cinema, an opponent of drifting Hollywood, is now going there.
We've arguably often seen him standing in bowls full of vinaigrettes, in sauces, pastries or other foods, and considered this to be quite tasteless, although we still found it funny at first.
Nevertheless, the profits from his sales flowed into his foundation for children in need.
Instead of resting on his myth, Newman got involved until the shot.
He left a letter to his five daughters with these sentences: `Always and until at the very end, your father was incredibly grateful for his good star.
It was an honor to be allowed to be here.”
And for us it was an honor to pursue the lives of such an intelligent and generous American actor.
An exemplary citizen, in the narrowest and broadest sense.
What was produced into the billing factory of Zurschlag & Co.?
Negotiations will begin on October 8 in the criminal case against Jáno's surcharge and his accomplices.
The defendants are accused in particular of involvement in organised crime, grossly negligent injury and business fraud, and other criminal offences, including forgery of documents.
According to the public prosecutor's office, the funds lucrated from the tenders were used for their own purposes, while the services rendered were often substantiated with forged invoices.
State funds and grants from self-government - a totall of HUF 65 million - are said to have flowed partly into the coffers of Fiatal Baloldal, partly into the private coffers of individuals.
As the investigating authorities announced, fictitious invoices have been submitted when receiving the tender money, and the clubs should not have fulfilled their duties either.
According to the indictment, much of the bills are said to have been forged.
The invoicing blocks were purchased on the market by Józsefváros, the round seals were made in Kiskunhalas.
The decision on which companies the invoices are to be issued was decided on that tour north of Lake Balaton, to which István Lados was sent by Jáno's surcharge.
In the course of this "exploration tour", Lados was supposed to locate guesthouses and restaurants.
He demanded bills for consumption in order to be able to issue invoices based on these templates for the Youth and Sports Ministry, the Ministry of Social Affairs and Labour, as well as the self-government of Budapest.
These invoices are said to have been manipulated by the majority By István Lados and his accomplice, András Őri, according to our information.
One of Lados' "exploration tour" was a beer restaurant.
According to two invoices issued by Zuschlag and Co in 2003, the gastronomy company is said to have hosted the ninety-member group of the association Biztos Pont Egyesület.
Now, however, the boss of the company states that such a large group would not have fit into the restaurant at once.
Another invoice, issued in the summer of 2002 at the Jobb Jövőért Foundation, later known as Együtt a Harmadik Évezredért, also exhibited serious form and content errors.
In a guesthouse in Badacsonytomaj, which has only three rooms and cannot accommodate more than ten people at the same time, 130 guests are said to have stayed according to the bill.
There were apparently no limits to the imagination of the forgers of invoices when it came to Pigal Kft., a gastronomy company in whose accounting documents of the customers Jobb Jövőért and Hoppá, according to the prosecution, did not even appear.
As Managing Director Győző Farkas told hvg.hu, it was immediately clear to him that they were forged documents when documents were presented to him by the prosecutor’s office during a questioning.
"Our company name and tax number were incorrectly attached to the stamp."
Apart from formal errors, the fact that the company is said to have accommodated one hundred people, after having a total of eleven rooms, which impossibly offer space for so many persons.
Food had also been cited on the invoice, although the restaurant had not been operational for a long time.
The busy forgeretters have also neglected to verify that the Budapest travel agency they have targeted exclusively offers trips abroad, and so they have indicated Hungary trips on the cash payment bill, which also contradicted the company's accounting practice.
As it became known in the course of the investigations, they have also cited VAT on the invoices, although travel agency services relating to a country outside the EU are VAT-free.
The amount of total VAT was incorrect on four invoices issued by Hotel Révfülöp Kft. to the Jobb Jövőért Foundation, because the company charges only 15 per cent VAT instead of 25, in addition, only machine invoices are issued by the employees, but the forged documents were handwritten.
Another error made by the "invoicers" was that, in one case, they had indicated "goods transport" as a service rendered, although this is not offered by the company specializing in investment advice, the prosecutor's office said.
It was thanks to further formal errors - incorrect formal format, wrong stamp - that the invoices issued to Együtt a Harmadik Évezredért Alapítvány, as well as to XXI Századi Kulturális Egyesület and Biztos Pont could be exposed as forgeries.
Likewise in the case of Ravenna Bt. and the Kunfehértó sports camp, whose managing directors asserted that they had never issued the invoices.
Another "mistakes" at HIB Kft. from Nyíregyháza have been underived by the counterfeiters at Nyíregyháza: this company was in dissolution at the time (2001), when the group of Fiatal V�árosért Egyesület is said to have eaten there - the mass steward therefore claimed not to have issued the bills themselves.
Consciously faked
The other group of accountants are persons who were well aware that they would have to pay fictitious invoices.
At the request of Fiatal Baloldal - Ifjú Szocialisták, the Esély Budapestért Alapítvány Foundation has issued invoices for the Együtt a Harmadik Évezredért Foundation, amounting to about HUF 4 million.
According to the indictment, the managing director of Collab Kft. was persuaded by one of the defendants, Ludovic Györfi, to issue the fictitious bills.
Biogate Kft., which is also currently in liquidation, is also said to have cooperated with surcharge and co.: In 2003, the GmbH is said to have provided Jövőért Alapítvány, as well as the associations Atlasz Egyesület and Vösfenyő Egyesület with forged invoices with a total value of HUF 5 million, which referred to services not rendered.
However, there were also no payment transactions, as the invoices were cancelled immediately after their exhibition, which those affected, however, have forgotten to note in their documents.
The third group of accountants includes persons directly associated with the defendants.
One of them is Iván Kubatov, the postman of the Ukrainian managing director of Delta Trió Kft., a Budapest-based fuel and vehicle trading company that cannot be found there.
This company is said to have issued several invoices for the sale of IT components with a total value of HUF about 18 million in the period from October to December 2005.
According to the investigating authority, Cash Investment Kft. from Kiskunhalas, which has been in liquidation since 14 September 2005, is said to have acted as the buyer of these invoices.
The managing director of this company was a Romanian, to whom the company documents should also be delivered by Iván Kubatov, between 2002 and 2005.
According to our informations, Kubatov confessed during the investigation of having issued invoices on behalf of the GmbH to the associations Atlasz and Hoppá, as well as to Együtt a Harmadik Évezredért Alapítvány and having collected their equivalents themselves.
Fa-Bani Jos Faipari Kft. and Jos-Investment Bt. are said to have been founded by Romanian bricklayers at Iván Kubatov's express request.
In truth, however, none of these companies were active, which according to the defendant was due to a lack of assignments.
Nevertheless, he was able to benefit from the companies: At the request of his accomplice, the lawyer Zsolt Szente, who was tasked with the preparation of the company foundation documents, he has issued invoices on behalf of the companies to the clubs associated with a surcharge, such as Atlasz and Y-Generáció.
Kubatov is said to have issued a total of $14.2 mill on behalf of Cash Investment, Delta Trió, Fa-Bani Jos, Jos Investment and Linaxtrade.
The public prosecutor's officence assumes that Kubatov may each have collected 10% of the invoice amount as well as VAT.
Kubatov is said to have even abused Szi-Beri Bt. for issuing fictitious invoices - in this case, the bills have been enclosed with Biztos Pont's applications.
Y-Generáció and Szövetség a Modern Magyarországért submitted their applications to the Socialist Party's non-profit organization, Táncsics Mihály Alapítvány ("TMA"), in January 2006.
Both received 500,000 forints each, but have used these amounts for the purpose.
To cover up this, they have submitted five fictitious invoices to the TMA from Works Team Kft.
By the way, these invoices were ensured during the search of Árpád Kemecsei's office in the Ministry of Education.
During the negotiations, some defendants are now expecting a confession about the procedure of forgery of invoices, as they have already been filed before the public prosecutor's office.
One sensation could be if the main defendant Jáno's surcharge decides to make a comprehensive confession.
His previous information was less detailed, and although he had already pledged several times to report comprehensively on everything, his confession is still pending.
The question therefore remains whether this is in his interest, whether the Socialists have concluded a pact with him regarding the reparation of the bulk of HUF's damage incurred by HUF 50 million, and whether surcharge in return is prepared to take responsibility.
However, it is quite possible that surcharge now plays the "insulted man" after his requests for mitigating imprisonment and house arrest had met with deaf ears.
While other criminals, who have caused far greater damage with economic offences and have not remunerated them, are on the loose, or at least under house arreasured, surchargist is denied this possibility.
The psychology of outbidding - or why we're willing to pay more
At auctions, buyers tend to spend more and thus oversate what is offered.
Neurologists and economists at the University of New York have now succeeded in discovering the neurological-economic foundations of this well-known and seemingly irrational behavior.
According to a study published in "Science" magazine, people do not want to give up in a competition, such as an auction on eBay, and are therefore willing to pay even excessive prices.
The researchers have tried to explore the neurological schema of this "game" with experimental auctions.
One group of volunteers was supposed to play, while the other participated in a playful action - in both cases the active brain regions of the subjects were examined by functional magnetic resonance (fMRI).
In both games, the participants were able to win money, but an increased activity of the striatum, a complex below the cortex, was measured among losers of the auction, in which the opponent's overbidding plays an essential role.
Among many other functions, this brain region is responsible for the "self-reward" of the nervous system.
Therefore, the researchers thought that the price increase could be explained by avoiding an overreaction of the striatum.
In order to substantiate their thesis, the economists in the research group also conducted investigations into the follow-up of money.
In this case, three groups should bid against a partner under different conditions.
The control group was informed of the true value of the product, after which they made their stakes.
Another group has also been promised €15 reward in case they emerge as winners from the game.
The third group received $15 before the auction, which was also lost in the event of a losing match.
Thus, the difference between the two groups was merely that the emphasis on the one was on winning, while in the other on losing.
As proof of the hypothesis expressed by neurologists, however, the members of the "against losing" playing group were ready for higher stakes than those in the control or "profit" group.
“These results cannot be derived by any previous economic theory.
In fact, these have so far justified the behaviours observed at auctions only with the joy of winning or risk avoidance.
The contradiction between these two theories has been resolved thanks to the imaging neurological procedures, so that we now have a third explanation available," said Andrew Schotter, professor of the Faculty of Economics at the University of New York.
Hepatitis: When is the doctor to visit?
Worldwide, around half a billion people suffer from chronic hepatitis B or C, and in Hungary this chronic liver disease is listed among the leading causes of death.
The number of people infected is estimated at a hundred thousand in this country, but only seven thousand are treated, which is because the patients often do not know that they are infected.
Hepatitis is the collective term for infectious liver diseases caused by various viruses.
The symptoms are often similar - liver inflammation and jaundiction are common, but the pathogens and the diseases differ from each other.
Depending on the pathogen, hepatitis viruses spread in two different ways: in hepatitis A and E through the digestive tract (for example by contaminated foods or water), in hepatitis B, C and D the viruses can be transmitted by blood and other body fluids (such as sperm, vaginal secretions or saliva).
In any case, travelers are recommended vaccination against viruses A and B, there is no vaccine against the virus C.
Symptoms of hepatitis A appear about two-three weeks after consuming the contaminated food and are generally accompanied by discomfort, weakness, abdominal pain and nausea.
After that, jaundice occurs on the skin and in the eyes, the stool becomes light, the urine dark, and the recovery can last for up to two-three weeks.
Worldwide, about 1 million people die every year from the far more severe diseases such as liver cancer and cirrhosis caused by hepatitis B.
The main reason for this is that it is estimated that only one in ten infected persons is undergoing drug treatment.
Hepatitis C can also have serious consequences: untreated, it can lead to cirrhosis and liver cancer.
Most infected people don't even know about their disease, because the pathogen insidiously destroys the liver cells - in many cases, the first symptoms appear after 20-30 years.
Therefore, timely prevention, such as regular check-ups, is essential.
There are three groups in which this disease is more common: those who underwent a blood transfusion before 1991, drug addicts, and tattoos.
Currently, the number of chronic hepatitis C sufferers worldwide is estimated at 200 million.
Since the disease is already detectable during a routine laboratory examination, it is advisable to visit the family doctor at the first signs of unknown origin.
Detected on time, the disease is curable, the virus can be exterminated, causing the risk of infection to be eliminated and preventing the development of cancer.
September 30 is World Hepatitis Day, hosted for the first time by patient representatives worldwide in 2004.
The aim of the action is to strengthen the awareness of this virus-caused chronic liver inflammation, its emergence and the importance of early detection.
Do believers have less pain?
According to the latest research, the religious faith acts like a kind of “painkillers” that is said to have facilitated the death of Catholic martyrs.
Scientists at Oxford University carried out an interesting experiment: it was investigated to what extent faith in God influences the sensation of pain of man, the British newspaper "Telegraph" reported.
In the course of the rather bizarre examination, 12 believers and atheists each received electric shocks while studying a painting of the Mother of God.
As the results showed, believers were able to turn off most of the pain.
Using a brain-scan technology, it could even be proven that Catholics were able to activate the part of their brain responsible for pain regulation.
The study was the first in a research project in which, in addition to scientists, philosophers and theologians from various departments of the university participate.
During the experiment, a sparkling device was attached to the left hand, which gave the electric shocks.
The participants were asked to focus heavily on two paintings: on the one hand a picture of the Mother of God, painted by Sassoferrato in the 17th century, and on the one hand Leonardo da Vinci's "Lady with the Hermelin".
The researchers hoped that the sight of the Virgin Mary among the faithful would evoke a kind of religious trance while selecting da Vinci's painting because it was hardly distinguished from the other painting and was perceived as reassuring.
The subjects each spent half an hour in an MRI scanner, and during this time received a total of 20 electric shocks in four stages, while they were supposed to look at either the religious or the other painting.
The Catholics reported that, at the sight of the Mother of God, they felt "in safety," as if they were "cared for," and they felt "calm and peace."
While contemplating the religious painting, they reported over 12% less pain than if they had Leonardo's work in mind.
The psychologist Miguel Farias, who also participated in the experiment, said that similar effects could also evoke similar effects with other, strong-acting images in the atheists, one would only have to find the corresponding photo.
"We'd have to find an image that triggers very positive sensations, such as a mother or a father," according to the psychologist.
Anglican Bishop Tom Wright welcomed the results of the research.
"Practicing the faith should change our personality, and in many cases," Wright said.
After these excellent results, the only question left is what effect a Madonna picture of Leonardo or another comparable painter would have on the pain regulation of the faithful.
Would the high-quality Virgin Mary achieve even better results?
You're what you say about yourself
On the occasion of her 10th birthday, Google can rightly claim that it can be found in people’s pockets as well as above their heads in the skies: she knows where people have spent their holidays, what specialists they are going to, and what moves them.
And that's still not enough, according to Eric Schmidt, one of Google's managing directors.
Toward scepticians plagued by Orwellian horror visions, he lets align: These would be overlooked just the essentials of Web 2.0.
In an interview with the Financial Times recently, Schmidt said Google's main goal was to collect even more personal data about users and thus get closer to the company's original objective, according to which it wants to "arrange all information in the world."
Schmidt explained that after ten years of world-changing work, Google is still only at the beginning of information gathering.
Asked about the status quo in five years, Schmidt said: he wished Google would be able to answer questions such as "What are I supposed to do tomorrow?" or "Which job really suits me, but software programs aren't able to do so, because they don't know enough about users.
Not all Internet users are aware that in the course of their Internet searches, the search terms would be registered, as well as the IP addresses from which they were searched and the pages visited.
For example, the "Personalized Search" introduced two years ago allows the visitor to save his own search history and later resort to it, so that the hits can later be generated much more precisely, and based on the same logic are also the so-called "recommendations", with which the system recommends tailor-made products and services due to previous preferences.
However, users wonder what happens in the event of data misuse - "If you look at what I'm doing" - some are concerned about the Google data monopoly.
In most cases, however, it's more the potential power represented by the firm that seems terrifying, and less the sense of guilt or the fear of punishment, the pursuit of freedom or rebellion on power that's refusing many Internet users.
However, it is a proven fact that the search terms are not only stored, but also passed on to third parties on occasion, and in these legal cases there is no protection for the personal rights of the Internet user.
A prime example of this has recently been the crackdown on paedophilia in Brazil, in the course of which Google handed over information to the Brazilian government, which was collected by the community site called "Orkut" and also made it possible to identify paedophilies.
However, users worried about personal rights were able to breathe a sigh of relief in May, when Google, together with the civil rights organization EFF, successfully went to court against the FBI: Google cannot be forced in the future with a point of the Constitution to issue information to the FBI on a secret request.
(However, the federal government has successfully obtained the publication of 50,000 web addresses and 1,000 search terms with reference to the Youth Protection Act passed years ago, but it has been deprived of the list of individual users, or their web and search term list for two months.)
Google's image therefore remains controversial: those who are reluctant to release their personal data because they fear the loss of their privacy and privacy through the use of Google's services, scourge the company with the indignation due to conservative companies.
However, American "geek culture," which is present in many countries and unites enthusiastic computer fans, activists and users, sees the next generation of "cool entrepreneurs" in them, as opposed to Microsoft, which is the market leader thanks to Windows, even if both are fighting for the same goal.
One possible reason for this is that, unlike Microsoft, Google doesn’t require any identification codes for its users and provides its services free of charge, making an open, constantly updated source code and program package seem to be far more attractive, youthful and cooler than the software giant’s official practices.
On the other hand, the geeks see through the problematic relationship of personal rights and the Internet razor-sharply - a relationship that has become the focus, especially in recent years thanks to the term "Web 2.0".
"Web 2.0" is about nothing more than collaboration, i.e. the users intentionally leave traces and use the available communication channels, and even if the dimensions are individually different, the main thing remains the same, namely to show themselves publicly.
An average younger can simultaneously manage their profile on several community portals - from Facebook to Xanga, can connect it via other services, such as Twitter, or music services such as last.fm, and can eventually integrate all of this into a tracking system such as FriendFeed or Blip.fm. In addition, they can conduct blogs that automatically display the latest photo galleries gosted on Flickr or Picasa.
And a number of other exemplary cases proves: When looking at the party photos of the previous night, the girlfriend soon finds out with whom her unfaithful friend has enjoyed, or the employee is fired, because in his blog all too honestly his annoyance about the company management - one thing is certain: The term "privacy" has now been completely reinterpreted.
Who leads an online life, is what he discloses of himself - one could roughly formulate the basic idea behind the behavior of today's youth.
And if you try to keep something secret, or even bypass the Google monopoly, you are welcome to do so - such as Scroogle, a tool that accesses Google's search engine, but intentionally leaves no traces.
Quite a few people deliberately do not register on community sites so that they cannot be found, but also Google’s online applications, such as word processing and graphical tools and RSS feeds, can be handled alternative versions that aren’t tied to huge server parks and therefore don’t generate the best possible profile from the user.
However, public opinion waits until Google shows its true face.
With its current value of $156 billion, the company is even ahead of IBM ($140 billion), Intel ($124 billion) or Coca-Cola ($110 billion), AdWords/AdSense’s advertising space threatens advertising agencies’ revenues, and the ever-increasingly rolled-up services not only Microsoft, but also other software developers.
Critical viewers, however, also find the disturbing anti-poles: For example, the Group management has completely rejected a protest of the employees, after the fees of the company’s own kindergarten were doubled, in addition, the management said that the repeated catering of the employees was too costly. However, all of this seems almost mild that Google even went to its knees in 2006 before the Chinese government and agreed with an agreement, according to which all the regime-critical hits.
The internal regulation of the group is that no new service is advertised in advance, but only if it is already available. Nevertheless, the events in the coming months and years could change a lot again: While some believe that Google's book and magazine scanning or archiving project now assumes that the company would soon have to deal with the Supreme Court, others are rather concerned about the "Health" system, which is the creation.
And the fact that Google builds up a genetic database already provides the next generation of sci-fi writers with the substance - such as "Scroogled", the famous novel by Cory Doctorow describes a future in the best Orwellian manner, in which a person becomes frighteningly approachable thanks to Google-related data.
Two teenagers help Ózd's double murderer with trace removal
In connection with the double murder of Ózd, the police have now interrogated two youths who are suspected to have helped in the removal of the traces, said the spokesman for the police commissioner Borsod on Tuesday.
The juveniles were confessed, they are currently being charged with aid.
According to the MTI news agency, these are two girls defending themselves on the loose.
Aid is threatened with imprisonment of up to five years.
The suspect of the double murder of Ózd, a 23-year-old local man, was taken into custody on Sunday.
The man is under urgent suspicion of intentionally murdering several people out of greed.
In the course of the investigation, it turned out that the 23-year-old had owed a 53-year-old a larger amount of money.
However, the suspect did not want to pay his debts, so he lured his believer last Wednesday to a site in Ózd, where he murdered him and his nine-year-old daughter.
According to the MTI, the man planned the offence by a long hand - this also proves the fact that he committed the killings with a weapon hidden at the scene.
Together with experts, the police are now investigating the murder weapon as well as the circumstances of its use.
Warsaw Stork Stock Puts Offer for Prague Stock Exchange
Around €200 million, or €5 billion Czech crowns, has been offered by the Warsaw Stock Exchange for the one-to-of-the-first purchase of the Prague Stock Exchange’s share packages.
Ludwik Sobolewski, president of the Warsaw Stock Exchange, reiterated the offer that the Poles had laid out despite the fact that they were formerly expelled by the Czechs as possible owners of their stock exchange.
This is due that the Warsaw Stock Exchange belongs entirely to the state.
99% of the shares are in the hands of the Polish state, which, however, intends to divest more than 60% of securities in the coming months.
According to the Czech daily newspaper "E15", the offer of the Poles includes the highest purchase price ever offered for the Prague Stock Exchange.
As the newspaper reports, the Vienna Stock Exchange, the Nasdaq Transatlantic, the New York Stock Exchange, the London Stock Exchange and the Deutsche Börse have so fared offers.
Petr Koblic, chief executive of the Prague Stock Exchange, said last week that of all those interested, the Warsaw Stock Exchange would arguably be the worst neighboring, and he also did not rule out that in the future this could even lead to the closure of the Prague Stock Exchange.
Market experts estimate the value of the Prague Stock Exchange at 5-10 billion crowns.
According to shareholder agreements, 90% of the shares could be sold.
Patria Finance Investmentbank is the largest owner of the Prague Stock Exchange with a 25 percent stake, followed by Ceská Sporitelna Bank with 14.7 and Tiger Holding Four S.a.r.l. with 13.5 percent.
The financial crisis from the point of view of European newspapers
In their commentaries, which were devoted by the ever-increasingling international financial crisis, some leading European newspapers already spoke on Tuesday of the "Second 11 September".
We owe the global financial disaster to the American president, for example "La Repubblica" - the president had not even been able to his own party for the bill behind him, which should bring some relief to the frightening situation...
Instead of a "new Pearl Harbor," as billionaire investor Warren Buffett called it, it'd be more appropriate to speak of a "Second 9/11."
Parts of the press in Madrid also locate the scapegoat in Washington.
According to the "El Mundo," the lack of political leadership is to blame because the global economy is now on the verge of collapsing.
The American financial system threatens to collapse more and more, and the whole world will suffer.
Bush's plan wasn't perfect, but now, after the vote in Congress, there's a danger that Washington has no political means for crisis management.
The world economy is hanging in the air, just at a time when one of the greatest transformations in the history of capitalism is taking place.
One can no longer disregard the threat, for example, according to the Genevan "Tages-Anzeiger".
Although some states could still make bailouts available to their faltering financial institutions, and save the banks with the money of the taxpayers from bankruptcy.
However, if a domino effect is triggered and even more banks go bust, then neither further financial injections nor other emergency measures help.
In the long term, EU member states would have to jump over their shade and centralize control of the money markets.
On Monday alone, more than €100 billion was paid out to debilitating financial institutions, the Viennese "Der Standard" recalls. In addition, according to the newspaper, the concerns are increasingly realistic that European taxpayers will now also have to reach a little deeper into their pockets to get out of the crisis.
Until now, the EU has not seriously dealt with the crisis, which is now sweeping like a tsunami over the old continents.
The "saviors" would not have to "wag" several hundred billion euros immediately, but it would be advisable to hold, in which case it would be reasonable to touch the taxpayers' money in order to avert even greater damage.
Intelligent car imitates the flight of bumblebees
What would be the best way to develop new technologies for accident avoidance if not to imitate the obstacle sensors from nature?
For example, those of bees.
CEATEC in Japan unveiled an astonishing project where Nissan engineers were inspired by bumblebees in the creation of new-aviding systems.
In collaboration with Tokyo University, Nissan has built the BR23C biomimetic system controlled by car robots, a mini-car equipped with the characteristics of the bees, with the aim of supporting the development of a system that avoids any collisions.
In flight, each bee forms its own, oval "private area," which is very similar to our own "protective shield," Kazuhhiro Doi, product manager for technology PR and research development, said at the exhibition.
The composite eye of the bee, which can see in any direction and at an angle of over 300 degrees, plays an important role.
To imitate the function of the assembled eye, the researchers picked up on the idea of the laser-guided distance meter.
This identifies obstacles in front of the BR23C in a field of view of 180 degrees within 2 meters, calculates its distances and sends signals to the microprocessor on board, which immediately initiates the collision-avoiding process.
The obstacle is registered in fractions of a second, and the car robot immediately changes course, similar to the movement of the bees by turning the tires around the appropriate angle.
In contrast to previous systems, this maneuver takes place purely instinctively.
"If it weren't like that, the car robot couldn't respond quickly enough to dodge the obstacles," explained Toshiyuki Andoh, head of the "Mobility Laboratory" and the car robot project.
The system must react instinctively and immediately, as this technology corresponds to the innermost and most vulnerable part of the protective shield - a layer within which accidents are currently still considered unavoidable.
The whole process must reflect the behavior of the bees when dodging other bees.
And that has to happen in a fraction of a second.
However, in contrast to the bees, the avoidance cannot take place up, down, or even diagonal - it can only be two-dimensional, and only in the direction of the reversibility of the tires.
While the bees have endless possibilities to avoid other bees, the researchers were only able to make use of the rotary movement or acceleration and braking in the car robot.
The BR23C contains lithium polymer batteries, which are lighter and more energy-efficient, and therefore have a longer service life than traditional lithium-ion batteries.
Andoh explained it depends on the instinctive, intelligent response, and less the processing of the data, and even less the ability to store it.
This device only needs to process a few inputs per second and react accordingly.
It does not have to store them, or produce an intuitive, logical reaction using previous maneuvers, so that it constantly takes up only a handful of KB memory.
It doesn't require gigabyte and megabyte-heavy memories.
This is the first step in the development of immediate collision avoidance technology, which, according to Nissan, will be in production as standard within the next ten years.
Another application could find the technology in GPS satellite navigation by deliberately steering the motorist around obstacles and leading him to his destination.
A name for the sin-expensive child
Now the written version of the $700-billion bailout law is available, which will be applied in several stages and is aimed at the purchase of banks' bad loans.
In the first round, half of the money is to be spent.
In a session that came to an end on Saturday after midnight local time East Coast, congressmen struggled to reach an agreement about the $700-billion-dollar bailout after several days of debates, Reuters reported.
"We've made substantial progress," Nancy Pelosi, Democratic president in the American House of Representatives, said saying the agreement only had to be put on paper.
Both presidential candidates - Barack Obama and John McCain supported the compromise reached over the bailout.
Finance Minister Henry Paulson said: The program will have an effect and it will be efficient, but you have to do more. According to Pelosi, it would not be the rescue of Wall Street, it would be a matter of taking the helm of the economy.
The written version of the package was released even before the opening of Asian markets, i.e., Washington time Sunday evening 8 a.m. (Central European time Monday 2 a.m.), but did not trigger a treble on the exchanges.
On Monday, the House of Representatives first votes on the draft law to stabilize the economy of 2008, then it is the Senate's turn.
President George W. Bush spoke to Pelosi on Saturday night, and the White House also welcomed the news of the settlement.
We are pleased with the progress made during the night and appreciate the efforts of both parties to stabilize our markets and protect our economy, said Tony Fratto, White House spokesman.
However, the program differs from the Bush administration’s original recommendation that the $700bn should be spent at once for the purchase of lazy, loan-burdened securities.
In an initial tranche, a maximum of half of the $700 billion is to be spent, with $250bn immediately available after Congress is expected to vote on the causa on Monday. That sum is to be spent on the purchase of credit-burdened, bad securities, and the other €100 billion will be decided by the president.
The programme will be reviewed by its own commission, which also includes the President of the Federal Reserve.
The other half of the $700 billion depends on another decision by Congress.
In the sense of the law, the Ministry of Finance can also use a combined tactic: at the same time, it can buy bad loans, mortgages, and securities burdened with mortgages, or other "toxic" loans.
As responsible persons of the ministry reported, the purchase can be made both in an auction or directly.
The law allows the companies concerned to participate in an insurance program in which the Ministry offinance guarantees the worrying funds, and the companies pay corresponding fees to the expected damages.
Henry Paulsen said: “The law defines the necessary funds so that up to $700 billion can be made available as emergency aid to the American financial system, whether it’s for large-scalled purchases of bad funds, their insurance, or avoiding a systemic risk triggered by the collapse of a larger financial institution.
We are taking the necessary steps so that we are ready to introduce the law once it has been signed, according to Paulson.
However, the bosses of banks that are in difficulty and now dependent on state aid can expect their clearances, which are salopingly called "Golden Handshake" in overseas slang, to be cut by the government.
There will be a reduction in salaries only in certain cases, and those cuts will depend on how the Treasury helps in each case.
The cut essentially means that if the ministry buys as part of an auction, according to the "golden handshake," is taxed unless the disused banking chief is deported to retirement.
In the case of a direct purchase of the lazy loans, the government will engage aggressively to ensure that managerial salaries aren't exaggerated.
The picture also includes that the existing Golden Handshake treaties will not be changed - the restrictions relate only to new agreements so that the more people participate in the program.
That's the first case in American history that cuts in managerial salaries are regulated by law, Democrat Barney Frank, chairman of the finance committee, commented on the measure.
The state gains participation rights in the participating banks, so that the risk for taxpayers decreases, as they can also participate as soon as the economy and thus also the bailed-out bank gets better.
The bill stipulates that the president must submit a proposal to Congress over the next five years on how taxpayers should get their lost money from the financial sector back.
However, there were skeptics on both sides who expressed their doubts about the plan.
That's not what American citizens expect of us doesn't protect taxpayers' money, according to Califorian Republican Darrell Issa.
His colleague from Ohio, Mary Kaptur, said the measures were driven by the same people who are also responsible for Wall Street collapse.
Industry prices continue to rise
According to the Central Statistical Office, domestic selling prices rose by 0.1 percent in August 2008 compared with the previous month, compared with August 2007, by 12.9 percent.
Export prices were 1.1 percent higher than in July 2008 and are down 3.9% from the previous yar-on-year prices.
Due to domestic price changes and the export sector, production prices were 0.7% higher than in the previous month, and 3.2 percent higher than in 2007.
Domestic selling prices have risen only marginally, i.e. by 0.1 percent, compared to the previous month.
The manufacturing industry's selling prices have also risen little - 0.1 percent - since July.
The biggest price increase in August 2008 was for leather goods (2.8 percent) - primarily for bags and belts - here the increase was 4.6 percent, for shoes, prices went up 2.5 percent.
Higher prices for plastics and chemical raw materials drove up the prices of chemicals and productions considerably (1.6 percent).
A price decline was recorded for crude oil processing (1.4 percent), electrical and technical equipment (0.9 percent), as well as slightly in wood processing (0.1 percent).
Prices in paper production, publishers and printing industry remained unchanged.
In other branches of the manufacturing industry, a price increase of between 0.2 and 0.9 percent was measured.
In industry's important domestic sales markets, such as electricity, gas, steam and water supplies, prices rose by 0.3 percent in August, primarily as a result of the price increase for gas introduced on July 1.
Looking at the domestic sales markets of the manufacturing industry year-on-year, prices in crude oil processing have risen the most (32.4 percent).
In the metalworking sector, a 13.2 percent increase was observed, while the increase was 11 per cent in the manufacture of food, beverages, and tobacco.
A fall in prices was observed in the textile industry (2.5 percent) and in electrical and technical equipment (0.3 percent).
The extent of inflation in the other manufacturing industries ranged from 0.6 to 9.1 percent.
Overall, prices in the manufacturing industry climbed 10.2 percent last year.
The prices of electricity, gas, steam and water rose by 18.7 percent year-on-year.
In the domestic sales market, the highest price increase (0.7 percent) was recorded in the fixed assets sector in August.
In the production sectors for consumer goods, prices went up 0.3 percent, while they have remained the same in the energy and processing sectors compared to the previous month.
The largest price increase in August 2008 was observed in the energy and reusaling sector compared to the comparable period of 2007 (16.8 percent).
A price increase of 6.8 percent were noteded in the consumer goods-producing sectors and a price reduction of 1.4 percent for fixed goods.
The industry's export prices measured in Forint rose by 1.1 percent in August compared to the previous month, but there was a decrease of 3.9 percent year-on-year.
Compared to the euro, the domestic currency weakened by 1.7 percent compared to the euro, by 7.1 percent, and compared to the course of August 2007 compared to the euro by 7.5 percent, the dollar by 15.9 percent.
No more visa to the United States from 2009 onwards?
Kinga Göncz sees good chances that the American-Hungarian visa agreement will come into force around January 12, as the Secretary of State explained before the representatives of the Hungarian community in Chicago.
In their view, "everything now depends only on whether the electronic immigration or control system overseas is introduced in time."
