Barack Obama and McCain support bailout for banks
Barack Obama and John McCain, the two presidential nominees of the major American parties, pledged their support for the $700 billion bailout for banks on Sunday. However, there are also politicians calling for the resignation of the Treasury secretary.
Congress and representatives of the government have announced after days of marathon deliberations on Sunday morning that they have come to an agreement on the banking bailout.
Wall Street's crisis, as well as thawing of the frozen credit market, became the central questions of the presidential campaign.
Obama and McCain - both claim to be better suited than the respective opponent to lead the country out of the economic crisis.
According to recent polls, Obama was able to further expand his lead thanks to the economic issues, while McCain's Hoch, which has given him the presentation of vice presidential nominee Sarah Palin after the Republican convention, has also melted away.
At a campaign event in Detroit on Sunday, Obama expressed his hope that Congress will swiftly adopt the law on the $700bn package.
He emphasized that the plan also includes those passages of consumer protection, which it has supported.
"We have to act now, responsibly (...) - your jobs, your savings, entire industries, and the stability of the entire economy are threatened," he told 35,000 spectators.
He has not failed to describe McCain's economic visions as "obsolete."
McCain said in an interview: “We’ll all have a hard to turn on that, we’re all going to do it, but doing nothing is certainly not an alternative.”
The Republican senator said the plan also prescribes the set-up of a control commission for the Treasury secretary, as well as the restriction of clearances for top executives - something he insisted on.
The CBS, Obama told McCain didn't play a definitive role in the negotiations, whereas he, Obama, was in contact with the minister.
On Sunday, House Speaker Nancy Pelosi announced that the House of Representatives is likely to vote on the bill as early as Monday.
Press review of German-speaking countries
The right-wing camp is intact, only the voters became more self-confident within the camp - this is how "Der Spiegel" comments on the weak performance of the CSU in Bavaria.
In Austria, Haider and Co are ready to govern to prevent a red-and-black coalition.
The mirror (spiegel.de)
In the online edition of the German news magazine on the left, Franz Walter analyzes the reasons for the dramatic defeat of the CSU in his article "Bavaria's revolution destroys their fathers".
According to the renowned politologist, the erosion of the CSU began long before the sad Beckstein-Huber duo took office.
The Christian socials have renewed Bavaria for decades - now modern citizens are turning away from them.
At the same time, breaking the epoch is a problem of Christian democracy, as well as for Angela Merkel.
Surprisingly, the disaster of the CSU did not come, but something came to an end on Sunday, which, however, almost exactly ten years ago: the undoubted hegemony of a state party
Ten years ago, in 1998, the CSU lands at 47.7 percent and thus fell below the fifty-percent mark for the first time since the 1950s
This was repeated three years ago in the 2005 federal elections, i.e. still in the much-cited Stoiber era.
The modernization consistently operated by the CSU in the federal state has finally destroyed its own mothers and fathers.
Sociologists had already predicted this 40 years ago, but estimated themselves considerably at pace. But without effects, the secularization, educational successes, the influction of East and North Germans into the prosperous regions of Bavaria on the loyalty of CSU voters could not remain.
Modernization is always accompanied by option multiplication, and this has political consequences.
The right camp is intact - only the voters within the camp became more critical and idiosyncratic.
It is striking that in recent weeks the classic civic groups have expressed the greatest distance to the CSU: the liberal professions, the commercial bourgeoisie and voters with a high school diploma.
Here, the CSU's support dropped to even below 40 percent.
The press (diepresse.com)
The conservative Viennese daily reports in its "Who with whom? party leaders cautiously on coalition issues" about the chaotic domestic political situation in Austria after the new elections.
The SPÖ wants to negotiate with the ÖVP.
The ÖVP prohibits the SPÖ's interference in internal affairs.
The BZÖ is open to talks in all directions, while the Greens do not yet want to commit with regard to a coalition.
The new elections are over, and now it's all about the coalition; in the debates on public television, the party leaders barely let look into their cards.
SPÖ leader Werner Faymann reaffirmed his desire to negotiate with the ÖVP.
The SPÖ wants to show that cooperation between the two parties is very possible - this is already the case in some federal states and in the social partnership.
The question of whether he could imagine a coalition with Wilhelm Molterer, the current ÖVP head as vice-chancellor, left Faymann open.
As he said, voters would have deselected the bowl-Molterer course.
A repetition of this direction would be out of the question.
Molterer, meanwhile, has reacted cautiously to red offers.
The cooperation could not be fruitful, he said, if Faymann tries to interfere in internal concerns of the ÖVP.
Whether his party goes into opposition, or into the coalition, is currently completely open, and these questions should be discussed internally.
BZÖ boss Jörg Haider was open in all directions.
"To prevent red-and-black, we are ready to assume government responsibility," Haider said.
Járóka: The vicious cycle of poverty and segregation must be breached
Today the European Summit on Equal Opportunities has begun in Paris, which will be hosted this year by the EU-Presidientiary of the Council of France.
In her speech to the plenary session, Lívia Járóka, Member of the European People's People's Party, drew particular attention to education segregation, as it is one of the most serious violations of the rights facing Roma children, and this is also the greatest obstacle to their need for validity.
The high-level conference was opened by French Employment and Social Affairs Minister Xavier Bertrand on behalf of the French Presidency of the Council, and Vladimir Spidla, Commissioner for Employment, Social Affairs and Equal Opportunities.
Among the participants were representatives of governments, the European social partners, as well as the most important European legal protection and civic organizations in the field of equal opportunities.
As the press office of the MEP of the Fidesz reports, Járóka has pointed out thating that, in addition to discrimination in the search for a job, the Roma would be ousted from the labour market primarily because of their low level of education.
“The vast majority of Roma young people don’t have middle education, and thanks to poor primary education, only a small proportion of them can achieve a college degree.”
Járóka also emphasized that education is a fundamental human right that would play a key role in asserting other human rights.
Investments in the early education of Roma children can strengthen the economy of the EU member states.
In the opinion of MEPs, it is important that these connections become as broad a circle as possible, i.e. Roma and non-Roma.
Because the sooner a Roma pupil starts school, the greater his chances of becoming a productive member of the labour market increase; and through income and sales tax they make their contribution to national spending.
"As EU citizens, Roma must be guaranteed the right to high-quality education - poor and segregated teaching must be discontinued," Lívia Járóka said.
As the MEP explained, a common, goal- and action-oriented approach by the European Union is necessary in order to speed up the degregation process in the education sector, while correct adherence to existing and planned anti-discrimination directives is essential.
"We must break the vicious circle of poverty, segregation, and unemployment and give Roma children the democratic opportunity to contribute to the future of Europe as full citizens," Járóka demanded.
Since last year’s congress in Berlin, the summit has been held annually with the aim of creating a unique forum to promote the objectives of equal opportunities and non-discrimination at European, but also national, in the sense of the “European Year of Equal Opportunities”.
CSU: Mercy on time
At the end of October, the fate of the fallen duo Huber-Beckstein will be decided
Despite the election debacle for the time being, no heads are rolling, at the head of the Bavarian CSU, the personnel debates have been adjourned.
Party leader Erwin Huber and Prime Minister Beckstein still remain in their offices, the question is only how long this grace lasts.
The fate of the tandem after the catastrophic 17 percent loss of vote will be decided on the last weekend of October at the Extraordinary Party Congress.
However, the much scolded duo already made it clear that it does not want to stick on their armchairs.
In doing so, they want to appease the disgruntled faction and the party base, which demanded personal consequences a day after the "Bavarian tsunami" in the face of losing the absolute majority.
Five years ago - admittedly, under the leadership of Edmund Stoiber, who stood at his career's climax - the Christian-Social Union still reached 60.7 percent. Now, however, a year after the coup against Stoiber, they were only able to secure 43.4 percent of the vote.
The CSU was "sole governor" in Bavaria for 46 years, which generates 18 percent of Germany's GDP.
The Conservatives remain the defining force in the southern state, which is also important for the Hungarian economy, but will have to govern in a coalition in the future.
As the first sign of the party's internal restructurings, the potential successor Hubers, Vice-President and Federal Minister of Agriculture Horst Seehofer will also be involved in the exploratory team with the liberal FDP or the right-wing "Free Voters" about a possible Munich coalition.
An irony of fate is that with the "Free Voters" list Gabriele Pauli comes into the legislature, whose anti-Stoiber demeanor the Huber-Beckstein duo first exploited, in order to then mercilessly dump the determined politician.
The CSU leadership is now focusing all its forces on stabilizing Beckstein.
In this transition process, Stoiber, who so far pulled the strings from the background, will play an active role. The former Prime Minister and CSU Chairman called September 28 the "mostest day of his political career."
On Monday, Huber made a few toxic remarks towards the CDU - in his view, the CSU would have deserved far more support from the sister party.
Other CSU politicians interpreted the defeat as a signal to the CDU and thus to Angela Merkel.
Should the CSU, which also contributed a lot to success at the national level in 2005, not be put back on its feet with a joint effort, Merkel cannot become chancellor again after the federal elections in September 2009.
Merkel, who made no secret of her disappointment at the fiasco, believes the CSU's loss of terrain will not have a negative impact on the grand coalition in Berlin.
What is relative, however, is that the wounded CSU will not be easier to govern, and the crisis of the Social Democrats continues.
With 18.6 percent, the SPD achieved its best result so far in Bavaria since 1946.
Lukashenko opposition: 110-0
In Sunday's elections, no opposition lawmaker made it into Ukraine's parliament.
Russian observers believe that this is due to prosperity and stability in the country led by Alexander Lukashenko, but the OECD suspects illegalities in counting votes.
The OECD, which has dispatched 450 observers from 43 countries, has not recognised the elections.
The legislature of the 10-million-inhabitant state has been without opposition for decades. Nevertheless, the counter-party gathered under the name "United Democratic Forces" (ODS) seriously hoped before yesterday's elections that Lukashenko, who in the unclean elections and thanks to a constitutional amendment in 2006, would "allocate" some members of the opposition in parliament this time.
That's why the ODS has even refrained from the previously planned boycotts.
Ukraine is becoming more and more economically dependent thanks to rising prices in natural gas imports from Russia. For alleviating the price increases, even loans have been taken out, and the country has also agreed to the sales of belt transgas, the country's disposal to the Russians. In the background of the opposition's expectations, there was the hope that Ukraine wanted to improve its relationship with the EU after Lukashenko and other high-ranking politicians have the political system.
A temporary détente was caused by the evaluation report of the OECD elections, in which the emphasis was on the positive movements rather than on the shortcomings - such as the exclusion of one-third of the opposition candidates from the elections.
However, the quick report of the OECD already criticized the campaign on Monday - therefore opposition activists should not appear in the press - and in particular the fact that the opposition was not allowed to be present in counting the votes.
In spite of all this, the opposition can see an improvement in the political system: political prisoners have been put on the loose, for example, and Minsk is even ready for smaller confrontations with Moscow by continuing to deny the recognition of the independence of the breakaway Georgian provinces of South Ossetia and Abkhazia, although Russia, with which Ukraine forms a federative state on paper, considers the two territories independent for a month.
"We have taken two steps forward and one backwards, but the most important thing is that dialogue between Belarus and the EU has begun," Alexander Milinkevich told our newspaper.
According to the opposition candidate of the 2006 presidential election, a continuation is desirable, although "the powerful have become afraid to let even an opposition activist into parliament."
The opposition does not plan protest measures in view of the results, but rather tries to make the need for economic reform clear to voters.
"We are struggling here with a lot of problems - the opposition newspapers have to be printed in Russia, the local authorities put stones in our way during distribution, but nevertheless I see that more and more people are supporting change, even the elderly," Milinkevich said, alluding to the fact that not even the most-supported layer of pensioners agrees with the Lukashenko regime, whose only merit is the production of social security.
"Thanks to the country's indebtedness and the increase in the trade deficit, it's just a matter of time before the "social security" reminiscent of the silence of a cemetery begins to crumble," according to Milinkevich.
Contrary to the opposition politician, Russian observers described the elections as democratic.
Sergei Lebedev, head of the mission of the Commonwealth of Independent States, told Ria Novosti: "The people believe the deeds, and not the words." The lack of the opposition is therefore a testament to the maturity of Belarusian voters.
Brunetta: "From now there will be breakfast before work"
For the first time in the history of Palazzo Chigi, there are rotatable doors for the entrances and exits of officials.
Starting today, the measure will come into force against those smart eggs, who first stamp and then go drinking coffee during working hours.
The new "Anti-Sloping Act" was presented today by Renato Brunetta, the Minister for Public Administration and Mauro Masi, Secretary General of the Bureau of the Council of Ministers.
"Rotatable doors are a means of increasing work efficiency through a higher attendance rate and more services," said Minister Brunetta.
“Now a new era of responsibility and transparency begins.
This is reformism," Brunetta underlines once again.
With the Palazzo Chigi, the establishment ends in all buildings of the Presidium of the Council of Ministers.
Masi says: "the choice of rotatable doors is also of great symbolic value".
There was no resistance on the part of the officials, the Secretary-General assured: "This process was supported by everyone, cooperation came from all sides".
All in all, there is no more breakfast during working hours; stop with coffee or croissant.
Brunettas advises in this regard with a smile: "A good cappuccino at 7 o'clock works wonders".
During a meeting of the Higher Committee of the Public Administration (Scuola Superiore della Pubblica Amministrazione).
from Rome, the minister announces that "within three months the absences of civil servants have been reduced to 50%, i.e. 60,000 more people a year are present at work."
He adds it is necessary to strengthen Italy's public administration
“In the last 8-10 years, their wage dynamics – according to the minister – have been twice the size of the private sector and well above the inflation rate.”
Thousands of officials have now joined forces to respond to the Brunetta Decree as part of Codacon's endorsed appeal.
"While the Minister for the Civil Service profiles himself with figures on lower absences at work - so writes the consumer headquarters - the people of 'active slackers' are growing uninterruptedly.
In fact, thousands of correct and valuable civil service officials have come together to declare war on the unlawful norms of the Decree."
In particular, additional financial compensation for work-related sickness is being fought before the Regional Administrative Court (TAR); the planned extension of the obligatory house arrest of the sick worker's employee, during which inspection visits can be made and other aspects of the Minister's innovations are contested.
Napolitano: "Prevention against workplace accidents is crucial"
Every day, 2500 workplace accidents occur in Italy, three people die and 27 remain invalids for life.
For the year 2007, the data of the Inail (Istituto nazionale contro gli infortuni sul lavoro) confirm that the fatal accidents at work were around 1200.
These digits demonstrate the seriousness of a phenomenon that causes almost twice as many deaths arise from murders.
Today in all the provinces of Italy (Associazione Nazionale Mutilati e Invalidi del Lavoro) in all the provinces of Italy celebrate the 58th national day for the victims of workplace accidents in order to draw the attention of social institutions and media to this dramatic situation.
"The worrying data disseminated by Anmil and the tragic news of these days confirm how crucial the issue of prevention in the workplace is," confirms the President of Giorgio Napolitano in his communication to the President of the Amil (Associazione nazionale mutilati ed invalidi del lavoro) Pietro Mercandelli.
The head of state expresses his "great appreciation for the constant commitment to prevention in workplaces, the protection of the injured workers, the aid to the families of the victims and to raise awareness of public opinion".
"An outraged "basta" has risen on this; understandable in tragedies, which, in terms of their order of magnitude, trigger some whirls in the media and sympathy of the public. But everyday reality often confronts us with dramatic events, which can even be repeated in their own dynamics."
Napolitano does not hide that "there is definitely also a resource problem: it must be invested decisively in education and information."
The numerous initiatives for a more direct awareness in school frameworks on the part of the young people who feed on the world of working are particularly important.
It is our duty to pay attention to this phenomenon and not to distract from its social seriousness. You have to put a legislative norm into effect and improve.
For his part, the president of the Gianfranco Fini Chamber has emphasized "how the phenomenon of work-related deaths constitutes an urgent social emergency, which insults the conscience of all of us and forces all institutions to work towards decisive rules that have the aim of putting an end to the problem".
"The dates on occupational accidents appear to signal a slight improvement, but this does not preclude that the number of work-related deaths is far too high," said Pietro Mercandelli.
"We don't want this "positive tendency" to mean less attention. That's exactly what causes the tragedy in the end."
"How to believe that one basically does too little to confront the phenomenon of accidents. One can fight against it through various actions.
Stronger checks and inspections are needed in our opinion – according to Mercandelli; but also stronger measures to educate the workers who are already starting at school.”
“Further effort is necessary – he adds – in terms of communication and raising awareness of public opinion.
But above all, a greater sense of responsibility on the part of employers is in demand towards a phenomenon that constitutes a social emergency.”
Mercandelli also spoke about "the progressive deterioration in protection for occupational accident victims" and denounced the "abolition of the welfare system against which we are fighting in recent years".
“The goal, therefore, is to return a guarantee to the employee on insurance so that the fate of the victim of an accident at work does not become dependent on voluntary or accidental donations, which are decided by a legislature from time.”
Mp3 player under indictment: risk of hearing damage
Listening to loud music over a longer period of time with portable musical devices, such as Mp3 players, can lead to permanent hearing damage.
This is reported by the European Commission, which, in a report circulated today in Brussels, cites the opinion of the Csrseri (scientific committee on the current and recently identified health risks) of the EU.
“The report – according to a note – makes it clear that 5-10% of those who listen to loud music with wearable musical devices for at least five years a week for more than an hour per nay are risking the permanent loss of their hearing.”
"I'm worried that such a large number of young people, who usually put portable music devices and cell phones at a high volume, will irretrievably damage their hearing without knowing it," says Meglena Kuneva.
“From the scientific findings – it is said on the part of the Eu – a clear risk emerges, which pushes for swift action.
What really matters is to sensitize consumers and turn this information into common good.
In view of these scientific findings, we need to re-examine the existing control systems so that they are completely effective and keep pace with the new technology.”
Brussels had mandated the independent scientific committee in view of the widespread use of portable music equipment among young people.
“Scientists, the EU’s communication concludes, confirm that they are rightly concerned and that, together with the other EU members and those concerned, will now examine the necessary measures to better protect children and adolescents from the noise coming from portable music equipment and similar devices.”
There is already a European standard at the moment that limits the level of noise from portable musical devices to 100 dB. The concern about the damage that could come from too much noise pollution.
One can very much limit this damage by resorting to solutions such as the reduction of the volume and the length of the noise exposure.
In their report, the Scientific Committee of the Eu points out that if users of portable music devices only listen to loud (louder than 89 decibels) loudly (louder than 89 decibels) music, so that the legal limits that apply with regard to the maximum noise level at the workplace are already exceeded.
Those who cross this limitage risk the complete loss of hearing within five years.
This would therefore involve 5-10% of users of those devices whose number can be estimated at between 2.5 and 10 million persons in the EU.
Roma, Alemanno gives the go-ahead "Commission for the future of the capital"
We start today, it's supposed to be over in March it's supposed to be taken stock in June.
This is the procedure set by the Capitol for the work of the "Commission for the Future of the Capital of Rome". The Commission met this morning in the new seat behind the Caracalla thermal baths.
In six months, President Marzano and the other 49 members of the Commission will draw uproduce the leadership lines for Rome.
Once the project will be completed, the city and local councils will evaluate the analyses and projects in order to be able to present the strategic plan for Rome with the "official stamp" of the Capitol and mayor Gianni Alemanno.
"The commission is absolutely independent and all political, social and cultural opinions are represented in it," Alemanno points out. "It will offer proposals for a development plan for Rome, whose political leaders remain the city and local council.
There is no redistribution of responsibilities, only the will to have a place where one can develop a great project for Rome.
Its task will be completed in March and the Commission will dissolve again, as such a project will have to have a terminated end.
In June, the mayor announces, we will present the strategic plan in which we will use the work done by the Commission."
Alemanno responds to anyone questioning the Commission's competence because their opinion is lacking in commitment: "Everything depends on the work that the Commission will do and the quality of its proposals.
If, as in France the Attali Commission, it delivers high-quality results – and this will certainly be the case in Rome – then its contribution will be fundamental.
Too often politics deals with the everyday problems and emergencies that occur from time to time.
We, on the other hand, want to keep an eye on a longer period of time in order to work seriously on the development of Rome and the future of the city in a deeper perspective.”
The mayor of Rome attaches great importance to specifying the following "This commission is neither a useless institution nor a wreck: it is absolutely free of charge, it will not cost the Roman citizens a cent and it will make us think. It will occupy the best minds in the capital".
According to Alemanno, "it is necessary to have a strategic plan that is neither hermetic nor static in order to enable our city to make a leap in quality - which it really needs, and thus to take a common step into the future."
A future, according to the mayor of Rome, which "will not only confine itself in the current municipal borders, but will look much further," not only with regard to Rome's role as a metropolis and political capital of Italy, but also as the future capital of Europe and the Mediterranean."
Obama – the first anti-American president? Here’s an explanation
A well-known American politician who cares a lot about being seen as independent has written: "Barack Obama becomes the second anti-American president.
The first was Jimmy Carter, who decimated through his decisions, the U.S. economy and who maintained (and still does so today as a private citizen).
The attacks against Obama are becominging increasingly violent, especially now that elections are approachinginging.
A lady from Miami has written on an internet page: "This person is full of ambitions.
Obama is very naughty: he thinks he will receive the highest office of a nation that has seen a total of two black governors and five black senators!"
Actually, however, these attacks don't have much to say; they're about racist attacks and they don't answer voters' question: will he be a good president? And then there's also the polls; they're all saying Obama will win with a big lead.
Also in this case, there are people there who speak with the heart and not the head.
A newspaper from Mississippi, in the deepest south of the United States, has written in its editorial: only in the voting booth will we probably know to whom the real American patriot will give his vote.
This is where a U.S. mentality comes out that has been kept hidden to the last.
“Say what you want, we’ll decide who we’re going to make our wreaping for, whether it’s for Obama or McCain.”
There's people there who swear: if Obama wins, we're going to emigrate from America.
Something you’ve never heard before – only now that the black candidate’s success seems so certain.
Definitely, despite all these considerations, the final weapon for Democrators is George Bush.
1500 people were interviewed: What mistakes did Bush make during his tenure? 89% have responded: the war in Iraq.
America is changing.
The nation lost 50000 troops in Vietnam and was sent back home with a kneck in the butt.
In Iraq, 4500 troops lost their lives and the Americans were not forced to pack.
Even so, Iraq remains Bush's weak point for the majority.
The one who will take his place faces a huge task.
USA. The financial collapse also leaves its mark on journalists: In the newspapers you can already feel the cuts. And many accuse the Internet, the "murderer" of the printing press.
Five hundred American journalists were interviewed by the PEW Research Center about their situation and mental scrutiny regarding the future.
Today, almost all journalists are pessimistic about their future.
Nevertheless, they did not really express their concern about the information on competent parties because of general credibility.
They don't feel as alone as they used to.
The problem for American journalists is money.
The 2008 crisis is for these journalists a crisis of an economic model that has been destroyed.
In addition, there are the big problems caused by the reductions that hit the newsroom, i.e. the room in which the editors work to put the newspaper together.
It’s a problem that needs to be solved – according to the journalists interviewed – if you want to prevent traditional journalism, as the big audience understands it, from shrinking substantially.
A clear but complicated pessimism fills the American journalist today.
Six out of ten journalists and executive seniors of the printing press believe that journalism is on the wrong track at national level.
In the local area, it is even about 65% for the executives.
Printed vs. broadcast.
It's not difficult for journalists to imagine what direction the web is going.
The most important publishing groups make their way into the Internet, which is regarded by many as the "killer" of the printing press.
The "economic pressure" that journalists are feeling has risen by up to 79%.
The 500 journalists interviewed have admitted wanting to join the new technology, hoping "they bring something good for journalism."
The biggest rift in American journalism today between the editor-in-chiefs of the newsroom and the reporters who work for them.
56% of the editors-in-chief at national level are certain that their reporters share their values with them, while at the local level it is only 23%.
Some think it has to do with "safety at work."
The plant managers, who are afraid of losing their jobs within three years, are 47% at the national level.
Among journalists, it's 30%.
The topic of "safe work" in the team (our "editors", ed.) is always a topical topic in the USA.
Only 13% of journalists work under contract.
In a vast nation like the U.S. is, with 300 million residents and thousands of newspapers, magazines, radio and TV stations and internet operators, employers offer as they like and so it becomes a matter of take or leave it.
The editors-in-chief very often receive very high wages, the editor, on the other hand, can sometimes only get 10000 euros per month, plus pension and insurance money.
As for the holiday, the matter becomes complicated: a certain amount of days in the first year, so and so many after the third, to finally reach 15 vacation days after a certain number of years.
But American journalists, as they themselves admit, frequently "sick" over the course of the year.
It's a matter of climate.
UNIPOL: COURT IN ROME: DANILO COPPOLA STAYS IN GROTTAFERRATA
The President of the Second Criminal Chamber of the Court in Rome, Carmelo Rinaudo, has decided to reject the real estate agent's request, who had asked to be transferred from Grottaferrata to Rome.
Coppola is being investigated for bankruptcy under the counter-agreement investigation in the event of Unipol-Bnl.'s acquisition.
In the course of the trial against him during the last week, his dismissal from house arrest was converted into a residence obligation in Grottaferrata.
His lawyers Michele Gentiloni and Gaetano Pecorella had asked to allow the real estate agent to be moved from the provincial town to an apartment in the capital.
According to the court, such relocations (against which the public prosecutor's office had argued) could give him the opportunity to influence his company and to get evidence accusing him.
The elite is in danger
The phenomenon had already revealed itself a few days ago, when the Chamber of Deputies voted against the $700 billion austerity package of the White House: A large proportion of Americans was and is more interested in punishing Wall Street than saving the economy.
This confirms yesterday's issue of the New York Times, which reports on how the crisis divides the republican party, which divides more and more into its old and moderate East Coast soul, which has always favored the state institutions and the business community, and those neoconservatives that are hostile to those in Washington" and the financial world and, above all, in the south and the center of the states.
This part of the party owes its ascension especially to the voice of what I mentioned above.
However, this just-mentioned example is only a small part of the major changes that the current economic earthquake is announcing or even represents: not only in the USA, but also in these in the West and perhaps beyond them will be affected by them.
In both the states and in Europe, the crisis seems to accelerate the long-feared events which, under these new conditions, finally take shape and come to light.
The first of these phenomena is the new discovery, the unheard-of renaissance of the state in connection with a kind of renationalization of the economy.
In fact, the crisis means a decline of the banking and financial institutions, which have close ties to foreign countries and at the same time manage an enormous amount of the assets of private households living in a single state.
This means an immediate political problem for the governments: namely, how to behave towards the affected families, which also represent an enormous mass of voters.
Thus, the crisis appears economically global, but politically it is almost purely national.
Political internationalism seems to have become sidelined in itself and to have lost its right to speak: the obvious proof of this has been given by the EU, which does not know how to find a common path due to its division into various national and international conflicts.
Therefore, everyone now expects the intervention of their own state; rescue plans, new rules, and, above all, the distribution of aid funds: Since the crisis became increasingly serious, only the USA is in the economic position to save itself without external help.
And in addition to the means, they also have the legitimacy to use them: thus two prerequisites not to be overvalued – under certain circumstances – on which otherwise no international organization and no market will be able to use in a similar way.
Moreover, the new emphasis placed on the state-national element is, moreover, in perfect harmony with the ever-increasing importance that has recently been linked in the economy to a most closely related sector: territoriality.
Paradoxically now, when we were so convinced that we had long since entered into a networked entire realm, into a realm of always differentiated technologies, the immaterial, that we suddenly have to realize how our future will depend more and more on the essential material things: water, harvest and oil.
These can certainly all be transported from one place to the other of the world, but are nevertheless connected to the utmost with a closely circumscribed piece of earth – a territory.
Therefore, the one who possesses such a territory is at an advantage over another who lives in a harvesterless, waterless or oil-provided territory.
I now come to the second novelty, which, however, requires a break.
Anyone who says territory, who speaks government, who says politics, also says leadership with it.
In this sense, the financial crisis appears to generate two converging phenomena.
On the one hand, there is the knowledge about the need for respected leadership, i.e. the need for someone who knows how to regulate things.
On the other hand, there is a wave of indignation over the existing leaderships, especially those of economics, which have turned out to be insufficient and full of shadow zones.
Finally, there is a deeper-reaching and, in a sense, unsettling aspect: a loss of trust, an implement of inexpediency, a sense of distance, which is all aimed at a whole class of managers of Western countries.
It seems to the conviction among large parts of the population that it is not only the deeds of the social elite, but already their world of ideas, their way of life, their culture in the broadest sense of the word, whose salary presents itself to the majority as increasingly foreign and overcoming.
The demand for leadership thus feeds underground a branched but powerful movement of disempoweration of the leading class and conventional political establishment. This manifests itself in unforeseen "revolts" of the voters and in the unmediated reversal of the collective state of mind of recent times: starting with the rise of the Conservatives in Austria and Bavaria, about the collapse of the Labour Party in Great Britain, to the rebellion of the American MPs against Bush.
One gains the impression that the old elite, their parties, their programs, but also their rites, magazines, intellectual creditors, some would say their format, more and more sympathetic problems and therefore less representative of what is going on today in the lower social classes of the West and is reinforced by the financial crisis and driven up to the point of over-sizedness.
In view of all this, it would simply be exaggerated to speak of a "rebellion of the masses".
Keeping your eyes wide open, however, is certainly not.
The super glue that lets go up on walls
Defeating the forces of gravity thanks to an adhesive and to be able to climb walls like Superman.
This is the subject of four American research centers, one of which has now seemingly reached its target.
With an adhesive applied to an area of only 2.5 qcm, the researchers have succeeded in fixing a body weighing 100 kg on a vertical wall.
STUDIO – has an American journal, has published the results of researchers from the University of Dayton, Akron, the Georgia Institute of Technology and the Air Force Research Laboratory.
The material invented by the team of researchers is a further development of the adhesives already used, which use a special carbon structure, called carbon nanotubes.
"The principle of glue can be imagined like a series to a stack of spaghetti-arranged tubes," explains Zhong Lin Wang of the Georgia Institute of Technology in Atlanta.
The scientists were inspired by the feet of geckos, which are covered by microscopic hairs that give the animal a perfect grip.
The tests carried out with various materials such as glass Teflon and sandpaper have shown that the new adhesive is ten times stronger than the holding ability of the reptiles' feet and at the same time "removed from the wall," according to Liming Dai, researcher at the University of Dayton's Faculty of Engineering.
"This material could be used as a transitory adhesive," Liming Dai adds.
For example, to go up like Spiderman on walls? Maybe so, but the researchers are more likely to think of a less fascinating benefit, such as the connection of electrical appliances, because the nanotubes are also conductors of heat and electricity.
Further areas of application could fall within the field of space travel.
For the moment, the superheroes still have to wait.
THE DIFFERENT KIND OF RESEARCH – the University of Berkeley, Califi, is also dedicated to a new type of adhesive.
In an article by the American Chemical Society, the Californian scientists present an adhesive made of microfibres that purify themselves from the dust like hairs by movement on a surface and thus always maintain perfect adhesion.
Medicinal products: never take with fruit juice
Beware of grapefruit, orange and apple juice.
These beverages, which are consumed due to their refreshing quality and vitamin richness especially in the summer, can cause serious health damage when taken at the same time with certain vital medications, as they can have an extreme effect on the absorption of these by the body.
David Bailey, a professor at the University of Western Ontario in London, repeatedly points out the risk of packaged as well as fresh fruit juices in connection with medication intake. As part of the 236th meeting of the American Chemical Society in Philadelphia, he again sounded the alarm: a glass of apple, orange or grapefruit juice is enough to reduce the absorption capacity of certain vital drugs to such an extent that they are virtually eliminated the health of patients.
Grapefruit juice was already suspected of being responsible for increasing the effects of some medications such as antibiotics, antiasthmatics, cardiovascular drugs, anxiolytic and neurological agents, up to toxic levels.
MEDICATIONS IN DANGE – In a report two years agove, the same agency of the drug had warned doctors and patients about the dangers of this mix and emphasizes to pay attention to what is being drunk in conjunction with agents such as diazepane or metadone.
IT’S NOT JUST GRAPEFRUIT – According to the experts, orange juice and apple juice also contain substances that can inhibit the medicines.
The list active ingredients inhibited by the fruit juices is long.
Hence the appeal of Michele Carruba, the director of the Università Statale di Milano: "Such results are usually immediately forwarded to the various international manufacturers who are thus free to change the consumer information.
Therefore, it is advisable to always read the package leaflet and, if there are still uncertainties, not to be afraid to ask a pharmacist or doctor," emphasizes the expert of the Ansa agency.
Nobel Prize goes to Paul Krugman
The Nobel Prize is to the American Paul Krugman for his analysis of trading patterns and the settlement of economic activities.
The 55-year-old Krugman is a Neokeynesian who teaches at the University of Princeton and is known for his regular articles for the New York Times.
Krugman is one of the Bush administration's biggest critics of economic and foreign policy.
A few months after the attacks of September 11, 2001, during the days of the Enron Collapse, Krugman had claimed in a New York Times editorial that this second event had been more crucial to U.S. history than the assassinations.
A prediction, which received quite a little polemics in response and to which the economist was still accused years later, but today proves to be completely correct.
"I am surprised and also shocked at how quickly the memory of the scandals have disappeared from the public field of sight as part of Enron or Worldcom's collapses," Krugman explained in late 2002.
According to Lorenzo Bini Smaghi of the Executive Committee of the European Central Bank, Krugman is an "economist of great value," but also a banalizer with a "very aggressive style that is particularly directed against the excessive liberalism of the Bush administration."
Open office, it releases the third version of the "Anti-Microsoft" package
OpenOffice.org 3.0’s release day has arrived; the open source software is gaininging an ever-increasing number of users, both at home and in the office, both among freelancers and in public administration.
After a year of intensive work by the voluntary developers, which is monitored by SUN Microsystems, the new Open Office now presents itself with some novelties to the audience: in addition to a redesigned and now more spartanly designed initial menu, a guaranteed compatibility with Apple's operating system Mac OS x has finally been achieved, as well as the support for the new ODF (Open Document Format) 1.2 format, i.e. the latest standard ISO version.
It was also about the compatibility with Microsoft's Office 2007, whose new formats (.docx, .xlsx, .pptx) can be read by OpenOffice.
Also interesting are some new features, such as those that make it possible to reduce the size of PowerPoint presentations, which makes it easier, for example to send via email. This also includes the ability to import PDF files in graphic editing modules and at the same time preserve the original appearance of the page.
For example, "hybride" PDF files can now also be generated, which extend the typical graphics of the Adobe format with the elements of a table and a word processing system.
Collaboration is one of the most improved elements of the applications: tables can now be edited multi-handed by different users, avoiding conflicts in the event of changes; in the case of the word processing system, each editor is assigned a request to ensure a better correction procedure.
Some companies are in the process of publishing various functions of OpenOffice on the Internet (on the cloud), while in the coming weeks the definitive result will be made public.
The Italian version is available for download on the following link: http://it.openoffice.org/download/3.0.0/download300.html. Thus, it follows the English version, which has been on the mirrors since Friday.
In addition to Windows and Mac OS, the program is also available for the Linux operating system.
On request you can have the installation CD sent to your home or office.
If, however, cost-of-free can be an incentive for the dissemination of the programme in private households, it is also especially in the area of business, where OpenOffice could succeed in the big shot: after all, various institutions of public administration and schools have already moved to OpenOffice.
In fact, there is not even a lack of a training offer, which means an optimal help for small and medium-sized companies, and thus even opens up business prospects.
OpenOffice is composed of five main components: Writer, the word corresponding text creation program; Calc, the table program, which aims to suppress Excel; Impress, to create presentations such as with PowerPoint; Dbase for operating databases in place of the better-known access; and lastly draw, a graphics module.
The objective for 2008 is to achieve 5 million downloads – a goal that can also be easily exceeded with 3 million and 600 thousand already reached.
Cabbage also tested positive and thus the fourth doping case
A new doping case opens within the framework of the past Tour de France: it is the Austrian sportsman Bernhard Kohl of Team Gerolsteiner, who as a third-clasped and winner of the jersey of the best climber.
The news was published by the newspaper L'Equipe on its website.
The 26-year-old Kohl from Team Gerolsteiner is the fourth rider to test positive for Cera, the third-generation era. Previously, this was the case with the Italians Riccardo Riccò and Leonardo Piepoli as well as the German Stefan Schumacher.
The analyses from Kohl's blood samples are among those carried out in recent weeks by Losanna and Chatenay-Malabry laboratories.
If the counter-samples now confirm the positive results on Cera, the Austrian will lose his third place and be excluded from the races for two years.
The head of France's anti-doping commission Pierre Bordry confirmed the positive epotest, and, according to the Associated Press, also notified the Austrian officials.
The 26-year-old Austrian climbed onto the podium behind Spaniard Carlos Sastre and American Cadel Evans and also claimed the jersey of the best climber for himself.
IMMIGRATION: THE NEW YORK TIMES ASK: IS ITALY RACIST?
Italy, "the former country of émigrés, has recently become one of the preferred countries for immigrants," which is received only with reluctancy.
The New York Times asks in a lengthy article dedicated entirely to the subject of racism in Italy and the case of the young Burkina Faso-born Abdul William Guibre, who was murdered in Milan a month agove after stealing a pack of biscuits.
The case testifies to what the Times describes as the "paradox of the North": the northern regions are those in which "most integration and job opportunities exist," and yet "the highest degree of anti-immigrant sentiments and the greatest support of the Lega Nord."
As confirmation of the growing tension between Italians and immigrants, the Times refers to further episodes of intolerance from Milan, Rome, Parma, Ciampino and Castel Volturno.
And not only this.
The new Lega-brought Legislation against Immigration and the faster expulsion of illegal immigrators is also cited as a sign of climate change in Italy.
"Giorgio Napolitano asked the pope at a meeting at the Quirinal for cooperation between the church and the state to combat racism."
Female students Victim of up to €900 per room
The market for housing for students knows no crises.
According to Sunia's latest investigation, the tenants' union, a single room can cost a student up to €900 in Milan, 700 in Florence, 650 in Naples and 600 in Rome.
"This phenomenon has already taken on very serious traits. Whole crowds of students lose their right to study, as their families are unable to support them at this level, according to Sunia's leader, Laura Mariani, "in these cities, the great demand on the part of the students leads to a deformation of the market, which has also harmed the other inhabitants.
In addition, most rental offers include a number of violations: free contracts, missing registrations, without rental price limit, unauthorised sub-rents.
For Sunia, there is a need for exceptional measures, which lead to lower rental prices due to a revision of taxation.
On closer inspection of the study, it turns out that a sleeping place is needed in Milan in the Brianza district about 450 euros; a single room starts at 650 euros (in the Nere district) and ends with 800 euros in the Lambiate, Udine and Fiera district or even 900 euros in the Vittoria district.
In Florence, a sleeping place costs an average of 350/400 euros, a room 700.
In Bologna, where the students are concentrated in the neighborhoods around the university, €250/280 is in need for a place to sleep, €370 to €500 for a single room.
In Rome, the prices in the neighborhoods around the university are around 600 euros for a single room, 450 for a sleeping place in a double room.
Only a little lower are the rents in the districts around the other universities of Rome: 550 euros in Ostiense and Cinecittà.
Only those who dodge to outskirts can save money: 300 euros for a sleeping place and 450 euros for a single room in Prenestina, Centocelle and similar quarters.
In Naples you need 300-450 euros for a sleeping place, for a room the price is between 400 and 600 euros in the most expensive neighborhoods, the Policlinico, Vomero and Colli Aminei.
In Bari a sleeping place costs 250-350 euros, a single room at least 350.
In the smallest cities, the prices are lower, but they have a greater weight in the urban economy.
In the cities in which the phenomenon of renting to non-European students is more common, such as Perugia, even another anomaly exists: a rent that is 25-30% above the average is demanded by foreign students.
Goodbye to the old cinema Paradiso – the cities close their historic cinema halls
The historical cinemas are closing due to the favored multiplex cinemas.
“A city without cinema halls is a blind city, like a face without eyes.”
This picture by the director Giuseppe Tornatore describes with effect what is currently happening in the great urban centers of Italy, and also in the provinces.
The process of exclusion of cinemas, which began with the birth of the large multi-room complexes in the periphery, in the long term means death for the single-room cinema in the center. A process that not only seems unavoidable and irrevocable, but also bears the features of a cultural crime.
Affected are mostly historical places full of memories, "it's like losing a friend with whom you've covered a piece of street full of emotions," according to the director of the film "Nuovo Cinema Paradiso," who earned him the Oskar and the Cannes Grand Prix 20 years ago. It's about the story of a suburban cinema and its nostalgia, which consists of living in a cinema world that is probably lost forever.
The figures provided by Anec, the central trade association, speak a clear language: in the last five years, the enormous quantity of 316 traditional cinemas have had to close; these collieries paid for the most part the cinemas in the big cities: 10 in Florence, 9 in Milan, Naples, Bologna and Rome, 6 in Turin and 5 in Palermo.
Among the most affected regions are Emilia-Romagna with 82 lost cinemas, followed by Tuscany with 44 and Lombardy at 41.
This testifies to the fact that the main culprits are multiplex cinemas, because it is precisely these three regions in which the largest number of these modern cinemas has been registered: with 195 in the last 5 years, Lombardy leads the lists, 81 in both Tuscany and Emilia-Romagna.
"The business has developed in an irrational way," says Paolo Protti, the president of Anec, "it is useful that new structures are also emerging, but in the first place is the preservation of the already existing one, because closes a hall in the city, not all his regular customers are willing to visit a multiplex cinema in the periphery instead of the old one.
And here remains the missing budget for the fund of the trade association outside eight: the debt burden recorded by the state amounts to about 10 million euros.
It's a disgrace from an ethical-moral standpoint that the government doesn't comply with existing laws.
To maintain the sector, between 16 and 18 million euros per year would be necessary.”
Interesting is the case of Bologna, the first Italian city, which, because of the fears about the old cinema halls, has decided to protect them through an agreement between the municipality and the Anec; it is the first agreement of its kind in Italy, which aims to protect and support traditional cinemas, both in the historical centre and in the periphery.
Among the foreseen measures are also tax advantages for the cinema operators as well as advantages for the public with regard to local traffic: it will be allowed to travel to the traffic-limited areas of the center and also to park at cheaper parking fees.
"It's a pleasing initiative," says Giuseppe Tornatore, "I hope that other cities will join our example and also design new concepts.
Certainly, however, we also need better audio and video equipment in order to be able to align something against the superpower of the multiplex halls, because after all, today’s audience is more demanding.
The defence of traditional cinema has always been an important issue for me: the closure of Baghiera's "Supercinema", the place of my childhood and adolescence, was the decisive point that led me to found the "Nuovo cinema Paradiso", even though I had been playing with the idea for 10 years.
For 25 years I have lived in Rome; when I walk past the Rialto or the Quirinale in Via Nazionale, where I have experienced so many different emotions, I turn to the side to avoid having to see the dust-covered posters.
The links between politics and 'Ndrangheta lead to the arrest of the mayor of Gioia Tauro
“The mayor of Gioia Tauro was prepared to adapt the decisions of the community’s interests to the interests of mafia group Piromalli.
The accusations made by the legalitary of the antimafia authority of Calabria against Mayor Giorgio Dal Torrione and his deputy Rosario Schiavone of the Gioia Tauro municipality, who were arrested this morning for involvement in mafia deals, are serious.
Gioacchino Piromalli, the older intermediary between the clan and the public administration, as well as his nephew, was also taken: the lawyer, who bears the same name as the clan, had already been convicted for involvement in criminal associations of the mafia, at that time to a compensation of 10 million euros.
"Disregarding any kind of moral and legal norm," the lawyers wrote, "the two local administrations had decided to pay lawyer Piromalli funds for consulting activities."
“Thus it had been made possible for the mafia group – according to the judges – to enter the town halls on an official way and to co-determine the politics of the public administration.”
The president of the regional committee of Calabria Agazio Loiero does not deny that in his region it is "anything but an exception to encounter connections from local administrations and organized crime." "There are places in which the concentration of the mafia is greater than in others, in which such connections are practically inevitable.
But there are also local politicians who resist the pressures of 'Ndrangheta: heroes who receive a white vest."
This morning's arrests follow those of last July, with which, according to the police, the clan Piromalli was beheaded.
Since the end of last April, the local council had been dissolved by Gioia Tauro, but as early as 1991 – and at that time due to alleged links between organized crime and the local administration – local politicians had been removed from their municipal offices.
Giorgio Dal Torrione, then mayor of the Udc, was elected head of the centre-rights coalition after the run-off election of May 2006.
He shouted Scandal when the prefecture ordered the dissolution of the local council.
He prosecuted Vice Interior Minister Marco Minniti, whom he accused of inventing everything for political-strategic reasons. The judges, however, declare in connection with this morning's arrest warrant that the Piromalli clan had even dared to request an amendment to the A3 project.
From a wiretapping action in connection with the investigations into the Dal Torrione case, the willingness to meet the wishes of an entrepreneur, who is known to be a representative of the large group around the Piromalli – making the measures of public administration identical to the interests of this clan.
When the energy springs out of the waves, suddenly the greats run up
It’s a non-drying source of money that covers over 70% of the planet’s surface and only now do the world’s big energy companies seem to grasp it.
Waves, currents and tides are the new renewable energy sources that everyone is looking at, and are also looking to occupy, such as giants like Chevron.
The possibility of extracting energy from the movements of the sea has already been considered and investigated for 70 years; not coincidentally, these studies intensified considerably since the first petroleum crisis.
When the price of oil collapsed around 1980, the idea of marine energy was forged, but now the environmental organizations and the increased price of oil are pushing for new impetus in this sector.
The World Energy Council (WEC) in London estimates that 15% of the world's energy needs could be met by the energies generated by the ocean waves.
This would be equivalent to twice the energy currently produced by nuclear power plants.
In addition, there are currents and tides.
Giants in attack.
The first company that now relies on marine energy is Enersis, which signed a contract in 2005 for the construction of a power plant in northern Portugal.
When this news was published, it seemed to be a revolutionary step.
Two years later, more and more companies from the traditional energy industry are relying on marine energy production.
“Chevron explores these technologies,” according to the spokesperson of a company known to the U.S. giant in the Wall Street Journal, and weighs the opportunity to work with us.”
It is about obtaining an approval from the American Energy Commission, which is needed to launch a project off the coast of Alaska.
This is not to be underestimated, especially when you consider that oil will continue to be pumped by the U.S., especially in Alaska, and President Bush has only recently started the plans for new drilling.
In addition to Chevron are the companies Verdant Power Inc. from New York and Ocean Power from New Jersey, which are investing in the new industry; if the details are not known, observers speak of rising interest.
Technologies that are still in need of improvement.
Experts speak of a technology that is still on the move, not least because the last doubts on the part of the environmentalists have not yet been clarified.
So far, projects such as those in Portugal and also Scotland are of a rather small dimension and do not seem to cause any serious ecological problems.
However, in order to produce energy on a large scale, technologies with a much greater impact on the environment are required.
There are various possibilities: Ocean Power, for example, uses a system of interconnected buoys.
The thrust of the shafts, which moves the buoys up and down, is converted into hydraulic pressure within the floats via pistons and cylinders.
This drives a turbine, which in turn operates a generator.
The electricity is sent to the coast via underwater lines.
Until now, there are four planned facilities of this kind, which are only waiting for government approval. Some of the energy generated could also serve for military facilities.
Verdant Power, meanwhile, is already producing energy for a department store and a parking lot with the help of undersea turbines in New York's East River.
The water movements during the tides drive the turbines, which feeds the generator as a result of the rotational movement.
According to Verdant, a long queue of possible clients is already waiting for the necessary authorizations to use these energy sources.
First the waves, then the tides.
According to the Institute for Electricity Generation Research, a special interest immediately deserves the potential that lies in the tides.
Although it is an irregular phenomenon, the tides are better predictable than wind currents, solar radiation and waves, and therefore a more trustworthy power source.
Tidal energy in particular is which states such as New York, Maine and Alaska, as well as other coastal states led to invest $7.5 million in 2008 – up to $35 million has already been planned for 2009.
